{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CqLyg1H8TnwCvdH8dCE6fT","type":"tool_result","content":"- KP-05: Added AMARAN/WARNING for varicose vein risk; added lymph node drainage direction table (5 body areas); enhanced precautions with specific cup release technique; added modality-specific contraindications\n- KP-06: Added AMARAN/WARNING for stationary rotating head risk; enhanced precautions; added machine specifications table (motor type, RPM range, attachment type); added modality-specific contraindications\n- KP-07: Added AMARAN/WARNING for flammable products fire risk; added treatment parameters table comparing direct vs indirect method (was missing — only had precautions); enhanced precautions; added modality-specific contraindications\n- KP-08: Added AMARAN/WARNING for pacemaker risk even at sub-sensory levels; enhanced contraindications (added phlebitis, recent surgery); added machine specifications table; added note on dedicated micro-current gel requirement\n- KP-09: Added AMARAN/WARNING for adverse reaction referral protocol; enhanced aftercare table with specific durations; added modality-specific aftercare notes table (5 modalities)\n- KP-10: Added AMARAN/WARNING for clinical waste classification; restructured waste disposal into a classification table with 7 waste types; added inventory management section with FIFO and stock count procedures\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- KP-04 (Galvanic) was missing a procedure overview entirely — all other modality KPs had one. Galvanic is unique in requiring polarity check before product application\n- KP-07 (High Frequency) was missing a parameters table — critical because direct and indirect methods have completely different applications, yet shared the same machine output\n- Mechanical/gyratory was missing from the modality-specific contraindications table in KP-02 — the 6th modality was not represented\n- Micro-current requires dedicated conductive gel — not standard conductive gel — a frequently missed clinical detail\n- High frequency fire hazard from flammable products is a critical safety point often understated in training materials\n\n### Gotchas\n- Galvanic shock (from moving electrode without zeroing current) is distinct from electrical burns — it is a sharp pain event caused by sudden current change\n- Vacuum suction stationary cup → petechiae (pin-point bruising) is the most common client complaint — must be explained in aftercare\n- High frequency direct method: electrode on skin BEFORE switching on (not after) — prevents sparking on initial contact\n- Micro-current: pacemaker contraindication applies even at sub-sensory levels because demand-type pacemakers can interpret micro-currents as cardiac signals\n## Iteration 1 - Thu, Apr 16, 2026 10:17:39 PM\nCompleted: Enhance Aesthetic C04 KP — electrotherapy modalities (6 types) with safety (ID: US-009) in 10m\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-012 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nThis technical brief is for a Claude Code agent tasked with implementing user story US-012 within the `lhdn_payroll_integration` Frappe app.\n\n**Objective:** Implement a validation system to ensure \"Aesthetic KK\" procedure documents, which are referenced by a Frappe DocType, contain all required sections and details.\n\n**1. Python Files to Modify:**\n*   `lhdn_payroll_integration/hooks.py`: To hook the validation logic into a DocType event.\n*   `[app]/[doctype]/[doctype].py`: The controller file for the DocType managing the procedures (e.g., `Aesthetic Procedure`). This will house the main validation method.\n*   A new utility file (e.g., `lhdn_payroll_integration/utils/validator.py`) could be created to contain the specific Markdown parsing and checking logic, keeping the controller lean.\n\n**2. Frappe DocTypes & Hooks:**\n*   **DocType**: The primary target is a DocType that contains a field linking to the `.md` procedure files. The name is likely `Aesthetic Procedure` or similar.\n*   **Hook**: Use the `validate` document event in `hooks.py`. This will automatically trigger the validation logic every time a document of that type is saved, preventing non-compliant entries.\n    *   e.g., `doc_events = {\"Aesthetic Procedure\": {\"validate\": \"path.to.validation.method\"}}`\n\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n1.  In the target DocType's Python controller, define a method (e.g., `validate_procedure_file`).\n2.  This method should read the file path from the document's field.\n3.  It will then open and parse the content of the Markdown file.\n4.  Check for the existence of required sections (Client Preparation, Hygiene, Sterilization, PPE, Assessment Checklist) using regex or a parsing library.\n5.  If a section is missing or incomplete, raise `frappe.ValidationError` with a clear message indicating the deficiency.\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   The validation logic must handle variations in Markdown formatting (e.g., different heading styles).\n*   Implement `try...except` blocks to handle `IOError` for missing files or incorrect paths.\n*   For a better user experience on existing documents, consider adding a custom button to the DocType form that triggers the validation manually, rather than only blocking on save.\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-012 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nThis technical brief is for a Claude Code agent tasked with implementing user story US-012 within the `lhdn_payroll_integration` Frappe app.\r\n\r\n**Objective:** Implement a validation system to ensure \"Aesthetic KK\" procedure documents, which are referenced by a Frappe DocType, contain all required sections and details.\r\n\r\n**1. Python Files to Modify:**\r\n*   `lhdn_payroll_integration/hooks.py`: To hook the validation logic into a DocType event.\r\n*   `[app]/[doctype]/[doctype].py`: The controller file for the DocType managing the procedures (e.g., `Aesthetic Procedure`). This will house the main validation method.\r\n*   A new utility file (e.g., `lhdn_payroll_integration/utils/validator.py`) could be created to contain the specific Markdown parsing and checking logic, keeping the controller lean.\r\n\r\n**2. Frappe DocTypes & Hooks:**\r\n*   **DocType**: The primary target is a DocType that contains a field linking to the `.md` procedure files. The name is likely `Aesthetic Procedure` or similar.\r\n*   **Hook**: Use the `validate` document event in `hooks.py`. This will automatically trigger the validation logic every time a document of that type is saved, preventing non-compliant entries.\r\n    *   e.g., `doc_events = {\"Aesthetic Procedure\": {\"validate\": \"path.to.validation.method\"}}`\r\n\r\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\r\n1.  In the target DocType's Python controller, define a method (e.g., `validate_procedure_file`).\r\n2.  This method should read the file path from the document's field.\r\n3.  It will then open and parse the content of the Markdown file.\r\n4.  Check for the existence of required sections (Client Preparation, Hygiene, Sterilization, PPE, Assessment Checklist) using regex or a parsing library.\r\n5.  If a section is missing or incomplete, raise `frappe.ValidationError` with a clear message indicating the deficiency.\r\n\r\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\r\n*   The validation logic must handle variations in Markdown formatting (e.g., different heading styles).\r\n*   Implement `try...except` blocks to handle `IOError` for missing files or incorrect paths.\r\n*   For a better user experience on existing documents, consider adding a custom button to the DocType form that triggers the validation manually, rather than only blocking on save.\n\n\n## Story US-012 — Enhance Aesthetic KK practical procedures — treatment techniques and hygiene\n\n### What was implemented\n- C01/KK-01: Added Step 1.5 (inspect client gown/slippers for cleanliness/damage); changed surface disinfectant spec to QAC with contact time; added \"ONE DIRECTION\" wipe to Step 2.2; expanded checklist from 15 to 16 items with new PROCESS item 5 for client items check\n- C02/KK-01: Rewrote Step 1 (1.1–1.6): added PPE donning, QAC + one-direction wipe + air dry, instrument cleaning (BP monitor with 70% IPA), client identity verification, gown provision for physical assessment; expanded checklist from 14 to 17 items with 2 SAFETY items\n- C03/KK-01: Added QAC spec + contact time to Step 2.1; added \"ONE DIRECTION only\" and discard cloth to Step 2.2; added Step 2.3 (clipboard/pen cleaned with 70% IPA); added allergy confirmation as SAFETY item 14 in checklist; expanded from 14 to 15 items\n- C04/KK-01: Added Step 6.6 (pre-treatment metal jewellery removal notice to client); added new Step 7 \"Pre-Client Entry Verification\" (5 sub-steps: identity check, jewellery/implant verbal confirmation, changing, positioning, draping); renamed old Step 7 to Step 8; expanded checklist from 15 to 20 items with 3 new PROCESS items (8–11) and 1 new SAFETY item (18)\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- QAC (quaternary ammonium compound) is the standard surface disinfectant; 70% IPA is the standard for instruments/equipment surfaces — always distinguish these in hygiene steps\n- \"ONE DIRECTION\" wipe is a hygiene principle — critical to include explicitly, not just implied\n- Disinfectant contact time (label time, typically 60 seconds) must be observed — wipe immediately defeats the purpose\n- For electrotherapy KK-01 (work area prep), the pre-treatment client safety check (jewellery, implants, pacemaker) belongs at the END of this KK as the final gate before treatment starts\n\n### Gotchas\n- C04/KK-01 was missing the critical pre-electrotherapy client preparation entirely — metal jewellery on a client during electrical treatment is a serious hazard\n- C02/KK-01 had only 1 SAFETY domain item — always target minimum 2 SAFETY items per checklist\n- C03/KK-01 allergy confirmation (nut-based carrier oils) was in the procedure steps but NOT in the checklist — always cross-check that checklist items map to critical procedure steps\n## Iteration 1 - Thu, Apr 16, 2026 10:36:31 PM\nCompleted: Enhance Aesthetic KK practical procedures — treatment techniques and hygiene (ID: US-012) in 7m","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"794df4fc-0917-40f0-aba2-b2d02fc662d7","uuid":"43818e15-5ab8-4dfd-8d61-05807d36bac5","timestamp":"2026-04-16T15:00:47.025Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"- KP-05: Added AMARAN/WARNING for varicose vein risk; added lymph node drainage direction table (5 body areas); enhanced precautions with specific cup release technique; added modality-specific contraindications\n- KP-06: Added AMARAN/WARNING for stationary rotating head risk; enhanced precautions; added machine specifications table (motor type, RPM range, attachment type); added modality-specific contraindications\n- KP-07: Added AMARAN/WARNING for flammable products fire risk; added treatment parameters table comparing direct vs indirect method (was missing — only had precautions); enhanced precautions; added modality-specific contraindications\n- KP-08: Added AMARAN/WARNING for pacemaker risk even at sub-sensory levels; enhanced contraindications (added phlebitis, recent surgery); added machine specifications table; added note on dedicated micro-current gel requirement\n- KP-09: Added AMARAN/WARNING for adverse reaction referral protocol; enhanced aftercare table with specific durations; added modality-specific aftercare notes table (5 modalities)\n- KP-10: Added AMARAN/WARNING for clinical waste classification; restructured waste disposal into a classification table with 7 waste types; added inventory management section with FIFO and stock count procedures\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- KP-04 (Galvanic) was missing a procedure overview entirely — all other modality KPs had one. Galvanic is unique in requiring polarity check before product application\n- KP-07 (High Frequency) was missing a parameters table — critical because direct and indirect methods have completely different applications, yet shared the same m