{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JGeJP4UumC2v8zBy61p43o","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# PENILAIAN PENGETAHUAN / KNOWLEDGE ASSESSMENT\n2\t\n3\t**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C02/KA\n4\t**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** MERAH JAMBU / PINK\n5\t\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t## Competency Unit: Perform Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification\n9\t\n10\t| Field | Detail |\n11\t|-------|--------|\n12\t| Duration | 2 hours |\n13\t| Total Marks | 100 |\n14\t| Passing Mark | 60% |\n15\t| References | KP(1/6) through KP(6/6) |\n16\t\n17\t---\n18\t\n19\t## SECTION A: Multiple Choice (20 marks — 2 marks each)\n20\t\n21\t**A1.** What colour identifies HV cables in a BEV? A. Red B. Blue C. Orange D. Yellow\n22\t\n23\t**A2.** What is the correct sequence for HV de-energisation? A. Remove interlock, then 12V, then key B. Remove key, then interlock, then 12V C. Turn off READY, store key, disconnect 12V, remove interlock, wait, verify D. Disconnect 12V, remove interlock, store key\n24\t\n25\t**A3.** The pre-charge system prevents: A. Battery overcharging B. Inrush current surge to inverter capacitors C. Coolant leakage D. Motor overheating\n26\t\n27\t**A4.** What is the typical pre-charge resistor value? A. 0.1 ohm B. 50-200 ohm C. 10,000 ohm D. 1 MOhm\n28\t\n29\t**A5.** The OBC converts: A. DC to AC for the motor B. AC from external source to DC for battery charging C. 12V to HV D. HV to heat\n30\t\n31\t**A6.** The DC-DC converter replaces which ICE vehicle component? A. Fuel pump B. Alternator C. Starter motor D. Radiator\n32\t\n33\t**A7.** Minimum personnel for battery pack removal: A. 1 person B. 2 persons C. 3 persons D. 5 persons\n34\t\n35\t**A8.** What should defective battery modules be stored at before disposal? A. 100% SOC B. >80% SOC C. <30% SOC D. 50% SOC\n36\t\n37\t**A9.** Insulation resistance of HV cables must exceed: A. 100 ohm B. 1 kOhm C. 1 MOhm D. 1 GOhm\n38\t\n39\t**A10.** Normal DC-DC converter output voltage: A. 5V B. 9V C. 13.5-14.4V D. 48V\n40\t\n41\t---\n42\t\n43\t## SECTION B: Short Answer (40 marks)\n44\t\n45\t**B1.** (10 marks) Describe the complete HV de-energisation procedure in correct sequence. Include all seven steps.\n46\t\n47\t**B2.** (8 marks) Explain the function of the following BMS components: a) HV contactors b) Pre-charge relay c) Interlock/service plug d) Current sensor\n48\t\n49\t**B3.** (8 marks) Describe the coolant drainage procedure for OBC replacement. Include waste management requirements.\n50\t\n51\t**B4.** (8 marks) Explain the traction battery pack architecture. List and describe SIX (6) main components.\n52\t\n53\t**B5.** (6 marks) List THREE (3) common OBC fault categories and explain the possible cause of each.\n54\t\n55\t---\n56\t\n57\t## SECTION C: Structured Questions (40 marks)\n58\t\n59\t**C1.** (15 marks) A BEV arrives with a fault code indicating pre-charge timeout.\n60\ta) Explain what this fault means and what happens during normal pre-charge sequence. (5 marks)\n61\tb) Describe the diagnostic procedure you would follow. (5 marks)\n62\tc) Describe the replacement procedure for the pre-charge relay pack. (5 marks)\n63\t\n64\t**C2.** (15 marks) You are tasked with replacing a faulty battery module in a traction battery pack.\n65\ta) Describe the complete battery pack removal procedure from de-energisation to securing on transport hanger. (7 marks)\n66\tb) Describe how you would identify the faulty module and perform the replacement. (5 marks)\n67\tc) What functionality tests must be performed after reinstallation? (3 marks)\n68\t\n69\t**C3.** (10 marks) Compare the OBC and DC-DC converter in terms of: function, input/output voltages, cooling requirements, and diagnostic parameters. Present in table format.\n70\t\n71\t---\n72\t\n73\t## SKEMA PEMARKAHAN / MARKING SCHEME\n74\t\n75\t### Section A\n76\tA1:C A2:C A3:B A4:B A5:B A6:B A7:C A8:C A9:C A10:C\n77\t\n78\t### Section B\n79\t**B1:** (10 marks) 1. READY OFF (1m) 2. Key in safety box (1m) 3. Understand HV signage (1m) 4. Disconnect 12V negative (2m) 5. Remove interlock/store in safety box (2m) 6. Wait 5-10 min for capacitor discharge (1m) 7. Verify zero energy with CAT III multimeter — 3 measurements (2m)\n80\t\n81\t**B2:** (8 marks — 2 each) a) Switch HV power on/off from battery to vehicle circuits b) Energises before main contactor to prevent inrush current c) Manual HV circuit break point for safety d) Measures charge/discharge current for BMS monitoring\n82\t\n83\t**B3:** (8 marks) Place container (1m), identify hoses (1m), loosen clamps with insulated tools (1m), drain completely (1m), cap hoses (1m), transfer to sealed container (1m), label as SW305 (1m), dispose via licensed contractor (1m)\n84\t\n85\t**B4:** (8 marks — any 6 at 1.3m each) Battery modules, cells, BMS controller, CMUs, contactors, current sensor, cooling plates, housing, service plug, HV junction box\n86\t\n87\t**B5:** (6 marks — 2 each) Communication fault — wiring/ECU failure; Over-temperature — blocked coolant; DC output fault — internal electronics failure; Isolation fault — moisture intrusion; AC input fault — cable/inlet issue. Any three.\n88\t\n89\t### Section C\n90\t**C1** (15 marks): a) Pre-charge timeout = capacitor voltage did not reach threshold within time limit. Normal sequence described (5m). b) Read fault codes, measure resistor value, relay coil resistance, check wiring, inspect connectors (5m). c) HV de-energisation, access relay pack, disconnect, remove, install new, reconnect, verify, test (5m).\n91\t\n92\t**C2** (15 marks): a) De-energise, drain coolant, raise vehicle, disconnect HV/LV cables, position lift table, remove bolts, lower pack, secure on hanger (7m). b) Open housing, visual inspection, measure module voltages, identify deviant module, disconnect bus-bars/CMU, remove, install new, balance voltages (5m). c) Cell voltages, insulation resistance, BMS communication, pre-charge, cooling, no fault codes (3m).\n93\t\n94\t**C3** (10 marks): Table with OBC vs DC-DC: Function (AC to DC vs HV DC to 12V DC), Input (AC 230/400V vs 200-800V DC), Output (200-800V DC vs 13.5-14.4V DC), Cooling (liquid vs liquid), Diagnostic (charging power/codes vs output voltage/stability).\n95\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"794df4fc-0917-40f0-aba2-b2d02fc662d7","uuid":"e51b27a3-c315-448b-9772-b18a281ff3f4","timestamp":"2026-04-16T15:00:58.766Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C02\\KA.md","content":"# PENILAIAN PENGETAHUAN / KNOWLEDGE ASSESSMENT\n\n**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C02/KA\n**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** MERAH JAMBU / PINK\n\n---\n\n## Competency Unit: Perform Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification\n\n| Field | Detail |\n|-------|--------|\n| Duration | 2 hours |\n| Total Marks | 100 |\n| Passing Mark | 60% |\n| References | KP(1/6) through KP(6/6) |\n\n---\n\n## SECTION A: Multiple Choice (20 marks — 2 marks each)\n\n**A1.** What colour identifies HV cables in a BEV? A. Red B. Blue C. Orange D. Yellow\n\n**A2.** What is the correct sequence for HV de-energisation? A. Remove interlock, then 12V, then key B. Remove key, then interlock, then 12V C. Turn off READY, store key, disconnect 12V, remove interlock, wait, verify D. Disconnect 12V, remove interlock, store key\n\n**A3.** The pre-charge system prevents: A. Battery overcharging B. Inrush current surge to inverter capacitors C. Coolant leakage D. Motor overheating\n\n**A4.** What is the typical pre-charge resistor value? A. 0.1 ohm B. 50-200 ohm C. 10,000 ohm D. 1 MOhm\n\n**A5.** The OBC converts: A. DC to AC for the motor B. AC from external source to DC for battery charging C. 12V to HV D. HV to heat\n\n**A6.** The DC-DC converter replaces which ICE vehicle component? A. Fuel pump B. Alternator C. Starter motor D. Radiator\n\n**A7.** Minimum personnel for battery pack removal: A. 1 person B. 2 persons C. 3 persons D. 5 persons\n\n**A8.** What should defective battery modules be stored at before disposal? A. 100% SOC B. >80% SOC C. <30% SOC D. 50% SOC\n\n**A9.** Insulation resistance of HV cables must exceed: A. 100 ohm B. 1 kOhm C. 1 MOhm D. 1 GOhm\n\n**A10.** Normal DC-DC converter output voltage: A. 5V B. 9V C. 13.5-14.4V D. 48V\n\n---\n\n## SECTION B: Short Answer (40 marks)\n\n**B1.** (10 marks) Describe the complete HV de-energisation procedure in correct sequence. Include all seven steps.\n\n**B2.** (8 marks) Explain the function of the following BMS components: a) HV contactors b) Pre-charge relay c) Interlock/service plug d) Current sensor\n\n**B3.** (8 marks) Describe the coolant drainage procedure for OBC replacement. Include waste management requirements.\n\n**B4.** (8 marks) Explain the traction battery pack architecture. List and describe SIX (6) main components.\n\n**B5.** (6 marks) List THREE (3) common OBC fault categories and explain the possible cause of each.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION C: Structured Questions (40 marks)\n\n**C1.** (15 marks) A BEV arrives with a fault code indicating pre-charge timeout.\na) Explain what this fault means and what happens during normal pre-charge sequence. (5 marks)\nb) Describe the diagnostic procedure you would follow. (5 marks)\nc) Describe the replacement procedure for the pre-charge relay pack. (5 marks)\n\n**C2.** (15 marks) You are tasked with replacing a faulty battery module in a traction battery pack.\na) Describe the complete battery pack removal procedure from de-energisation to securing on transport hanger. (7 marks)\nb) Describe how you would identify the faulty module and perform the replacement. (5 marks)\nc) What functionality tests must be performed after reinstallation? (3 marks)\n\n**C3.** (10 marks) Compare the OBC and DC-DC converter in terms of: function, input/output voltages, cooling requirements, and diagnostic parameters. Present in table format.\n\n---\n\n## SKEMA PEMARKAHAN / MARKING SCHEME\n\n### Section A\nA1:C A2:C A3:B A4:B A5:B A6:B A7:C A8:C A9:C A10:C\n\n### Section B\n**B1:** (10 marks) 1. READY OFF (1m) 2. Key in safety box (1m) 3. Understand HV signage (1m) 4. Disconnect 12V negative (2m) 5. Remove interlock/store in safety box (2m) 6. Wait 5-10 min for capacitor discharge (1m) 7. Verify zero energy with CAT III multimeter — 3 measurements (2m)\n\n**B2:** (8 marks — 2 each) a) Switch HV power on/off from battery to vehicle circuits b) Energises before main contactor to prevent inrush current c) Manual HV circuit break point for safety d) Measures charge/discharge current for BMS monitoring\n\n**B3:** (8 marks) Place container (1m), identify hoses (1m), loosen clamps with insulated tools (1m), drain completely (1m), cap hoses (1m), transfer to sealed container (1m), label as SW305 (1m), dispose via licensed contractor (1m)\n\n**B4:** (8 marks — any 6 at 1.3m each) Battery modules, cells, BMS controller, CMUs, contactors, current sensor, cooling plates, housing, service plug, HV junction box\n\n**B5:** (6 marks — 2 each) Communication fault — wiring/ECU failure; Over-temperature — blocked coolant; DC output fault — internal electronics failure; Isolation fault — moisture intrusion; AC input fault — cable/inlet issue. Any three.\n\n### Section C\n**C1** (15 marks): a) Pre-charge timeout = capacitor voltage did not reach threshold within time limit. Normal sequence described (5m). b) Read fault codes, measure resistor value, relay coil resistance, check wiring, inspect connectors (5m). c) HV de-energisation, access relay pack, disconnect, remove, install new, reconnect, verify, test (5m).\n\n**C2** (15 marks): a) De-energise, drain coolant, raise vehicle, disconnect HV/LV cables, position lift table, remove bolts, lower pack, secure on hanger (7m). b) Open housing, visual inspection, measure module voltages, identify deviant module, disconnect bus-bars/CMU, remove, install new, balance voltages (5m). c) Cell voltages, insulation resistance, BMS communication, pre-charge, cooling, no fault codes (3m).\n\n**C3** (10 marks): Table with OBC vs DC-DC: Function (AC to DC vs HV DC to 12V DC), Input (AC 230/400V vs 200-800V DC), Output (200-800V DC vs 13.5-14.4V DC), Cooling (liquid vs liquid), Diagnostic (charging power/codes vs output voltage/stability).\n","numLines":95,"startLine":1,"totalLines":95}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CFbtcFthJNUnWbatwmkuU7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# PENILAIAN PENGETAHUAN / KNOWLEDGE ASSESSMENT\n2\t\n3\t**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C01/KA\n4\t**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** MERAH JAMBU / PINK\n5\t\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t## Competency Unit: Perform BEV Scheduled Maintenance (G452-010-3:2023-C01)\n9\t\n10\t| Field | Detail |\n11\t|-------|--------|\n12\t| Duration | 1 hour 30 minutes |\n13\t| Total Marks | 100 |\n14\t| Passing Mark | 60% (60 marks) |\n15\t| References Covered | KP(1/3), KP(2/3), KP(3/3) |\n16\t\n17\t---\n18\t\n19\t## Instructions to Candidates\n20\t\n21\t1. This assessment consists of THREE (3) sections: A, B, and C\n22\t2. Answer ALL questions in ALL sections\n23\t3. Write your answers in the spaces provided or on the answer sheet\n24\t4. Marks for each question are indicated in brackets\n25\t\n26\t---\n27\t\n28\t## SECTION A: Multiple Choice Questions (20 marks)\n29\t\n30\tChoose the BEST answer for each question. Each question carries 2 marks.\n31\t\n32\t**A1.** Which Act governs occupational safety and health in a BEV maintenance workshop in Malaysia?\n33\t- A. Employment Act 1955\n34\t- B. Occupational Safety and Health Act 1994\n35\t- C. Road Transport Act 1987\n36\t- D. Factories and Machinery Act 1967\n37\t\n38\t**A2.** What is the minimum rated voltage for insulated gloves used in BEV HV work (Class 0)?\n39\t- A. 500V AC\n40\t- B. 750V AC\n41\t- C. 1000V AC\n42\t- D. 7500V AC\n43\t\n44\t**A3.** What type of fire extinguisher is required for lithium battery fires?\n45\t- A. Class A (water)\n46\t- B. Class B (foam)\n47\t- C. Class C (CO2)\n48\t- D. Class D (dry powder for metal fires)\n49\t\n50\t**A4.** What is the function of the Insulation Monitoring Device (IMD) in a BEV?\n51\t- A. Monitor engine oil pressure\n52\t- B. Monitor insulation resistance between HV circuits and vehicle chassis\n53\t- C. Monitor cabin temperature\n54\t- D. Monitor tyre pressure\n55\t\n56\t**A5.** How many characters are in a Vehicle Identification Number (VIN)?\n57\t- A. 10\n58\t- B. 13\n59\t- C. 15\n60\t- D. 17\n61\t\n62\t**A6.** Which CAT rating is required for a digital multimeter used on BEV HV circuits?\n63\t- A. CAT I — 600V\n64\t- B. CAT II — 600V\n65\t- C. CAT III — 1000V\n66\t- D. CAT IV — 300V\n67\t\n68\t**A7.** What is the typical acceptable cell voltage deviation in a BEV battery pack?\n69\t- A. +/- 0.005V\n70\t- B. +/- 0.05V\n71\t- C. +/- 0.5V\n72\t- D. +/- 5.0V\n73\t\n74\t**A8.** Which of the following is NOT a BEV scheduled maintenance item?\n75\t- A. Cabin air filter replacement\n76\t- B. Engine oil change\n77\t- C. Brake fluid replacement\n78\t- D. 12V auxiliary battery check\n79\t\n80\t**A9.** In the hierarchy of controls, which is the FIRST (most effective) level?\n81\t- A. PPE\n82\t- B. Administrative controls\n83\t- C. Elimination\n84\t- D. Engineering controls\n85\t\n86\t**A10.** Why are BEV-specific coolants non-conductive?\n87\t- A. To prevent engine corrosion\n88\t- B. To maintain HV insulation between coolant circuit and chassis\n89\t- C. To reduce cost\n90\t- D. To improve engine performance\n91\t\n92\t---\n93\t\n94\t## SECTION B: Short Answer Questions (40 marks)\n95\t\n96\tAnswer ALL questions. Provide clear and concise responses.\n97\t\n98\t**B1.** (8 marks) List FOUR (4) regulatory authorities and their respective legislation governing BEV maintenance activities in Malaysia. Present your answer in a table format.\n99\t\n100\t**B2.** (8 marks) Describe the procedure for inspecting insulated gloves before use. State FOUR (4) conditions that require immediate replacement.\n101\t\n102\t**B3.** (8 marks) Explain the Job Hazard Analysis (JHA) process. List the FIVE (5) steps involved.\n103\t\n104\t**B4.** (8 marks) Describe the function of the following ECUs found in a BEV:\n105\t- a) Battery Management System (BMS) ECU\n106\t- b) Motor Control Unit (MCU)\n107\t- c) Vehicle Control Unit (VCU)\n108\t- d) Thermal Management ECU\n109\t\n110\t**B5.** (8 marks) Compare BEV scheduled maintenance with ICE vehicle maintenance. List FOUR (4) maintenance items present in ICE vehicles but absent in BEVs, and FOUR (4) items unique to BEVs.\n111\t\n112\t---\n113\t\n114\t## SECTION C: Structured Questions (40 marks)\n115\t\n116\tAnswer ALL questions with detailed explanations.\n117\t\n118\t**C1.** (12 marks) A BEV arrives at your workshop for its first scheduled maintenance at 15,000 km.\n119\t\n120\ta) Describe the FIVE (5) types of hazards you would identify in a JHA for this service. (5 marks)\n121\t\n122\tb) Explain the workplace preparation procedure you would follow before commencing work. Include at least FIVE (5) key preparation steps. (7 marks)\n123\t\n124\t**C2.** (14 marks) You are performing a BEV health check on a 2024 model year vehicle.\n125\t\n126\ta) Describe the systematic BEV health check procedure from vehicle reception to report submission. Include at least SEVEN (7) major steps. (7 marks)\n127\t\n128\tb) During the diagnostic scan, you discover the following:\n129\t- Battery SOH: 94%\n130\t- Cell voltage deviation: Cell #47 reads 3.72V while all other cells read 3.85V\n131\t- HV insulation resistance: 850 ohm/V\n132\t- Fault code P0A1F stored (Battery Energy Control Module)\n133\t\n134\tInterpret each finding and state what action is required for each. (7 marks)\n135\t\n136\t**C3.** (14 marks) Describe the complete procedure for the following BEV maintenance activities:\n137\t\n138\ta) Cabin air filter replacement — include all steps from glove box access to completion. (4 marks)\n139\t\n140\tb) Fluid level checks — describe the procedure for checking brake fluid and HV coolant. Include safety considerations for each. (5 marks)\n141\t\n142\tc) Underbody inspection — describe what items to inspect when the vehicle is raised on a lift. Include the critical safety consideration regarding lift point placement. (5 marks)\n143\t\n144\t---\n145\t\n146\t## SKEMA PEMARKAHAN / MARKING SCHEME\n147\t\n148\t### Section A (20 marks — 2 marks each)\n149\tA1: B | A2: C | A3: D | A4: B | A5: D | A6: C | A7: B | A8: B | A9: C | A10: B\n150\t\n151\t### Section B\n152\t\n153\t**B1** (8 marks — 2 marks each authority/legislation pair):\n154\t1. JPJ — Road Transport Act 1987 (Act 333)\n155\t2. DOSH — OSHA 1994 (Act 514)\n156\t3. Energy Commission — Electricity Supply Act 1990 (Act 447) / Energy Commission Act 2001\n157\t4. DOE — Environmental Quality Act 1974 (Act 127)\n158\t\n159\t**B2** (8 marks):\n160\tProcedure (4 marks): Roll cuff towards fingers to trap air (1m). Squeeze gently to check for leaks (1m). Visually inspect entire surface (1m). Check markings are legible (1m).\n161\tReplacement conditions (4 marks — 1 each): puncture, crack/cut, discolouration, exposure of inner insulation layer.\n162\t\n163\t**B3** (8 marks):\n164\tJHA explanation (3 marks): systematic process to identify hazards, assess risks, and determine controls for each work step.\n165\tFive steps (5 marks — 1 each): (1) Identify task and break into steps, (2) Identify hazards at each step, (3) Assess risk level (likelihood x severity), (4) Determine control measures, (5) Document and communicate.\n166\t\n167\t**B4** (8 marks — 2 each):\n168\ta) BMS ECU: Monitors battery SOC, SOH, cell voltages, temperatures; manages charge/discharge.\n169\tb) MCU: Controls motor performance, inverter operation, regenerative braking.\n170\tc) VCU: Coordinates overall vehicle systems; central controller.\n171\td) Thermal Management ECU: Controls cooling system — pumps, valves, fan operation.\n172\t\n173\t**B5** (8 marks):\n174\tICE items absent in BEV (4 marks — 1 each): engine oil/filter, spark plugs, timing belt, transmission fluid.\n175\tBEV-unique items (4 marks — 1 each): HV battery coolant, HV insulation resistance check, battery SOH assessment, software update check.\n176\t\n177\t### Section C\n178\t\n179\t**C1** (12 marks):\n180\ta) Five hazards (5 marks — 1 each): electrical, chemical, mechanical, thermal, ergonomic.\n181\tb) Preparation steps (7 marks — 1 each, max 7): PPE inspection and donning, work bay cleaning, barrier placement, equipment verification (fire extinguisher, rescue hook), tool inspection, JHA completion, toolbox talk.\n182\t\n183\t**C2** (14 marks):\n184\ta) Seven steps (7 marks — 1 each): receive vehicle/verify info, exterior walk-around, interior check, connect diagnostic tool, fault code reading, BEV system checks (battery/powertrain/thermal/charging), prepare report.\n185\tb) Interpretation (7 marks):\n186\t- SOH 94%: GOOD — within acceptable range for service age (1m)\n187\t- Cell #47 deviation: DEFECTIVE — 0.13V deviation exceeds +/- 0.05V tolerance. Indicates potential cell degradation. Recommend further investigation / battery module assessment (2m)\n188\t- Insulation resistance 850 ohm/V: GOOD — exceeds minimum 500 ohm/V threshold (1m)\n189\t- P0A1F fault code: Stored fault related to battery energy control. Cross-reference with TSB. May be related to cell imbalance. Recommend clearing and monitoring for recurrence (3m)\n190\t\n191\t**C3** (14 marks):\n192\ta) Cabin air filter (4 marks): Open glove box, release stops, access filter housing, remove old filter noting airflow direction, install new filter with correct orientation, close housing, refit glove box.\n193\tb) Fluid checks (5 marks): Brake fluid — locate reservoir, check MIN/MAX, test moisture content, top up with correct spec if low (investigate leaks). HV coolant — visual check ONLY, do NOT open cap, report if below MIN.\n194\tc) Underbody inspection (5 marks): Battery pack housing (dents/cracks/leaks), HV cable routing, suspension components, brake pads/discs, tyre inner sidewalls. Critical: lift pads must be at manufacturer-specified points to avoid battery pack damage.\n195\t\n196\t---\n197\t\n198\t## BANK SOALAN Q-FORMAT / Q-FORMAT QUESTION BANK\n199\t\n200\t*Format JPK WIM — Jumlah: 35 soalan, 25 markah / Total: 35 questions, 25 marks*\n201\t*Taburan / Distribution: HV Safety 40% (Q1–Q14), PPE 30% (Q15–Q25), Battery Basics 30% (Q26–Q35)*\n202\t\n203\t---\n204\t\n205\t### Bahagian A / Section A — Pilihan Berganda / Multiple Choice (20 markah / marks)\n206\t\n207\t*Arahan: Pilih jawapan TERBAIK. Setiap soalan bernilai 1 markah. / Choose the BEST answer. Each question carries 1 mark.*\n208\t\n209\t**Q1.** OSHA 1994 (Act 514) in Malaysia is enforced by: A. JPJ  B. DOSH  C. Energy Commission  D. DOE  [CoCU C01.1.1]\n210\t**Q2.** The correct HV de-energisation first step is: A. Remove service plug  B. Disconnect 12V battery  C. Switch READY OFF and secure key in safety box  D. Open bonnet  [CoCU C01.1.2]\n211\t**Q3.** Minimum waiting time after HV de-energisation before zero-energy verification: A. 30 seconds  B. 2 minutes  C. 5–10 minutes  D. 30 minutes  [CoCU C01.1.2]\n212\t**Q4.** Zero-energy verification must be performed at: A. Battery positive terminal only  B. One point chosen by technician  C. Minimum three separate HV circuit points  D. 12V battery terminals only  [CoCU C01.1.2]\n213\t**Q5.** The Insulation Monitoring Device (IMD) detects: A. Battery SOC  B. Insulation breakdown between HV circuit and chassis  C. Coolant leaks  D. Motor bearing wear  [CoCU C01.2.3]\n214\t**Q6.** CAT III — 1000V is mandatory for BEV HV work because: A. It is the cheapest rating  B. It protects against transient overvoltage in HV distribution environments  C. CAT II is rated for higher voltage  D. Only required for 400V systems  [CoCU C01.1.3]\n215\t**Q7.** Minimum acceptable HV insulation resistance for a BEV in service: A. 100 Ω/V  B. 300 Ω/V  C. 500 Ω/V  D. 1000 Ω/V  [CoCU C01.2.3]\n216\t**Q8.** A non-conductive rescue hook in a BEV workshop is used to: A. Lift battery packs  B. Separate a casualty from a live HV source without physical contact  C. Test cable insulation  D. Secure vehicle to lift  [CoCU C01.1.2]\n217\t**Q9.** Which Act governs scheduled waste management in a BEV workshop? A. OSHA 1994  B. Road Transport Act 1987  C. Environmental Quality Act 1974  D. Energy Commission Act 2001  [CoCU C01.1.5]\n218\t**Q10.** The JHA must be completed: A. Once per month  B. Only after an incident occurs  C. Before each work activity for each vehicle  D. Only for new vehicles  [CoCU C01.1.4]\n219\t**Q11.** What is the Hierarchy of Controls' MOST effective level? A. PPE  B. Administrative controls  C. Engineering controls  D. Elimination  [CoCU C01.1.4]\n220\t**Q12.** A BEV fire extinguisher in the work bay must include: A. Class A (water) only  B. CO₂ and Class D (dry powder for metal fires) types  C. Any type available  D. Foam only  [CoCU C01.1.2]\n221\t**Q13.** The interlock system opens main contactors when: A. Battery SOC drops below 20%  B. Coolant temperature exceeds 60°C  C. The service plug/interlock connector is removed  D. 12V auxiliary battery is disconnected  [CoCU C01.1.2]\n222\t**Q14.** Scheduled Waste SW305 in a BEV workshop refers to: A. Used engine oil  B. Glycol-based HV coolant  C. Worn brake pads  D. Used cabin air filter  [CoCU C01.1.5]\n223\t**Q15.** Class 0 insulated gloves protect up to: A. 500V  B. 1000V  C. 7500V  D. 17000V  [CoCU C01.1.2]\n224\t**Q16.** The pre-use inspection of insulated gloves requires: A. Immersion in water  B. Roll-cuff air-squeeze test to detect leaks  C. Multimeter continuity test  D. Colour check only  [CoCU C01.1.2]\n225\t**Q17.** HV-rated insulated hand tools must be rated at minimum: A. 500V  B. 750V  C. 1000V  D. 1500V  [CoCU C01.1.3]\n226\t**Q18.** Arc-rated flash suits are rated in units of: A. kPa  B. Amperes  C. cal/cm²  D. Watts  [CoCU C01.1.2]\n227\t**Q19.** Insulated gloves with chemical discolouration must be: A. Cleaned with solvent and reused  B. Stored separately for 1 month  C. Immediately withdrawn from service  D. Used only for LV work  [CoCU C01.1.2]\n228\t**Q20.** HV PPE when not in use must be stored: A. On the workshop floor  B. Folded in a metal toolbox  C. In a cool, dry, purpose-made bag away from chemicals and sharp objects  D. Hung in direct sunlight  [CoCU C01.1.2]\n229\t\n230\t### Bahagian B / Section B — Soalan Senario Bermarkah / Graded Scenario Questions (5 markah / marks)\n231\t\n232\t*Jawab SEMUA soalan. Setiap soalan bernilai 1 markah. / Answer ALL questions. Each question carries 1 mark.*\n233\t\n234\t**Q21.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] A technician enters a BEV maintenance bay and finds wet floor from an unknown liquid and no fire extinguisher in the bay. Describe the required actions before commencing any work. [CoCU C01.1.4 / C01.1.6]\n235\t\n236\t*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* The technician must NOT commence work. Notify the supervisor immediately. Identify the liquid — if glycol-based HV coolant, it is Schedule Waste SW305 requiring trained clean-up with PPE. Install wet floor signs and barriers. Obtain and position the correct fire extinguisher (CO₂ and Class D) before any activity begins. Complete the JHA checklist to confirm workplace compliance. Document the finding. No HV work may begin until all hazards are eliminated.\n237\t\n238\t**Q22.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] During a BEV health check scan, fault code P0AFA (HV Battery Current Sensor Performance) is stored. Describe your diagnostic response and what this fault indicates. [CoCU C01.2.4]\n239\t\n240\t*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Record the fault code and freeze-frame data. P0AFA indicates the BMS current sensor is reporting values outside expected parameters, possibly caused by sensor failure, corroded connector, or damaged wiring at the HV junction box. Inspect the current sensor harness and connector for moisture, corrosion, and pin damage. Measure sensor supply voltage and ground. Compare sensor readings against manufacturer's specification table. If sensor is confirmed faulty, raise a CU C02 rectification work order. Document all findings in the BEV Health Check Report before returning the vehicle.\n241\t\n242\t**Q23.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] Cell #7 in Module 3 reads 3.67V while all other cells read 3.82–3.84V — a deviation of 0.15V. Interpret this finding and state the required action. [CoCU C01.2.3]\n243\t\n244\t*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* A deviation of 0.15V significantly exceeds the acceptable tolerance of ±0.05V. This indicates Cell #7 has substantially reduced capacity relative to the rest of the module, likely due to cell degradation, internal micro-short circuit, or electrolyte depletion. The BMS will limit the entire pack's charge and discharge capacity to protect this weakest cell, reducing vehicle range. Document the finding in the health check diagnostic report. Escalate to a CU C02 Battery Management System Rectification work order for module inspection and possible replacement.\n245\t\n246\t**Q24.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] During glove pre-use inspection, the roll-cuff squeeze test causes immediate air escape before pressure is applied. State what this indicates and the required action. [CoCU C01.1.2]\n247\t\n248\t*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Immediate air loss before pressure application indicates a significant puncture or rupture in the insulating rubber — the glove no longer provides reliable HV electrical protection. The technician must immediately remove the gloves from service. Label them clearly \"DEFECTIVE — DO NOT USE\" and quarantine them to prevent accidental reuse by others. Obtain a replacement pair of Class 0 or Class 1 insulated gloves in verified good condition and inspect them before any HV work proceeds. Report the defective PPE to the Safety and Health Officer for investigation and replacement procurement.\n249\t\n250\t**Q25.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] A BEV arrives for 60,000 km service. Workshop records show HV coolant was topped up at 45,000 km with no root cause identified. Describe how you should handle the fluid check at this service. [CoCU C01.3.4 / C01.3.5]\n251\t\n252\t*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Previous unexplained coolant loss indicates an unresolved leak that was masked without investigation. Before routine fluid checks, conduct a HV coolant circuit pressure test to locate the leak source. Inspect all coolant hoses, clamps, pump shaft seals, cooler matrix connections, and expansion tank cap seal. Visually check the battery pack underside and inverter housing for coolant staining. Do NOT simply top up the coolant again — identify and rectify the root cause first. Document findings fully in the work order and advise the customer that rectification work is required before the service can be completed safely.\n253\t\n254\t### Bahagian C / Section C — Senario Pengayaan / Enrichment Practice Scenarios (0 markah / marks)\n255\t\n256\t*Soalan latihan untuk kefahaman mendalam. / Practice questions for deeper understanding.*\n257\t\n258\t**Q26.** Describe the correct sequence for donning HV PPE before commencing BEV HV circuit work, including the order each item is put on. [CoCU C01.1.2]\n259\t\n260\t*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Correct donning sequence: (1) Safety boots — foot protection before all else. (2) Arc-rated overalls or suit over all clothing. (3) Safety glasses and face shield. (4) Class 0 or Class 1 insulated gloves — put on LAST, inspected with roll-cuff squeeze test first. Gloves must overlap suit sleeves. Removal order reverses: gloves first, then face shield, then suit. Never touch the outer surface of insulated gloves with bare hands after donning. Inspect cuff markings remain legible before each use.\n261\t\n262\t**Q27.** Explain the difference between a hazard and a risk in the BEV workshop context. Provide one BEV-specific example of each. [CoCU C01.1.4]\n263\t\n264\t*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* A hazard is a source with potential to cause harm — it exists independently of anyone being present. A risk is the probability that the hazard will cause harm, combined with the severity of that harm. BEV hazard example: an orange HV cable with visibly cracked insulation running beneath the battery pack — the damaged insulation is the hazard. BEV risk example: a technician reaching under the pack to retrieve a dropped tool without de-energising first — the probability (high if distracted) multiplied by severity (electrocution/fatal) = HIGH risk requiring immediate engineering or administrative control before work proceeds.\n265\t\n266\t**Q28.** Explain what happens electrically if the pre-charge circuit fails and why this causes damage to the inverter. [CoCU C01.2.3]\n267\t\n268\t*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* If the pre-charge circuit fails, the main positive contactor closes directly against uncharged inverter DC-link capacitors. Since capacitors at 0V present near-zero impedance, the battery pack discharges into them in an instantaneous current surge of hundreds of amperes. This inrush current: (1) arc-welds the main contactor contacts in the closed position (weld failure — contactor can no longer open), permanently connecting the battery to the HV bus; (2) may destroy the inverter's IGBT power transistors and capacitor dielectric through over-current stress. The pre-charge resistor (typically 50-200 Ω) prevents this by limiting inrush and allowing capacitors to charge gradually over 0.5-2 seconds before the main contactor engages.\n269\t\n270\t**Q29.** A flood-damaged BEV is delivered to your workshop. List the unique hazards and the special handling protocol required before any work begins. [CoCU C01.1.4 / C01.1.6]\n271\t\n272\t*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Unique hazards: (1) Submerged cells may have internal micro-short circuits causing delayed thermal runaway hours or days after recovery. (2) Moisture inside battery housing creates unpredictable HV-to-chassis leakage paths not detected by standard IMD. (3) HV connector seals may be compromised, allowing water ingress. (4) Coolant and electrolyte may have mixed with contaminated floodwater — unknown chemical hazard. Special protocol: do NOT attempt READY ON under any circumstances. Park in an isolated open area at least 10 m from structures and other vehicles. Post continuous fire watch for minimum 48 hours. Do NOT open battery housing without specialist equipment. Notify the manufacturer's emergency technical hotline and document with photographs before any handling. Inform the workshop Safety and Health Officer before proceeding.\n273\t\n274\t**Q30.** Explain the three-point zero-energy verification procedure for a BEV using a CAT III — 1000V multimeter. [CoCU C01.1.2]\n275\t\n276\t*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Step 1: Before testing the BEV, verify multimeter function by testing on a confirmed live LV circuit — confirms meter leads and fuses are functional. Step 2: After full HV de-energisation and 5-10 minute capacitor discharge wait, set meter to DC Voltage, 1000V range. Measure POINT 1: HV junction box main positive terminal to chassis ground — must read 0V DC. Step 3: Measure POINT 2: HV junction box main negative terminal to chassis ground — must read 0V DC. Step 4: Measure POINT 3: Battery pack positive terminal (accessed via service port) to chassis ground — must read 0V DC. All three points must confirm zero voltage before touching any HV component. Record readings on the work order before proceeding.\n277\t\n278\t**Q31.** Describe the Hierarchy of Controls. Give one BEV HV-specific example of a control at each level. [CoCU C01.1.4]\n279\t\n280\t*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Five levels, most to least effective: (1) Elimination — remove the hazard entirely. BEV example: permanently decommission and dispose of a thermally compromised battery pack rather than attempting repair. (2) Substitution — replace with a less hazardous alternative. BEV example: use a wireless HV immobiliser system instead of manual cable disconnection for certain diagnostic operations. (3) Engineering controls — physically isolate the hazard. BEV example: automatic interlock that opens main contactors and energises warning lights when the battery housing is opened. (4) Administrative controls — change work procedures. BEV example: two-person rule requiring a second qualified technician to be present during all HV disconnection work. (5) PPE — last resort only. BEV example: Class 0 insulated gloves, arc-rated suit, and face shield for any HV component contact.\n281\t\n282\t**Q32.** At what SOH percentage is traction battery pack replacement typically recommended? Explain why the BMS limits performance before this threshold. [CoCU C01.2.3]\n283\t\n284\t*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Pack replacement is typically recommended when SOH drops below 80%. Before this threshold, the BMS progressively limits performance to protect degraded cells: charge current is reduced to prevent voltage overshoot in weaker cells; discharge cut-off voltage is raised to prevent over-discharge of the lowest-capacity cells; available energy (usable kWh) is restricted to the capacity of the weakest cell in the series string, reducing range; regenerative braking current is reduced. These limitations protect the weakest cells from thermal runaway caused by over-charge or over-discharge stress, but they signal to the driver that pack replacement is approaching. Below 70% SOH, restrictions become severe enough to significantly impair daily usability.\n285\t\n286\t**Q33.** Describe how to identify a BEV's HV system voltage before PPE selection. Why does the system voltage affect PPE class choice? [CoCU C01.3.2]\n287\t\n288\t*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Identification steps: (1) Check the manufacturer's workshop manual or Technical Service Bulletin using the vehicle VIN to confirm nominal HV system voltage (commonly 400V DC or 800V DC in current models). (2) Locate the HV warning label on the battery pack housing — Malaysian regulatory requirements specify that operating voltage must be displayed. (3) If uncertain, use a non-contact voltage detector at a safe distance to confirm HV presence before close approach. PPE class selection is affected because: Class 0 insulated gloves are proof-tested to 1000V and rated for use up to 500V AC / 750V DC systems. Class 1 gloves (tested to 7500V) should be used for 800V DC systems where transient overvoltage could exceed Class 0 limits. The wrong PPE class provides insufficient arc energy protection for the actual system voltage.\n289\t\n290\t**Q34.** A BEV owner reports gradual range reduction over 2 years. List three battery-related causes a technician should investigate during a health check. [CoCU C01.2.3 / C01.2.4]\n291\t\n292\t*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Three causes to investigate: (1) Cell capacity degradation — cyclic and calendar aging reduce individual cell Ah capacity. Measure each cell's SOH via BMS diagnostic data; SOH below 80% confirms significant capacity loss as the primary cause. (2) Cell imbalance — one or more weak cells limit the entire pack's usable capacity. The BMS stops discharge when the weakest cell reaches cut-off voltage even though other cells remain charged. Identify by reviewing individual cell voltage spread at end-of-discharge in the diagnostic report — deviations exceeding ±0.05V indicate imbalance. (3) Increased internal resistance from electrolyte decomposition — causes greater voltage sag under load, reducing usable energy without obvious SOH change. Identify by comparing cell internal resistance measurements against baseline values in the manufacturer specification table.\n293\t\n294\t**Q35.** State the specific regulatory obligations for disposing of BEV HV glycol coolant and 12V lead-acid batteries, including relevant legislation. [CoCU C01.1.5]\n295\t\n296\t*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* HV glycol coolant — classified as Scheduled Waste SW305 under the Environmental Quality (Scheduled Wastes) Regulations 2005 made under the Environmental Quality Act 1974 (Act 127). Must be stored in sealed, labelled containers; cannot be disposed via drain, soil, or municipal waste. Must be consigned to a DOE-licensed Scheduled Waste contractor using the prescribed waste manifest form. Workshop must retain manifest records for minimum 3 years. 12V lead-acid batteries — classified as Scheduled Waste SW102 (used lead-acid batteries) under the same Regulations. Must be returned to licensed lead recyclers or manufacturer take-back programmes. Never crush, incinerate, or dispose in landfill. Penalty for improper disposal: up to RM500,000 fine and/or 5 years imprisonment under EQA 1974 Section 34B.\n297\t\n298\t---\n299\t\n300\t### Skema Pemarkahan Q-Format / Q-Format Marking Scheme (25 Markah / Marks)\n301\t\n302\t**Bahagian A / Section A (Q1–Q20 — 1 markah setiap soalan / 1 mark each):**\n303\t\n304\t| Q | Jwp | Q | Jwp | Q | Jwp | Q | Jwp |\n305\t|---|-----|---|-----|---|-----|---|-----|\n306\t| Q1 | B | Q6 | B | Q11 | D | Q16 | B |\n307\t| Q2 | C | Q7 | C | Q12 | B | Q17 | C |\n308\t| Q3 | C | Q8 | B | Q13 | C | Q18 | C |\n309\t| Q4 | C | Q9 | C | Q14 | B | Q19 | C |\n310\t| Q5 | B | Q10 | C | Q15 | B | Q20 | C |\n311\t\n312\t**Bahagian B / Section B (Q21–Q25 — 1 markah setiap soalan / 1 mark each):**\n313\tAward 1 mark for responses that correctly identify the key safety action(s) and reference relevant CoCU assessment criteria. See sample answers above.\n314\t\n315\t**Bahagian C / Section C (Q26–Q35 — Pengayaan / Enrichment — 0 markah / 0 marks):**\n316\tSample answers to be provided by trainer for guided discussion.\n317\t\n318\t**Jumlah Q-Format / Q-Format Total: 25 markah / marks**\n319\t*Lulus / Pass: 60% = 15 markah/marks. Rujukan / References: CoCU C01 — semua kriteria penilaian / all assessment criteria.*\n320\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"794df4fc-0917-40f0-aba2-b2d02fc662d7","uuid":"dc914c19-0c29-4baf-a648-89ad3dc65441","timestamp":"2026-04-16T15:00:58.908Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C01\\KA.md","content":"# PENILAIAN PENGETAHUAN / KNOWLEDGE ASSESSMENT\n\n**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C01/KA\n**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** MERAH JAMBU / PINK\n\n---\n\n## Competency Unit: Perform BEV Scheduled Maintenance (G452-010-3:2023-C01)\n\n| Field | Detail |\n|-------|--------|\n| Duration | 1 hour 30 minutes |\n| Total Marks | 100 |\n| Passing Mark | 60% (60 marks) |\n| References Covered | KP(1/3), KP(2/3), KP(3/3) |\n\n---\n\n## Instructions to Candidates\n\n1. This assessment consists of THREE (3) sections: A, B, and C\n2. Answer ALL questions in ALL sections\n3. Write your answers in the spaces provided or on the answer sheet\n4. Marks for each question are indicated in brackets\n\n---\n\n## SECTION A: Multiple Choice Questions (20 marks)\n\nChoose the BEST answer for each question. Each question carries 2 marks.\n\n**A1.** Which Act governs occupational safety and health in a BEV maintenance workshop in Malaysia?\n- A. Employment Act 1955\n- B. Occupational Safety and Health Act 1994\n- C. Road Transport Act 1987\n- D. Factories and Machinery Act 1967\n\n**A2.** What is the minimum rated voltage for insulated gloves used in BEV HV work (Class 0)?\n- A. 500V AC\n- B. 750V AC\n- C. 1000V AC\n- D. 7500V AC\n\n**A3.** What type of fire extinguisher is required for lithium battery fires?\n- A. Class A (water)\n- B. Class B (foam)\n- C. Class C (CO2)\n- D. Class D (dry powder for metal fires)\n\n**A4.** What is the function of the Insulation Monitoring Device (IMD) in a BEV?\n- A. Monitor engine oil pressure\n- B. Monitor insulation resistance between HV circuits and vehicle chassis\n- C. Monitor cabin temperature\n- D. Monitor tyre pressure\n\n**A5.** How many characters are in a Vehicle Identification Number (VIN)?\n- A. 10\n- B. 13\n- C. 15\n- D. 17\n\n**A6.** Which CAT rating is required for a digital multimeter used on BEV HV circuits?\n- A. CAT I — 600V\n- B. CAT II — 600V\n- C. CAT III — 1000V\n- D. CAT IV — 300V\n\n**A7.** What is the typical acceptable cell voltage deviation in a BEV battery pack?\n- A. +/- 0.005V\n- B. +/- 0.05V\n- C. +/- 0.5V\n- D. +/- 5.0V\n\n**A8.** Which of the following is NOT a BEV scheduled maintenance item?\n- A. Cabin air filter replacement\n- B. Engine oil change\n- C. Brake fluid replacement\n- D. 12V auxiliary battery check\n\n**A9.** In the hierarchy of controls, which is the FIRST (most effective) level?\n- A. PPE\n- B. Administrative controls\n- C. Elimination\n- D. Engineering controls\n\n**A10.** Why are BEV-specific coolants non-conductive?\n- A. To prevent engine corrosion\n- B. To maintain HV insulation between coolant circuit and chassis\n- C. To reduce cost\n- D. To improve engine performance\n\n---\n\n## SECTION B: Short Answer Questions (40 marks)\n\nAnswer ALL questions. Provide clear and concise responses.\n\n**B1.** (8 marks) List FOUR (4) regulatory authorities and their respective legislation governing BEV maintenance activities in Malaysia. Present your answer in a table format.\n\n**B2.** (8 marks) Describe the procedure for inspecting insulated gloves before use. State FOUR (4) conditions that require immediate replacement.\n\n**B3.** (8 marks) Explain the Job Hazard Analysis (JHA) process. List the FIVE (5) steps involved.\n\n**B4.** (8 marks) Describe the function of the following ECUs found in a BEV:\n- a) Battery Management System (BMS) ECU\n- b) Motor Control Unit (MCU)\n- c) Vehicle Control Unit (VCU)\n- d) Thermal Management ECU\n\n**B5.** (8 marks) Compare BEV scheduled maintenance with ICE vehicle maintenance. List FOUR (4) maintenance items present in ICE vehicles but absent in BEVs, and FOUR (4) items unique to BEVs.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION C: Structured Questions (40 marks)\n\nAnswer ALL questions with detailed explanations.\n\n**C1.** (12 marks) A BEV arrives at your workshop for its first scheduled maintenance at 15,000 km.\n\na) Describe the FIVE (5) types of hazards you would identify in a JHA for this service. (5 marks)\n\nb) Explain the workplace preparation procedure you would follow before commencing work. Include at least FIVE (5) key preparation steps. (7 marks)\n\n**C2.** (14 marks) You are performing a BEV health check on a 2024 model year vehicle.\n\na) Describe the systematic BEV health check procedure from vehicle reception to report submission. Include at least SEVEN (7) major steps. (7 marks)\n\nb) During the diagnostic scan, you discover the following:\n- Battery SOH: 94%\n- Cell voltage deviation: Cell #47 reads 3.72V while all other cells read 3.85V\n- HV insulation resistance: 850 ohm/V\n- Fault code P0A1F stored (Battery Energy Control Module)\n\nInterpret each finding and state what action is required for each. (7 marks)\n\n**C3.** (14 marks) Describe the complete procedure for the following BEV maintenance activities:\n\na) Cabin air filter replacement — include all steps from glove box access to completion. (4 marks)\n\nb) Fluid level checks — describe the procedure for checking brake fluid and HV coolant. Include safety considerations for each. (5 marks)\n\nc) Underbody inspection — describe what items to inspect when the vehicle is raised on a lift. Include the critical safety consideration regarding lift point placement. (5 marks)\n\n---\n\n## SKEMA PEMARKAHAN / MARKING SCHEME\n\n### Section A (20 marks — 2 marks each)\nA1: B | A2: C | A3: D | A4: B | A5: D | A6: C | A7: B | A8: B | A9: C | A10: B\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1** (8 marks — 2 marks each authority/legislation pair):\n1. JPJ — Road Transport Act 1987 (Act 333)\n2. DOSH — OSHA 1994 (Act 514)\n3. Energy Commission — Electricity Supply Act 1990 (Act 447) / Energy Commission Act 2001\n4. DOE — Environmental Quality Act 1974 (Act 127)\n\n**B2** (8 marks):\nProcedure (4 marks): Roll cuff towards fingers to trap air (1m). Squeeze gently to check for leaks (1m). Visually inspect entire surface (1m). Check markings are legible (1m).\nReplacement conditions (4 marks — 1 each): puncture, crack/cut, discolouration, exposure of inner insulation layer.\n\n**B3** (8 marks):\nJHA explanation (3 marks): systematic process to identify hazards, assess risks, and determine controls for each work step.\nFive steps (5 marks — 1 each): (1) Identify task and break into steps, (2) Identify hazards at each step, (3) Assess risk level (likelihood x severity), (4) Determine control measures, (5) Document and communicate.\n\n**B4** (8 marks — 2 each):\na) BMS ECU: Monitors battery SOC, SOH, cell voltages, temperatures; manages charge/discharge.\nb) MCU: Controls motor performance, inverter operation, regenerative braking.\nc) VCU: Coordinates overall vehicle systems; central controller.\nd) Thermal Management ECU: Controls cooling system — pumps, valves, fan operation.\n\n**B5** (8 marks):\nICE items absent in BEV (4 marks — 1 each): engine oil/filter, spark plugs, timing belt, transmission fluid.\nBEV-unique items (4 marks — 1 each): HV battery coolant, HV insulation resistance check, battery SOH assessment, software update check.\n\n### Section C\n\n**C1** (12 marks):\na) Five hazards (5 marks — 1 each): electrical, chemical, mechanical, thermal, ergonomic.\nb) Preparation steps (7 marks — 1 each, max 7): PPE inspection and donning, work bay cleaning, barrier placement, equipment verification (fire extinguisher, rescue hook), tool inspection, JHA completion, toolbox talk.\n\n**C2** (14 marks):\na) Seven steps (7 marks — 1 each): receive vehicle/verify info, exterior walk-around, interior check, connect diagnostic tool, fault code reading, BEV system checks (battery/powertrain/thermal/charging), prepare report.\nb) Interpretation (7 marks):\n- SOH 94%: GOOD — within acceptable range for service age (1m)\n- Cell #47 deviation: DEFECTIVE — 0.13V deviation exceeds +/- 0.05V tolerance. Indicates potential cell degradation. Recommend further investigation / battery module assessment (2m)\n- Insulation resistance 850 ohm/V: GOOD — exceeds minimum 500 ohm/V threshold (1m)\n- P0A1F fault code: Stored fault related to battery energy control. Cross-reference with TSB. May be related to cell imbalance. Recommend clearing and monitoring for recurrence (3m)\n\n**C3** (14 marks):\na) Cabin air filter (4 marks): Open glove box, release stops, access filter housing, remove old filter noting airflow direction, install new filter with correct orientation, close housing, refit glove box.\nb) Fluid checks (5 marks): Brake fluid — locate reservoir, check MIN/MAX, test moisture content, top up with correct spec if low (investigate leaks). HV coolant — visual check ONLY, do NOT open cap, report if below MIN.\nc) Underbody inspection (5 marks): Battery pack housing (dents/cracks/leaks), HV cable routing, suspension components, brake pads/discs, tyre inner sidewalls. Critical: lift pads must be at manufacturer-specified points to avoid battery pack damage.\n\n---\n\n## BANK SOALAN Q-FORMAT / Q-FORMAT QUESTION BANK\n\n*Format JPK WIM — Jumlah: 35 soalan, 25 markah / Total: 35 questions, 25 marks*\n*Taburan / Distribution: HV Safety 40% (Q1–Q14), PPE 30% (Q15–Q25), Battery Basics 30% (Q26–Q35)*\n\n---\n\n### Bahagian A / Section A — Pilihan Berganda / Multiple Choice (20 markah / marks)\n\n*Arahan: Pilih jawapan TERBAIK. Setiap soalan bernilai 1 markah. / Choose the BEST answer. Each question carries 1 mark.*\n\n**Q1.** OSHA 1994 (Act 514) in Malaysia is enforced by: A. JPJ  B. DOSH  C. Energy Commission  D. DOE  [CoCU C01.1.1]\n**Q2.** The correct HV de-energisation first step is: A. Remove service plug  B. Disconnect 12V battery  C. Switch READY OFF and secure key in safety box  D. Open bonnet  [CoCU C01.1.2]\n**Q3.** Minimum waiting time after HV de-energisation before zero-energy verification: A. 30 seconds  B. 2 minutes  C. 5–10 minutes  D. 30 minutes  [CoCU C01.1.2]\n**Q4.** Zero-energy verification must be performed at: A. Battery positive terminal only  B. One point chosen by technician  C. Minimum three separate HV circuit points  D. 12V battery terminals only  [CoCU C01.1.2]\n**Q5.** The Insulation Monitoring Device (IMD) detects: A. Battery SOC  B. Insulation breakdown between HV circuit and chassis  C. Coolant leaks  D. Motor bearing wear  [CoCU C01.2.3]\n**Q6.** CAT III — 1000V is mandatory for BEV HV work because: A. It is the cheapest rating  B. It protects against transient overvoltage in HV distribution environments  C. CAT II is rated for higher voltage  D. Only required for 400V systems  [CoCU C01.1.3]\n**Q7.** Minimum acceptable HV insulation resistance for a BEV in service: A. 100 Ω/V  B. 300 Ω/V  C. 500 Ω/V  D. 1000 Ω/V  [CoCU C01.2.3]\n**Q8.** A non-conductive rescue hook in a BEV workshop is used to: A. Lift battery packs  B. Separate a casualty from a live HV source without physical contact  C. Test cable insulation  D. Secure vehicle to lift  [CoCU C01.1.2]\n**Q9.** Which Act governs scheduled waste management in a BEV workshop? A. OSHA 1994  B. Road Transport Act 1987  C. Environmental Quality Act 1974  D. Energy Commission Act 2001  [CoCU C01.1.5]\n**Q10.** The JHA must be completed: A. Once per month  B. Only after an incident occurs  C. Before each work activity for each vehicle  D. Only for new vehicles  [CoCU C01.1.4]\n**Q11.** What is the Hierarchy of Controls' MOST effective level? A. PPE  B. Administrative controls  C. Engineering controls  D. Elimination  [CoCU C01.1.4]\n**Q12.** A BEV fire extinguisher in the work bay must include: A. Class A (water) only  B. CO₂ and Class D (dry powder for metal fires) types  C. Any type available  D. Foam only  [CoCU C01.1.2]\n**Q13.** The interlock system opens main contactors when: A. Battery SOC drops below 20%  B. Coolant temperature exceeds 60°C  C. The service plug/interlock connector is removed  D. 12V auxiliary battery is disconnected  [CoCU C01.1.2]\n**Q14.** Scheduled Waste SW305 in a BEV workshop refers to: A. Used engine oil  B. Glycol-based HV coolant  C. Worn brake pads  D. Used cabin air filter  [CoCU C01.1.5]\n**Q15.** Class 0 insulated gloves protect up to: A. 500V  B. 1000V  C. 7500V  D. 17000V  [CoCU C01.1.2]\n**Q16.** The pre-use inspection of insulated gloves requires: A. Immersion in water  B. Roll-cuff air-squeeze test to detect leaks  C. Multimeter continuity test  D. Colour check only  [CoCU C01.1.2]\n**Q17.** HV-rated insulated hand tools must be rated at minimum: A. 500V  B. 750V  C. 1000V  D. 1500V  [CoCU C01.1.3]\n**Q18.** Arc-rated flash suits are rated in units of: A. kPa  B. Amperes  C. cal/cm²  D. Watts  [CoCU C01.1.2]\n**Q19.** Insulated gloves with chemical discolouration must be: A. Cleaned with solvent and reused  B. Stored separately for 1 month  C. Immediately withdrawn from service  D. Used only for LV work  [CoCU C01.1.2]\n**Q20.** HV PPE when not in use must be stored: A. On the workshop floor  B. Folded in a metal toolbox  C. In a cool, dry, purpose-made bag away from chemicals and sharp objects  D. Hung in direct sunlight  [CoCU C01.1.2]\n\n### Bahagian B / Section B — Soalan Senario Bermarkah / Graded Scenario Questions (5 markah / marks)\n\n*Jawab SEMUA soalan. Setiap soalan bernilai 1 markah. / Answer ALL questions. Each question carries 1 mark.*\n\n**Q21.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] A technician enters a BEV maintenance bay and finds wet floor from an unknown liquid and no fire extinguisher in the bay. Describe the required actions before commencing any work. [CoCU C01.1.4 / C01.1.6]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* The technician must NOT commence work. Notify the supervisor immediately. Identify the liquid — if glycol-based HV coolant, it is Schedule Waste SW305 requiring trained clean-up with PPE. Install wet floor signs and barriers. Obtain and position the correct fire extinguisher (CO₂ and Class D) before any activity begins. Complete the JHA checklist to confirm workplace compliance. Document the finding. No HV work may begin until all hazards are eliminated.\n\n**Q22.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] During a BEV health check scan, fault code P0AFA (HV Battery Current Sensor Performance) is stored. Describe your diagnostic response and what this fault indicates. [CoCU C01.2.4]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Record the fault code and freeze-frame data. P0AFA indicates the BMS current sensor is reporting values outside expected parameters, possibly caused by sensor failure, corroded connector, or damaged wiring at the HV junction box. Inspect the current sensor harness and connector for moisture, corrosion, and pin damage. Measure sensor supply voltage and ground. Compare sensor readings against manufacturer's specification table. If sensor is confirmed faulty, raise a CU C02 rectification work order. Document all findings in the BEV Health Check Report before returning the vehicle.\n\n**Q23.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] Cell #7 in Module 3 reads 3.67V while all other cells read 3.82–3.84V — a deviation of 0.15V. Interpret this finding and state the required action. [CoCU C01.2.3]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* A deviation of 0.15V significantly exceeds the acceptable tolerance of ±0.05V. This indicates Cell #7 has substantially reduced capacity relative to the rest of the module, likely due to cell degradation, internal micro-short circuit, or electrolyte depletion. The BMS will limit the entire pack's charge and discharge capacity to protect this weakest cell, reducing vehicle range. Document the finding in the health check diagnostic report. Escalate to a CU C02 Battery Management System Rectification work order for module inspection and possible replacement.\n\n**Q24.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] During glove pre-use inspection, the roll-cuff squeeze test causes immediate air escape before pressure is applied. State what this indicates and the required action. [CoCU C01.1.2]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Immediate air loss before pressure application indicates a significant puncture or rupture in the insulating rubber — the glove no longer provides reliable HV electrical protection. The technician must immediately remove the gloves from service. Label them clearly \"DEFECTIVE — DO NOT USE\" and quarantine them to prevent accidental reuse by others. Obtain a replacement pair of Class 0 or Class 1 insulated gloves in verified good condition and inspect them before any HV work proceeds. Report the defective PPE to the Safety and Health Officer for investigation and replacement procurement.\n\n**Q25.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] A BEV arrives for 60,000 km service. Workshop records show HV coolant was topped up at 45,000 km with no root cause identified. Describe how you should handle the fluid check at this service. [CoCU C01.3.4 / C01.3.5]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Previous unexplained coolant loss indicates an unresolved leak that was masked without investigation. Before routine fluid checks, conduct a HV coolant circuit pressure test to locate the leak source. Inspect all coolant hoses, clamps, pump shaft seals, cooler matrix connections, and expansion tank cap seal. Visually check the battery pack underside and inverter housing for coolant staining. Do NOT simply top up the coolant again — identify and rectify the root cause first. Document findings fully in the work order and advise the customer that rectification work is required before the service can be completed safely.\n\n### Bahagian C / Section C — Senario Pengayaan / Enrichment Practice Scenarios (0 markah / marks)\n\n*Soalan latihan untuk kefahaman mendalam. / Practice questions for deeper understanding.*\n\n**Q26.** Describe the correct sequence for donning HV PPE before commencing BEV HV circuit work, including the order each item is put on. [CoCU C01.1.2]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Correct donning sequence: (1) Safety boots — foot protection before all else. (2) Arc-rated overalls or suit over all clothing. (3) Safety glasses and face shield. (4) Class 0 or Class 1 insulated gloves — put on LAST, inspected with roll-cuff squeeze test first. Gloves must overlap suit sleeves. Removal order reverses: gloves first, then face shield, then suit. Never touch the outer surface of insulated gloves with bare hands after donning. Inspect cuff markings remain legible before each use.\n\n**Q27.** Explain the difference between a hazard and a risk in the BEV workshop context. Provide one BEV-specific example of each. [CoCU C01.1.4]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* A hazard is a source with potential to cause harm — it exists independently of anyone being present. A risk is the probability that the hazard will cause harm, combined with the severity of that harm. BEV hazard example: an orange HV cable with visibly cracked insulation running beneath the battery pack — the damaged insulation is the hazard. BEV risk example: a technician reaching under the pack to retrieve a dropped tool without de-energising first — the probability (high if distracted) multiplied by severity (electrocution/fatal) = HIGH risk requiring immediate engineering or administrative control before work proceeds.\n\n**Q28.** Explain what happens electrically if the pre-charge circuit fails and why this causes damage to the inverter. [CoCU C01.2.3]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* If the pre-charge circuit fails, the main positive contactor closes directly against uncharged inverter DC-link capacitors. Since capacitors at 0V present near-zero impedance, the battery pack discharges into them in an instantaneous current surge of hundreds of amperes. This inrush current: (1) arc-welds the main contactor contacts in the closed position (weld failure — contactor can no longer open), permanently connecting the battery to the HV bus; (2) may destroy the inverter's IGBT power transistors and capacitor dielectric through over-current stress. The pre-charge resistor (typically 50-200 Ω) prevents this by limiting inrush and allowing capacitors to charge gradually over 0.5-2 seconds before the main contactor engages.\n\n**Q29.** A flood-damaged BEV is delivered to your workshop. List the unique hazards and the special handling protocol required before any work begins. [CoCU C01.1.4 / C01.1.6]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Unique hazards: (1) Submerged cells may have internal micro-short circuits causing delayed thermal runaway hours or days after recovery. (2) Moisture inside battery housing creates unpredictable HV-to-chassis leakage paths not detected by standard IMD. (3) HV connector seals may be compromised, allowing water ingress. (4) Coolant and electrolyte may have mixed with contaminated floodwater — unknown chemical hazard. Special protocol: do NOT attempt READY ON under any circumstances. Park in an isolated open area at least 10 m from structures and other vehicles. Post continuous fire watch for minimum 48 hours. Do NOT open battery housing without specialist equipment. Notify the manufacturer's emergency technical hotline and document with photographs before any handling. Inform the workshop Safety and Health Officer before proceeding.\n\n**Q30.** Explain the three-point zero-energy verification procedure for a BEV using a CAT III — 1000V multimeter. [CoCU C01.1.2]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Step 1: Before testing the BEV, verify multimeter function by testing on a confirmed live LV circuit — confirms meter leads and fuses are functional. Step 2: After full HV de-energisation and 5-10 minute capacitor discharge wait, set meter to DC Voltage, 1000V range. Measure POINT 1: HV junction box main positive terminal to chassis ground — must read 0V DC. Step 3: Measure POINT 2: HV junction box main negative terminal to chassis ground — must read 0V DC. Step 4: Measure POINT 3: Battery pack positive terminal (accessed via service port) to chassis ground — must read 0V DC. All three points must confirm zero voltage before touching any HV component. Record readings on the work order before proceeding.\n\n**Q31.** Describe the Hierarchy of Controls. Give one BEV HV-specific example of a control at each level. [CoCU C01.1.4]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Five levels, most to least effective: (1) Elimination — remove the hazard entirely. BEV example: permanently decommission and dispose of a thermally compromised battery pack rather than attempting repair. (2) Substitution — replace with a less hazardous alternative. BEV example: use a wireless HV immobiliser system instead of manual cable disconnection for certain diagnostic operations. (3) Engineering controls — physically isolate the hazard. BEV example: automatic interlock that opens main contactors and energises warning lights when the battery housing is opened. (4) Administrative controls — change work procedures. BEV example: two-person rule requiring a second qualified technician to be present during all HV disconnection work. (5) PPE — last resort only. BEV example: Class 0 insulated gloves, arc-rated suit, and face shield for any HV component contact.\n\n**Q32.** At what SOH percentage is traction battery pack replacement typically recommended? Explain why the BMS limits performance before this threshold. [CoCU C01.2.3]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Pack replacement is typically recommended when SOH drops below 80%. Before this threshold, the BMS progressively limits performance to protect degraded cells: charge current is reduced to prevent voltage overshoot in weaker cells; discharge cut-off voltage is raised to prevent over-discharge of the lowest-capacity cells; available energy (usable kWh) is restricted to the capacity of the weakest cell in the series string, reducing range; regenerative braking current is reduced. These limitations protect the weakest cells from thermal runaway caused by over-charge or over-discharge stress, but they signal to the driver that pack replacement is approaching. Below 70% SOH, restrictions become severe enough to significantly impair daily usability.\n\n**Q33.** Describe how to identify a BEV's HV system voltage before PPE selection. Why does the system voltage affect PPE class choice? [CoCU C01.3.2]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Identification steps: (1) Check the manufacturer's workshop manual or Technical Service Bulletin using the vehicle VIN to confirm nominal HV system voltage (commonly 400V DC or 800V DC in current models). (2) Locate the HV warning label on the battery pack housing — Malaysian regulatory requirements specify that operating voltage must be displayed. (3) If uncertain, use a non-contact voltage detector at a safe distance to confirm HV presence before close approach. PPE class selection is affected because: Class 0 insulated gloves are proof-tested to 1000V and rated for use up to 500V AC / 750V DC systems. Class 1 gloves (tested to 7500V) should be used for 800V DC systems where transient overvoltage could exceed Class 0 limits. The wrong PPE class provides insufficient arc energy protection for the actual system voltage.\n\n**Q34.** A BEV owner reports gradual range reduction over 2 years. List three battery-related causes a technician should investigate during a health check. [CoCU C01.2.3 / C01.2.4]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Three causes to investigate: (1) Cell capacity degradation — cyclic and calendar aging reduce individual cell Ah capacity. Measure each cell's SOH via BMS diagnostic data; SOH below 80% confirms significant capacity loss as the primary cause. (2) Cell imbalance — one or more weak cells limit the entire pack's usable capacity. The BMS stops discharge when the weakest cell reaches cut-off voltage even though other cells remain charged. Identify by reviewing individual cell voltage spread at end-of-discharge in the diagnostic report — deviations exceeding ±0.05V indicate imbalance. (3) Increased internal resistance from electrolyte decomposition — causes greater voltage sag under load, reducing usable energy without obvious SOH change. Identify by comparing cell internal resistance measurements against baseline values in the manufacturer specification table.\n\n**Q35.** State the specific regulatory obligations for disposing of BEV HV glycol coolant and 12V lead-acid batteries, including relevant legislation. [CoCU C01.1.5]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* HV glycol coolant — classified as Scheduled Waste SW305 under the Environmental Quality (Scheduled Wastes) Regulations 2005 made under the Environmental Quality Act 1974 (Act 127). Must be stored in sealed, labelled containers; cannot be disposed via drain, soil, or municipal waste. Must be consigned to a DOE-licensed Scheduled Waste contractor using the prescribed waste manifest form. Workshop must retain manifest records for minimum 3 years. 12V lead-acid batteries — classified as Scheduled Waste SW102 (used lead-acid batteries) under the same Regulations. Must be returned to licensed lead recyclers or manufacturer take-back programmes. Never crush, incinerate, or dispose in landfill. Penalty for improper disposal: up to RM500,000 fine and/or 5 years imprisonment under EQA 1974 Section 34B.\n\n---\n\n### Skema Pemarkahan Q-Format / Q-Format Marking Scheme (25 Markah / Marks)\n\n**Bahagian A / Section A (Q1–Q20 — 1 markah setiap soalan / 1 mark each):**\n\n| Q | Jwp | Q | Jwp | Q | Jwp | Q | Jwp |\n|---|-----|---|-----|---|-----|---|-----|\n| Q1 | B | Q6 | B | Q11 | D | Q16 | B |\n| Q2 | C | Q7 | C | Q12 | B | Q17 | C |\n| Q3 | C | Q8 | B | Q13 | C | Q18 | C |\n| Q4 | C | Q9 | C | Q14 | B | Q19 | C |\n| Q5 | B | Q10 | C | Q15 | B | Q20 | C |\n\n**Bahagian B / Section B (Q21–Q25 — 1 markah setiap soalan / 1 mark each):**\nAward 1 mark for responses that correctly identify the key safety action(s) and reference relevant CoCU assessment criteria. See sample answers above.\n\n**Bahagian C / Section C (Q26–Q35 — Pengayaan / Enrichment — 0 markah / 0 marks):**\nSample answers to be provided by trainer for guided discussion.\n\n**Jumlah Q-Format / Q-Format Total: 25 markah / marks**\n*Lulus / Pass: 60% = 15 markah/marks. Rujukan / References: CoCU C01 — semua kriteria penilaian / all assessment criteria.*\n","numLines":320,"startLine":1,"totalLines":320}}}
