{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ViFECM9tb6ceUMkZNRZs4Q","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# NOSS Extract: G452-010-3:2023 Battery Electric Vehicle (BEV) Diagnostic and Rectification\n2\t\n3\t> **Source:** G452-010-3:2023 Battery Electric Vehicle (BEV) Diagnostic and Rectification (138 pages)\n4\t> **Level:** 3\n5\t> **Extracted:** 2026-04-16\n6\t> **Purpose:** Source-of-truth for WIM document generation\n7\t\n8\t---\n9\t\n10\t## 1. NOSS Identification\n11\t\n12\t| Field | Value |\n13\t|-------|-------|\n14\t| NOSS Code | G452-010-3:2023 |\n15\t| NOSS Title | Battery Electric Vehicle (BEV) Diagnostic and Rectification |\n16\t| NOSS Title (BM) | Diagnostik dan Pembaikan Kenderaan Elektrik Bateri (BEV) |\n17\t| Level | Three (3) |\n18\t| Section | (G) Wholesale and Retail Trade; Repair of Motor Vehicles and Motorcycles |\n19\t| Group | (452) Maintenance and Repair of Motor Vehicle |\n20\t| Area | Battery Electric Vehicle (BEV) Maintenance |\n21\t\n22\t## 2. Occupational Prerequisites\n23\t\n24\t- Minimum age 18 years old (Employment Act 1955 - Act 265)\n25\t- Medically fit (medical check-up by a licensed Medical Officer or Occupational Health Doctor)\n26\t- Pass colour blind test (verified by licensed Medical Officer)\n27\t- Certified BEV Technician (SKM Level 3) or equivalent\n28\t\n29\t## 3. Training Prerequisites (General)\n30\t\n31\tOne of the following certificates:\n32\t\n33\t1. G452-002-2:2018 Light Vehicle - Repair Service\n34\t2. G452-001-2:2017 Commercial Vehicles - Repair Service\n35\t3. TP-300-2:2013 Automotive After Sales Servicing\n36\t4. P-115-2 Mekanik Kenderaan Motor\n37\t5. P-119-2 Mekanik Kenderaan Motor\n38\t\n39\tPlus: Able to read and write (Bahasa Malaysia and/or English language)\n40\t\n41\t## 4. Regulatory/Statutory Requirements\n42\t\n43\t| Authority | Legislation |\n44\t|-----------|-------------|\n45\t| Department of Safety and Health | Occupational Safety and Health Act 1994 (Act 514) |\n46\t| Department of Environment | Environmental Quality Act 1974 (Act 127) |\n47\t| Department of Road Transport | Road Transport Act 1987 (Act 333) |\n48\t| Energy Commission (EC) | Energy Commission Act 2001; EC (Amendment) Act 2010; Electricity Supply Act 1990 (Act 447); Electricity Supply (Amendment) Act 2015 (Act A1501) |\n49\t\n50\t---\n51\t\n52\t## 5. Competency Profile Chart (CPC)\n53\t\n54\t### Summary of All Competency Units\n55\t\n56\t| CU Code | CU Title | Type | No. of WA |\n57\t|---------|----------|------|-----------|\n58\t| G452-010-3:2023-C01 | Perform BEV Scheduled Maintenance | Core | 3 |\n59\t| G452-010-3:2023-C02 | Perform Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification | Core | 6 |\n60\t| G452-010-3:2023-C03 | Perform BEV Thermal Cooling System Rectification | Core | 3 |\n61\t| G452-010-3:2023-C04 | Perform BEV Electric Drive Train System Rectification | Core | 5 |\n62\t| G452-010-3:2023-C05 | Perform BEV Workshop Supervisory Function | Core | 4 |\n63\t\n64\t### CPC Detail - Work Activities per CU\n65\t\n66\t#### CU C01: Perform BEV Scheduled Maintenance\n67\t\n68\t| WA Code | Work Activity |\n69\t|---------|---------------|\n70\t| G452-010-3:2023-C01-W01 | Carry Out BEV Schedule Maintenance Workplace Preparation |\n71\t| G452-010-3:2023-C01-W02 | Carry Out BEV Health Check Activities |\n72\t| G452-010-3:2023-C01-W03 | Carry Out BEV Maintenance Activities |\n73\t\n74\t#### CU C02: Perform Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification\n75\t\n76\t| WA Code | Work Activity |\n77\t|---------|---------------|\n78\t| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W01 | Carry Out Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification Workplace Preparation |\n79\t| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W02 | Carry Out BEV High Voltage Cable Replacement |\n80\t| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W03 | Carry Out BEV On-Board Charger (OBC) Replacement |\n81\t| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W04 | Carry Out BEV Pre-Charge Control System Servicing |\n82\t| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W05 | Carry Out BEV Traction Battery Pack Replacement |\n83\t| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W06 | Carry Out BEV DC-DC Converter Replacement |\n84\t\n85\t#### CU C03: Perform BEV Thermal Cooling System Rectification\n86\t\n87\t| WA Code | Work Activity |\n88\t|---------|---------------|\n89\t| G452-010-3:2023-C03-W01 | Carry Out Thermal Cooling System Rectification Workplace Preparation |\n90\t| G452-010-3:2023-C03-W02 | Carry Out BEV Liquid Cooling System Servicing |\n91\t| G452-010-3:2023-C03-W03 | Carry Out BEV Heating, Ventilation and Air Conditioning (HVAC) System Servicing |\n92\t\n93\t#### CU C04: Perform BEV Electric Drive Train System Rectification\n94\t\n95\t| WA Code | Work Activity |\n96\t|---------|---------------|\n97\t| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W01 | Carry Out Electric Drive Train System Rectification Workplace Preparation |\n98\t| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W02 | Carry Out BEV Inverter Unit Replacement |\n99\t| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W03 | Carry Out BEV Traction Motor Replacement |\n100\t| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W04 | Carry Out BEV Motorised Transaxle Servicing |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"794df4fc-0917-40f0-aba2-b2d02fc662d7","uuid":"6fe535a2-7acb-4a70-a8c0-18c16f60864b","timestamp":"2026-04-16T15:04:06.988Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\00-noss-extract.md","content":"# NOSS Extract: G452-010-3:2023 Battery Electric Vehicle (BEV) Diagnostic and Rectification\n\n> **Source:** G452-010-3:2023 Battery Electric Vehicle (BEV) Diagnostic and Rectification (138 pages)\n> **Level:** 3\n> **Extracted:** 2026-04-16\n> **Purpose:** Source-of-truth for WIM document generation\n\n---\n\n## 1. NOSS Identification\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| NOSS Code | G452-010-3:2023 |\n| NOSS Title | Battery Electric Vehicle (BEV) Diagnostic and Rectification |\n| NOSS Title (BM) | Diagnostik dan Pembaikan Kenderaan Elektrik Bateri (BEV) |\n| Level | Three (3) |\n| Section | (G) Wholesale and Retail Trade; Repair of Motor Vehicles and Motorcycles |\n| Group | (452) Maintenance and Repair of Motor Vehicle |\n| Area | Battery Electric Vehicle (BEV) Maintenance |\n\n## 2. Occupational Prerequisites\n\n- Minimum age 18 years old (Employment Act 1955 - Act 265)\n- Medically fit (medical check-up by a licensed Medical Officer or Occupational Health Doctor)\n- Pass colour blind test (verified by licensed Medical Officer)\n- Certified BEV Technician (SKM Level 3) or equivalent\n\n## 3. Training Prerequisites (General)\n\nOne of the following certificates:\n\n1. G452-002-2:2018 Light Vehicle - Repair Service\n2. G452-001-2:2017 Commercial Vehicles - Repair Service\n3. TP-300-2:2013 Automotive After Sales Servicing\n4. P-115-2 Mekanik Kenderaan Motor\n5. P-119-2 Mekanik Kenderaan Motor\n\nPlus: Able to read and write (Bahasa Malaysia and/or English language)\n\n## 4. Regulatory/Statutory Requirements\n\n| Authority | Legislation |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| Department of Safety and Health | Occupational Safety and Health Act 1994 (Act 514) |\n| Department of Environment | Environmental Quality Act 1974 (Act 127) |\n| Department of Road Transport | Road Transport Act 1987 (Act 333) |\n| Energy Commission (EC) | Energy Commission Act 2001; EC (Amendment) Act 2010; Electricity Supply Act 1990 (Act 447); Electricity Supply (Amendment) Act 2015 (Act A1501) |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Competency Profile Chart (CPC)\n\n### Summary of All Competency Units\n\n| CU Code | CU Title | Type | No. of WA |\n|---------|----------|------|-----------|\n| G452-010-3:2023-C01 | Perform BEV Scheduled Maintenance | Core | 3 |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C02 | Perform Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification | Core | 6 |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C03 | Perform BEV Thermal Cooling System Rectification | Core | 3 |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C04 | Perform BEV Electric Drive Train System Rectification | Core | 5 |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C05 | Perform BEV Workshop Supervisory Function | Core | 4 |\n\n### CPC Detail - Work Activities per CU\n\n#### CU C01: Perform BEV Scheduled Maintenance\n\n| WA Code | Work Activity |\n|---------|---------------|\n| G452-010-3:2023-C01-W01 | Carry Out BEV Schedule Maintenance Workplace Preparation |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C01-W02 | Carry Out BEV Health Check Activities |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C01-W03 | Carry Out BEV Maintenance Activities |\n\n#### CU C02: Perform Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification\n\n| WA Code | Work Activity |\n|---------|---------------|\n| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W01 | Carry Out Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification Workplace Preparation |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W02 | Carry Out BEV High Voltage Cable Replacement |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W03 | Carry Out BEV On-Board Charger (OBC) Replacement |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W04 | Carry Out BEV Pre-Charge Control System Servicing |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W05 | Carry Out BEV Traction Battery Pack Replacement |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W06 | Carry Out BEV DC-DC Converter Replacement |\n\n#### CU C03: Perform BEV Thermal Cooling System Rectification\n\n| WA Code | Work Activity |\n|---------|---------------|\n| G452-010-3:2023-C03-W01 | Carry Out Thermal Cooling System Rectification Workplace Preparation |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C03-W02 | Carry Out BEV Liquid Cooling System Servicing |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C03-W03 | Carry Out BEV Heating, Ventilation and Air Conditioning (HVAC) System Servicing |\n\n#### CU C04: Perform BEV Electric Drive Train System Rectification\n\n| WA Code | Work Activity |\n|---------|---------------|\n| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W01 | Carry Out Electric Drive Train System Rectification Workplace Preparation |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W02 | Carry Out BEV Inverter Unit Replacement |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W03 | Carry Out BEV Traction Motor Replacement |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W04 | Carry Out BEV Motorised Transaxle Servicing |","numLines":100,"startLine":1,"totalLines":962}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_015ZEMW9hRSE446gFg9k6fU2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019iBXHzHYKdzvYdKzFQSqPV","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\00-noss-extract.md","offset":100,"limit":150},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2565,"cache_read_input_tokens":57588,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2565},"output_tokens":65,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"794df4fc-0917-40f0-aba2-b2d02fc662d7","uuid":"8ffd8bbe-4bea-4df1-b4f9-5157902f6a37"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019iBXHzHYKdzvYdKzFQSqPV","type":"tool_result","content":"100\t| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W04 | Carry Out BEV Motorised Transaxle Servicing |\n101\t| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W05 | Carry Out BEV Integrated Brake Control System Replacement |\n102\t\n103\t#### CU C05: Perform BEV Workshop Supervisory Function\n104\t\n105\t| WA Code | Work Activity |\n106\t|---------|---------------|\n107\t| G452-010-3:2023-C05-W01 | Conduct BEV Daily Briefing |\n108\t| G452-010-3:2023-C05-W02 | Monitor BEV Subordinate's Performance |\n109\t| G452-010-3:2023-C05-W03 | Coordinate BEV Subordinate's Training |\n110\t| G452-010-3:2023-C05-W04 | Monitor BEV Workshop Safety, Health & Environment |\n111\t\n112\t---\n113\t\n114\t## 6. Competency Profile (CP)\n115\t\n116\t### 6.1 CU C01: Perform BEV Scheduled Maintenance (G452-010-3:2023-C01)\n117\t\n118\t**CU Descriptor:** Perform BEV Scheduled Maintenance preliminary is the first and necessary steps to set-up a working place. This is to determine the working environment for servicing and maintenance of BEV are equipped, safe and suitable. Check the function and condition of safety equipment and component of BEV. The person who is competent in this CU should be able to carry out BEV scheduled maintenance workplace preparation, carry out BEV health check activities and carry out BEV maintenance activities. The outcome of this CU is the workplace preparation activities can be conducted in a safe manner and with an appropriate procedure in accordance with company safety and health policy.\n119\t\n120\t#### WA 1: Carry out BEV schedule maintenance workplace preparation\n121\t\n122\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n123\t|-----------|---------------------|\n124\t| 1.1 Identify licensing legislative, regulatory or certification requirement | 1.1 Licensing, legislative, regulatory or certification requirement determined in accordance with regulatory authority |\n125\t| 1.2 Identify BEV schedule maintenance safety & health requirement | 1.2 BEV workplace safety & health applied in accordance with manufacturer and company safety & health guideline |\n126\t| 1.3 Identify schedule maintenance tools and equipment | 1.3 Tools and equipment required for scheduled maintenance prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n127\t| 1.4 Identify hazard in relation to working within a workshop environment | 1.4 Hazard in relation to working in workshop environment determined in accordance with company Job Hazard Analysis (JHA) checklist |\n128\t| 1.5 Identify scheduled waste management procedure | 1.5 Waste management procedure applied in accordance with environmental regulation guideline |\n129\t| 1.6 Arrange BEV scheduled maintenance workplace preparation | 1.6 BEV scheduled maintenance workplace preparation prepared in accordance with company standard operating procedure |\n130\t\n131\t#### WA 2: Carry out BEV health check activities\n132\t\n133\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n134\t|-----------|---------------------|\n135\t| 2.1 Identify BEV health check safety requirement | 2.1 BEV health check safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n136\t| 2.2 Identify BEV health check tools and equipment | 2.2 BEV health check tools and equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n137\t| 2.3 Execute BEV components active test | 2.3 BEV components active test checked in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n138\t| 2.4 Identify diagnostic fault code | 2.4 Fault code recorded in accordance with diagnostic result |\n139\t| 2.5 Prepare BEV health check report | 2.5 BEV health check report submitted and filed in accordance with company standard operating procedure |\n140\t\n141\t#### WA 3: Carry out BEV maintenance activities\n142\t\n143\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n144\t|-----------|---------------------|\n145\t| 3.1 Identify BEV maintenance safety requirement | 3.1 BEV maintenance activities safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n146\t| 3.2 Identify type of BEV and specification | 3.2 BEV manufacture, model, and specification determined in accordance with vehicle registration form |\n147\t| 3.3 Interpret BEV scheduled maintenance checklist | 3.3 BEV scheduled maintenance checklist confirmed in accordance with company standard operating procedure (SOP) |\n148\t| 3.4 Prepare BEV maintenance diagnostic tools and equipment | 3.4 BEV maintenance diagnostic tools and equipment setup in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n149\t| 3.5 Inspect BEV maintenance item | 3.5 BEV maintenance item rectified in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n150\t\n151\t### 6.2 CU C02: Perform Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification (G452-010-3:2023-C02)\n152\t\n153\t**CU Descriptor:** Perform Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification is the activities on how to rectify battery management system fault, which is diagnosis, interpret, inspect, and replace the battery pack in accordance with manufacturer's service manual. The person who is competent in this CU should be able to carry out BMS rectification workplace preparation, carry out BEV high voltage cable replacement, carry out BEV On-Board Charger (OBC) replacement, carry out BEV pre-charge control system servicing, carry out BEV traction battery pack replacement and carry out BEV DC-DC converter replacement. The outcome of this CU is able to define the BEV battery component and demonstrate suitable approached for BMS replacement in accordance with manufacturer's service manual.\n154\t\n155\t#### WA 1: Carry out Battery Management System (BMS) rectification workplace preparation\n156\t\n157\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n158\t|-----------|---------------------|\n159\t| 1.1 Identify licensing legislative, regulatory or certification requirement | 1.1 Licensing, legislative, regulatory or certification requirement determined in accordance with regulatory authority |\n160\t| 1.2 Identify BMS rectification safety and health requirements | 1.2 BMS rectification workplace preparation safety & health requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer and company safety & health guideline |\n161\t| 1.3 Identify BMS rectification high voltage tools and equipment requirement | 1.3 BMS rectification high voltage isolated tools & equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n162\t| 1.4 Identify BMS rectification hazard in relation to working within a workshop environment | 1.4 BMS rectification hazard in relation to working within a workshop environment identified in accordance with company Job Hazard Analysis Checklist |\n163\t| 1.5 Identify BMS rectification schedule waste management procedure | 1.5 BMS rectification scheduled waste management procedure applied in accordance with company safety, health and environment guideline |\n164\t| 1.6 Arrange BMS rectification workplace preparation | 1.6 BMS workplace rectification preparation prepared in accordance with company standard operating procedure |\n165\t\n166\t#### WA 2: Carry out BEV high voltage cable replacement\n167\t\n168\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n169\t|-----------|---------------------|\n170\t| 2.1 Identify BEV high voltage cable replacement safety requirements | 2.1 BEV high voltage cable replacement safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer and company safety manual |\n171\t| 2.2 Identify BEV high voltage cable replacement tools and equipment | 2.2 BEV high voltage cable replacement tools and lifting equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n172\t| 2.3 Disconnect BEV low voltage circuit | 2.3 BEV low voltage circuit disconnected in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n173\t| 2.4 Disconnect BEV high voltage circuit | 2.4 BEV high voltage circuit disconnected in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n174\t| 2.5 Inspect high voltage cable condition | 2.5 High voltage cable condition checked in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n175\t| 2.6 Inspect high voltage cable interlock connection | 2.6 High voltage cable interlock condition checked in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n176\t| 2.7 Change high voltage cable | 2.7 High voltage cable resistant and continuity replaced in accordance with diagnostic result |\n177\t| 2.8 Test high voltage system functionality | 2.8 High voltage system functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n178\t\n179\t#### WA 3: Carry out BEV On-Board Charger (OBC) replacement\n180\t\n181\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n182\t|-----------|---------------------|\n183\t| 3.1 Identify BEV OBC safety requirements | 3.1 OBC replacement safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n184\t| 3.2 Identify BEV OBC high voltage cable replacement tools and equipment | 3.2 BEV OBC high voltage cable replacement tools and equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n185\t| 3.3 Inspect BEV OBC functionality | 3.3 BEV OBC functionality checked using diagnostic tools in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n186\t| 3.4 Disconnect BEV low voltage circuit | 3.4 BEV low voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n187\t| 3.5 Disconnect BEV high voltage circuit | 3.5 BEV high voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n188\t| 3.6 Drain out coolant from the high voltage system | 3.6 Coolant from the high voltage system emptied in accordance with manufacturer's service manual and waste management procedure |\n189\t| 3.7 Disconnect OBC high voltage cable | 3.7 High voltage cable from the OBC detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n190\t| 3.8 Diagnose BEV on-board charger functionality | 3.8 BEV OBC fault code identified in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n191\t| 3.9 Change BEV OBC | 3.9 New OBC installed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n192\t| 3.10 Refill high voltage system coolant | 3.10 High voltage system coolant refill confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n193\t| 3.11 Test BEV OBC functionality | 3.11 BEV OBC functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n194\t\n195\t#### WA 4: Carry out BEV pre-charge control system servicing\n196\t\n197\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n198\t|-----------|---------------------|\n199\t| 4.1 Identify BEV pre-charge relay pack safety requirements | 4.1 BEV pre-charge relay pack servicing safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n200\t| 4.2 Identify BEV high voltage cable replacement tools and equipment | 4.2 BEV high voltage cable replacement tools and equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n201\t| 4.3 Inspect BEV pre-charge control circuits | 4.3 BEV pre-charge control circuit functionality checked in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n202\t| 4.4 Disconnect BEV low voltage circuit | 4.4 BEV low voltage circuit disconnected in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n203\t| 4.5 Disconnect BEV high voltage circuit | 4.5 BEV high voltage circuit disconnected in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n204\t| 4.6 Diagnose pre-charge control circuits components | 4.6 Pre-charge control circuits component's fault code identified in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n205\t| 4.7 Change defective pre-charge relay pack | 4.7 Defective pre-charge relay pack replaced in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n206\t| 4.8 Test BEV pre-charge relay pack functionality | 4.8 BEV pre-charge relay pack functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n207\t\n208\t#### WA 5: Carry out BEV traction battery pack replacement\n209\t\n210\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n211\t|-----------|---------------------|\n212\t| 5.1 Identify BEV traction battery pack replacement safety requirements | 5.1 BEV traction battery pack replacement safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n213\t| 5.2 Identify traction battery pack diagnosis & replacement tools and lifting equipment | 5.2 Tools and equipment for traction battery pack diagnosis & replacement prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n214\t| 5.3 Inspect traction battery pack functionality | 5.3 Traction battery packed functionality checked using diagnostic tools in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n215\t| 5.4 Disconnect BEV low voltage circuit | 5.4 BEV low voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n216\t| 5.5 Disconnect BEV high voltage circuit | 5.5 BEV high voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n217\t| 5.6 Drain traction battery pack coolant or refrigerant | 5.6 Traction battery pack coolant or refrigerant emptied in accordance with manufacturer's service manual and waste management system |\n218\t| 5.7 Remove traction battery pack | 5.7 Traction battery pack detached from the vehicle in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n219\t| 5.8 Inspect battery module system | 5.8 Battery module system checked in accordance with manufacturer's specification |\n220\t| 5.9 Change faulty battery module unit | 5.9 Faulty battery module unit replaced in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n221\t| 5.10 Refill BEV traction battery coolant or refrigerant | 5.10 BEV traction battery coolant or refrigerant refill confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n222\t| 5.11 Test traction battery pack | 5.11 Traction battery pack condition and functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n223\t\n224\t#### WA 6: Carry out BEV DC-DC converter replacement\n225\t\n226\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n227\t|-----------|---------------------|\n228\t| 6.1 Identify BEV DC-DC converter replacement safety requirement | 6.1 DC-DC converter replacement safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n229\t| 6.2 Identify DC-DC converter diagnosis & replacement tools and equipment | 6.2 Tools and equipment for DC-DC converter diagnosis & replacement prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n230\t| 6.3 Inspect DC-DC converter's functionality | 6.3 DC-DC converter's functionality checked using diagnostic tools in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n231\t| 6.4 Diagnose BEV DC-DC converter | 6.4 BEV DC-DC converter fault code identified in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n232\t| 6.5 Drain DC-DC converter coolant | 6.5 DC-DC converter coolant emptied in accordance with manufacturer's service manual and waste management procedure |\n233\t| 6.6 Disconnect BEV low voltage circuit | 6.6 BEV low voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n234\t| 6.7 Disconnect BEV high voltage circuit | 6.7 BEV high voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n235\t| 6.8 Change faulty DC-DC converter | 6.8 Faulty DC-DC converter replaced in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n236\t| 6.9 Refill DC-DC converter coolant | 6.9 BEV DC-DC converter coolant refill confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n237\t| 6.10 Test DC-DC converter functionality | 6.10 DC-DC converter functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n238\t\n239\t### 6.3 CU C03: Perform BEV Thermal Cooling System Rectification (G452-010-3:2023-C03)\n240\t\n241\t**CU Descriptor:** Perform BEV Thermal Cooling System Rectification is the activities to prepare working place, the method how to examine, rectified thermal cooling system, service liquid and air conditioning cooling system according to manufacturer's service manual. The person who is competent in this CU should be able to carry out thermal cooling system rectification workplace preparation, carry out BEV liquid cooling system servicing and carry out BEV HVAC system servicing. The outcome of this CU is the functionality of vehicle thermal cooling system can be restored and prevention of electric traction motor and batteries from over-heating in accordance with manufacturer's service manual.\n242\t\n243\t#### WA 1: Carry out thermal cooling system rectification workplace preparation\n244\t\n245\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n246\t|-----------|---------------------|\n247\t| 1.1 Identify licensing, legislative, regulatory or certificate requirement | 1.1 Licensing, legislative, regulatory or certification requirement determined in accordance with regulatory authority |\n248\t| 1.2 Identify BEV thermal cooling system rectification safety & health requirements | 1.2 BEV thermal cooling system rectification safety & health requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer and company safety & health guideline |\n249\t| 1.3 Identify thermal cooling system diagnosis & replacement tools and equipment | 1.3 Thermal cooling system rectification high voltage isolated tools & equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"794df4fc-0917-40f0-aba2-b2d02fc662d7","uuid":"a3939f02-0162-491c-953c-6f2acee0f839","timestamp":"2026-04-16T15:04:09.705Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\00-noss-extract.md","content":"| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W04 | Carry Out BEV Motorised Transaxle Servicing |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W05 | Carry Out BEV Integrated Brake Control System Replacement |\n\n#### CU C05: Perform BEV Workshop Supervisory Function\n\n| WA Code | Work Activity |\n|---------|---------------|\n| G452-010-3:2023-C05-W01 | Conduct BEV Daily Briefing |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C05-W02 | Monitor BEV Subordinate's Performance |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C05-W03 | Coordinate BEV Subordinate's Training |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C05-W04 | Monitor BEV Workshop Safety, Health & Environment |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Competency Profile (CP)\n\n### 6.1 CU C01: Perform BEV Scheduled Maintenance (G452-010-3:2023-C01)\n\n**CU Descriptor:** Perform BEV Scheduled Maintenance preliminary is the first and necessary steps to set-up a working place. This is to determine the working environment for servicing and maintenance of BEV are equipped, safe and suitable. Check the function and condition of safety equipment and component of BEV. The person who is competent in this CU should be able to carry out BEV scheduled maintenance workplace preparation, carry out BEV health check activities and carry out BEV maintenance activities. The outcome of this CU is the workplace preparation activities can be conducted in a safe manner and with an appropriate procedure in accordance with company safety and health policy.\n\n#### WA 1: Carry out BEV schedule maintenance workplace preparation\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 1.1 Identify licensing legislative, regulatory or certification requirement | 1.1 Licensing, legislative, regulatory or certification requirement determined in accordance with regulatory authority |\n| 1.2 Identify BEV schedule maintenance safety & health requirement | 1.2 BEV workplace safety & health applied in accordance with manufacturer and company safety & health guideline |\n| 1.3 Identify schedule maintenance tools and equipment | 1.3 Tools and equipment required for scheduled maintenance prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 1.4 Identify hazard in relation to working within a workshop environment | 1.4 Hazard in relation to working in workshop environment determined in accordance with company Job Hazard Analysis (JHA) checklist |\n| 1.5 Identify scheduled waste management procedure | 1.5 Waste management procedure applied in accordance with environmental regulation guideline |\n| 1.6 Arrange BEV scheduled maintenance workplace preparation | 1.6 BEV scheduled maintenance workplace preparation prepared in accordance with company standard operating procedure |\n\n#### WA 2: Carry out BEV health check activities\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 2.1 Identify BEV health check safety requirement | 2.1 BEV health check safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n| 2.2 Identify BEV health check tools and equipment | 2.2 BEV health check tools and equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 2.3 Execute BEV components active test | 2.3 BEV components active test checked in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 2.4 Identify diagnostic fault code | 2.4 Fault code recorded in accordance with diagnostic result |\n| 2.5 Prepare BEV health check report | 2.5 BEV health check report submitted and filed in accordance with company standard operating procedure |\n\n#### WA 3: Carry out BEV maintenance activities\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 3.1 Identify BEV maintenance safety requirement | 3.1 BEV maintenance activities safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n| 3.2 Identify type of BEV and specification | 3.2 BEV manufacture, model, and specification determined in accordance with vehicle registration form |\n| 3.3 Interpret BEV scheduled maintenance checklist | 3.3 BEV scheduled maintenance checklist confirmed in accordance with company standard operating procedure (SOP) |\n| 3.4 Prepare BEV maintenance diagnostic tools and equipment | 3.4 BEV maintenance diagnostic tools and equipment setup in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 3.5 Inspect BEV maintenance item | 3.5 BEV maintenance item rectified in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n\n### 6.2 CU C02: Perform Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification (G452-010-3:2023-C02)\n\n**CU Descriptor:** Perform Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification is the activities on how to rectify battery management system fault, which is diagnosis, interpret, inspect, and replace the battery pack in accordance with manufacturer's service manual. The person who is competent in this CU should be able to carry out BMS rectification workplace preparation, carry out BEV high voltage cable replacement, carry out BEV On-Board Charger (OBC) replacement, carry out BEV pre-charge control system servicing, carry out BEV traction battery pack replacement and carry out BEV DC-DC converter replacement. The outcome of this CU is able to define the BEV battery component and demonstrate suitable approached for BMS replacement in accordance with manufacturer's service manual.\n\n#### WA 1: Carry out Battery Management System (BMS) rectification workplace preparation\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 1.1 Identify licensing legislative, regulatory or certification requirement | 1.1 Licensing, legislative, regulatory or certification requirement determined in accordance with regulatory authority |\n| 1.2 Identify BMS rectification safety and health requirements | 1.2 BMS rectification workplace preparation safety & health requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer and company safety & health guideline |\n| 1.3 Identify BMS rectification high voltage tools and equipment requirement | 1.3 BMS rectification high voltage isolated tools & equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 1.4 Identify BMS rectification hazard in relation to working within a workshop environment | 1.4 BMS rectification hazard in relation to working within a workshop environment identified in accordance with company Job Hazard Analysis Checklist |\n| 1.5 Identify BMS rectification schedule waste management procedure | 1.5 BMS rectification scheduled waste management procedure applied in accordance with company safety, health and environment guideline |\n| 1.6 Arrange BMS rectification workplace preparation | 1.6 BMS workplace rectification preparation prepared in accordance with company standard operating procedure |\n\n#### WA 2: Carry out BEV high voltage cable replacement\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 2.1 Identify BEV high voltage cable replacement safety requirements | 2.1 BEV high voltage cable replacement safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer and company safety manual |\n| 2.2 Identify BEV high voltage cable replacement tools and equipment | 2.2 BEV high voltage cable replacement tools and lifting equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 2.3 Disconnect BEV low voltage circuit | 2.3 BEV low voltage circuit disconnected in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 2.4 Disconnect BEV high voltage circuit | 2.4 BEV high voltage circuit disconnected in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 2.5 Inspect high voltage cable condition | 2.5 High voltage cable condition checked in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 2.6 Inspect high voltage cable interlock connection | 2.6 High voltage cable interlock condition checked in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 2.7 Change high voltage cable | 2.7 High voltage cable resistant and continuity replaced in accordance with diagnostic result |\n| 2.8 Test high voltage system functionality | 2.8 High voltage system functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n\n#### WA 3: Carry out BEV On-Board Charger (OBC) replacement\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 3.1 Identify BEV OBC safety requirements | 3.1 OBC replacement safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n| 3.2 Identify BEV OBC high voltage cable replacement tools and equipment | 3.2 BEV OBC high voltage cable replacement tools and equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 3.3 Inspect BEV OBC functionality | 3.3 BEV OBC functionality checked using diagnostic tools in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 3.4 Disconnect BEV low voltage circuit | 3.4 BEV low voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 3.5 Disconnect BEV high voltage circuit | 3.5 BEV high voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 3.6 Drain out coolant from the high voltage system | 3.6 Coolant from the high voltage system emptied in accordance with manufacturer's service manual and waste management procedure |\n| 3.7 Disconnect OBC high voltage cable | 3.7 High voltage cable from the OBC detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 3.8 Diagnose BEV on-board charger functionality | 3.8 BEV OBC fault code identified in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 3.9 Change BEV OBC | 3.9 New OBC installed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 3.10 Refill high voltage system coolant | 3.10 High voltage system coolant refill confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 3.11 Test BEV OBC functionality | 3.11 BEV OBC functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n\n#### WA 4: Carry out BEV pre-charge control system servicing\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 4.1 Identify BEV pre-charge relay pack safety requirements | 4.1 BEV pre-charge relay pack servicing safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n| 4.2 Identify BEV high voltage cable replacement tools and equipment | 4.2 BEV high voltage cable replacement tools and equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 4.3 Inspect BEV pre-charge control circuits | 4.3 BEV pre-charge control circuit functionality checked in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 4.4 Disconnect BEV low voltage circuit | 4.4 BEV low voltage circuit disconnected in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 4.5 Disconnect BEV high voltage circuit | 4.5 BEV high voltage circuit disconnected in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 4.6 Diagnose pre-charge control circuits components | 4.6 Pre-charge control circuits component's fault code identified in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 4.7 Change defective pre-charge relay pack | 4.7 Defective pre-charge relay pack replaced in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 4.8 Test BEV pre-charge relay pack functionality | 4.8 BEV pre-charge relay pack functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n\n#### WA 5: Carry out BEV traction battery pack replacement\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 5.1 Identify BEV traction battery pack replacement safety requirements | 5.1 BEV traction battery pack replacement safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n| 5.2 Identify traction battery pack diagnosis & replacement tools and lifting equipment | 5.2 Tools and equipment for traction battery pack diagnosis & replacement prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 5.3 Inspect traction battery pack functionality | 5.3 Traction battery packed functionality checked using diagnostic tools in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 5.4 Disconnect BEV low voltage circuit | 5.4 BEV low voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 5.5 Disconnect BEV high voltage circuit | 5.5 BEV high voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 5.6 Drain traction battery pack coolant or refrigerant | 5.6 Traction battery pack coolant or refrigerant emptied in accordance with manufacturer's service manual and waste management system |\n| 5.7 Remove traction battery pack | 5.7 Traction battery pack detached from the vehicle in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 5.8 Inspect battery module system | 5.8 Battery module system checked in accordance with manufacturer's specification |\n| 5.9 Change faulty battery module unit | 5.9 Faulty battery module unit replaced in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 5.10 Refill BEV traction battery coolant or refrigerant | 5.10 BEV traction battery coolant or refrigerant refill confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 5.11 Test traction battery pack | 5.11 Traction battery pack condition and functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n\n#### WA 6: Carry out BEV DC-DC converter replacement\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 6.1 Identify BEV DC-DC converter replacement safety requirement | 6.1 DC-DC converter replacement safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n| 6.2 Identify DC-DC converter diagnosis & replacement tools and equipment | 6.2 Tools and equipment for DC-DC converter diagnosis & replacement prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 6.3 Inspect DC-DC converter's functionality | 6.3 DC-DC converter's functionality checked using diagnostic tools in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 6.4 Diagnose BEV DC-DC converter | 6.4 BEV DC-DC converter fault code identified in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 6.5 Drain DC-DC converter coolant | 6.5 DC-DC converter coolant emptied in accordance with manufacturer's service manual and waste management procedure |\n| 6.6 Disconnect BEV low voltage circuit | 6.6 BEV low voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 6.7 Disconnect BEV high voltage circuit | 6.7 BEV high voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 6.8 Change faulty DC-DC converter | 6.8 Faulty DC-DC converter replaced in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 6.9 Refill DC-DC converter coolant | 6.9 BEV DC-DC converter coolant refill confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 6.10 Test DC-DC converter functionality | 6.10 DC-DC converter functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n\n### 6.3 CU C03: Perform BEV Thermal Cooling System Rectification (G452-010-3:2023-C03)\n\n**CU Descriptor:** Perform BEV Thermal Cooling System Rectification is the activities to prepare working place, the method how to examine, rectified thermal cooling system, service liquid and air conditioning cooling system according to manufacturer's service manual. The person who is competent in this CU should be able to carry out thermal cooling system rectification workplace preparation, carry out BEV liquid cooling system servicing and carry out BEV HVAC system servicing. The outcome of this CU is the functionality of vehicle thermal cooling system can be restored and prevention of electric traction motor and batteries from over-heating in accordance with manufacturer's service manual.\n\n#### WA 1: Carry out thermal cooling system rectification workplace preparation\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 1.1 Identify licensing, legislative, regulatory or certificate requirement | 1.1 Licensing, legislative, regulatory or certification requirement determined in accordance with regulatory authority |\n| 1.2 Identify BEV thermal cooling system rectification safety & health requirements | 1.2 BEV thermal cooling system rectification safety & health requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer and company safety & health guideline |\n| 1.3 Identify thermal cooling system diagnosis & replacement tools and equipment | 1.3 Thermal cooling system rectification high voltage isolated tools & equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |","numLines":150,"startLine":100,"totalLines":962}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_019zB2vVJGxhfGkzF35qZtRE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the CoCU structure. For C02:\n- WA 1: BMS rectification workplace preparation → C02.1.x\n- WA 2: HV cable replacement → C02.2.x\n- WA 3: OBC replacement → C02.3.x\n- WA 4: Pre-charge control system → C02.4.x\n- WA 5: Traction battery pack replacement → C02.5.x\n- WA 6: DC-DC converter replacement → C02.6.x\n\n\n- WA 7: Additional work area → C02.7.x\n\nEach work area has its own set of CoCU codes ranging from .1 through varying upper limits depending on the complexity of the task. I'm organizing the content structure around three main knowledge areas: BMS theory covering battery architecture and controller functions, HV cable systems for safety and specifications, and OBC/charging systems including the DC-DC converter. Now I'm drafting the actual exam questions, starting with a 20-question multiple choice section focused on BMS fundamentals. I'm working through the second half of the passive cell balancing question—heat dissipation through resistors is the correct mechanism. Moving on to the pre-charge circuit, which prevents inrush current damage to the DC-link capacitors when contactors engage. Then I'm reviewing thermal runaway triggers in lithium-ion cells, where overcharge, mechanical damage, or external heat are the primary culprits. Now I'm transitioning into HV cable systems for the next section of questions. The interlock loop ensures contactors open if any connector is unseated, which aligns with inspecting the high voltage cable interlock connection. For battery removal, three technicians are required including a safety observer. HV cable insulation resistance between conductor and chassis must exceed 1 megohm. Now I'm working through the Lockout-Tagout procedures and when they're applied in the disconnection process. Now I'm working through questions about the DC-DC converter's role in replacing the alternator, its standard output voltage, proper disposal procedures for OBC coolant as scheduled waste, and how level 2 AC chargers connect through the AC inlet socket.\n\nFor the next question, the critical safety test after OBC replacement is the HV insulation resistance test, which must be performed before any other checks. The exam then moves into graded scenario questions covering BMS cell imbalance, thermal runaway detection, HV cable damage assessment, lockout-tagout procedures, and charging timeout faults, followed by enrichment sections on BMS theory, HV cable systems, and OBC operation.\n\nNow I'm calculating the content structure for the full C02 Q-format document. The multiple-choice section will take roughly 60 lines, the five graded scenarios with sample answers need about 70 lines, and the enrichment questions with answers will span around 150 lines. Adding the marking scheme brings the total to approximately 310 lines, which fits within the limit. I should keep sample answers concise at minimum 50 words each to stay efficient. I also need to verify whether C01 KA.md requires any updates to maintain consistency across both documents.\n\nLooking back at C01, it appears complete with all 35 questions, the answer key, and proper distribution across the three topic areas. The scenario questions make up the right proportion, and the sample answers all exceed the 50-word minimum. However, I'm noticing the assessment criteria references in C01 use just the code format like `[CoCU C01.1.2]` without the descriptive text that the acceptance criteria seem to expect, such as `CoCU C01.3.2 - HV Safety Procedures`. I should check whether this format needs updating to match the expected standard.\n\nThe story shows it failed on the last attempt earlier today, and the C01 file was modified in git, suggesting changes were made but didn't resolve the issue. I'm also realizing there's a mismatch in the grep command being used to validate — it's searching for `CU-0[12]` with a hyphen, but the actual directory structure uses `C01` and `C02` without that prefix, so that validation check would fail to find anything even if the content is correct.\n\nThe real acceptance criteria are what matter: C01 needs 35+ questions with CoCU references and a 25-mark answer key (already done), C02 needs 40+ questions with CoCU references and a 25-mark answer key (still needed), and both files need 30%+ scenario questions with 50+ word sample answers (C01 is done, C02 still needs work).\n\nI'm going to focus only on updating C02/KA.md to add the Q-format section with the required questions and answer key, keeping the diff around 310 lines to stay within budget.\n\nWriting out the C02 content now...\n\nFor the CoCU references, I'm mapping the actual WA codes to the competency structure: C02.1 covers BMS rectification workplace preparation, C02.2 is HV cable replacement, C02.3 is OBC replacement, C02.4 is pre-charge control systems, C02.5 is traction battery pack replacement, and C02.6 is DC-DC converter replacement. I'll use the WA step numbers as sub-references, so something like C02.2.1 for identifying HV cable safety requirements, C02.2.4 for disconnecting HV circuits, C02.2.5 for inspecting cable condition, and so on through the battery and converter work.\n\nGenerating the question set... I'm mapping out the content distribution across the three domains: BMS theory covering battery architecture and management systems, HV cable systems with safety components, and OBC charging circuits. Now I'm structuring the 40 questions with multiple-choice items in the first section and graded scenario questions following, making sure the proportions align with the acceptance criteria. Now I'm drafting the full question bank with the proper formatting and distribution I verified earlier. I'll keep each question concise at 2-3 lines and limit sample answers to 50-80 words, with MCQ answers fitting on a single line. Continuing with the remaining questions on battery management systems and electrical safety—Q3 asks what the BMS doesn't directly control, Q4 covers the CMU's reporting hierarchy, Q5 addresses passive balancing mechanisms, and Q6 focuses on the pre-charge circuit's role in protecting against inrush current when contactors engage.\n\nNow moving through thermal safety and identification standards: Q7 covers lithium-ion thermal runaway triggers like overcharging and mechanical damage, Q8 identifies HV cables by their distinctive orange insulation or conduit, and Q9 explains how the manual service plug physically isolates the battery pack by splitting it into two disconnected halves.\n\nContinuing with the interlock and maintenance procedures: Q10 addresses the HV interlock loop that opens contactors when connectors are unseated or the service plug is removed, Q11 specifies that battery pack removal requires three personnel including a safety observer, and Q12 establishes the insulation resistance threshold at 1 megohm between conductor and chassis.\n\nNow moving through the remaining questions on lockout-tagout procedures, pre-charge resistor specifications, and the on-board charger's conversion function from DC to AC power. Looking at questions about OBC coolant disposal classification, Level 2 charger connections, and the critical safety test sequence after OBC replacement—these are covering waste handling procedures, charging infrastructure standards, and post-service verification protocols.\n\nNow moving into the scenario-based section where I'm analyzing a BMS diagnostic case involving a significant cell voltage deviation in Module 4, specifically Cell #11 reading 0.27V below the acceptable tolerance, which points to internal degradation and requires module replacement.\n\nFor the second scenario, I'm examining a pre-charge circuit timeout fault where the DC-link capacitors failed to reach the required voltage threshold within the 2-second window, indicating either a capacitor failure or a high-resistance connection in the pre-charge circuit path.\n\nNow I'm moving through the diagnostic steps: measuring the pre-charge resistor against spec using a CAT III multimeter, checking the relay coil and contact resistance, and inspecting the entire pre-charge loop wiring and connectors for corrosion or damage. If the relay is defective, I'll create a work order for pre-charge relay pack replacement.\n\nFor the third scenario, I'm evaluating an orange HV cable with outer conduit abrasion damage where the insulation is scuffed but no copper conductor is exposed yet. Even though the conductor isn't visible, the compromised outer conduit still represents a safety risk and requires immediate action. The service plug is the only physical isolation point — relying solely on 12V disconnect leaves the HV contactors energized and vulnerable to unexpected closure if the battery reconnects or a control fault occurs. I need to stop all HV work immediately, notify the supervisor, document the missing plug, and obtain a replacement before proceeding.\n\nNow I'm moving to the next diagnostic scenario involving a Level 2 charger that stopped after 20 minutes with an amber warning and two fault codes pointing to battery voltage sensing and CAN bus communication issues.\n\nI'll record both codes with their freeze-frame data first, then analyze what's happening — the combination of P0AA9 and U0100 suggests either the OBC lost CAN communication entirely or a supply voltage problem knocked both ECUs out of sync. I need to inspect the OBC CAN connector for corrosion and pin damage, verify the 12V supply and ground at the OBC, check the AC inlet socket for burn marks or deformation from overheating, and if the OBC tests faulty through substitution, I'll create a replacement work order following the procedure.\n\nNow I'm looking at the enrichment practice questions on battery management — SOC tells you the current charge level as a percentage of what the pack can actually deliver right now, while SOH measures how much capacity the pack has lost over time compared to when it was new. Both matter because a fully charged degraded pack won't give you the same range as a fully charged healthy one, and the BMS adjusts its charge and discharge limits based on SOH to keep stressed cells from pushing toward thermal runaway. For cell balancing, passive methods bleed excess charge as heat while active methods transfer energy between cells, and most production BEVs use passive because it's simpler and cheaper even though active is more efficient. When a cell enters thermal runaway, it releases intense heat and toxic gases like hydrogen fluoride and carbon monoxide, causing adjacent cells to cascade into their own thermal runaway within seconds. The sealed battery housing prevents standard fire suppression from reaching internal cells, so extinguishing a BEV battery fire requires large volumes of water for immersion cooling rather than CO₂ or dry powder — JBPM guidelines mandate continuous flooding until the runaway completely stops. The BMS contactor driver circuit protects the battery during collisions by receiving crash signals from the airbag module via CAN bus and immediately opening both main contactors to isolate the pack within milliseconds. affected cells show greater voltage sag under load due to the IR drop, while at low discharge rates the current is small enough that internal resistance effects become negligible. The underlying cause is usually advanced electrolyte decomposition or lithium plating from repeated fast charging cycles. I'd confirm this by measuring individual cell internal resistance with the BMS diagnostic tool—any cells significantly above the manufacturer's baseline specification would need module replacement.\n\nFor the LOTO procedure, the six steps involve notifying personnel, turning off READY and securing the key, disconnecting the 12V auxiliary battery, removing and securing the manual service plug, applying the LOTO padlock and warning tag to the service plug port and battery disconnect points, then verifying zero energy at minimum three HV measurement points using a CAT III multimeter. After LOTO is applied, I need to complete the LOTO record form documenting the technician name, work order number, date, time, measurement readings, and supervisor sign-off before any HV work begins.\n\nFor HV cable routing, I'm considering the manufacturer's minimum bend radius requirements—typically three times the cable outer diameter—to avoid insulation damage, along with securing cables in the specified clamps at required intervals. HV cables must be kept separate from LV harnesses because shared conduit creates electromagnetic interference risks and complicates troubleshooting if faults occur.\n\nNow I'm looking at the cable replacement procedure, which starts with full de-energization, LOTO application, and zero-energy verification at three points, followed by photographing the damage and measuring the affected section.\n\nFor replacement, I need to disconnect both ends using insulated tools while documenting the original routing path, segregation requirements, and clamp positions, then install the new cable according to manufacturer specifications and reconnect all interlock connectors. Post-work involves removing LOTO, reconnecting the service plug and 12V battery in reverse order, cycling to READY, checking for HV fault codes, running an insulation resistance test with an insulation tester, verifying interlock continuity, and performing a functional test to confirm everything is working properly.\n\nNow looking at the welded contactor scenario — the key diagnostic sign is that the HV bus voltage persists even after the full de-energisation sequence, and the BMS shows the contactor driver commanded open but the voltage isn't dropping. When I measure the HV bus after de-energisation and voltage is still there, it indicates the contactor contacts have welded shut from inrush current arc damage. This creates a critical safety hazard because the welded contactor leaves the HV bus permanently live with no way to isolate it through normal procedures, which defeats the primary safety isolation mechanism. Any vehicle with a welded contactor must be treated as continuously energised and requires specialist insulation tools and advanced disassembly techniques before any repair work can safely begin.\n\nFor the cable replacement scenario without post-replacement insulation testing, an improperly installed cable with degraded insulation could create a leakage path to chassis that goes undetected, potentially leading to electric shock hazards, unintended HV system shutdown, or fire risk during operation. The PFC circuit actively shapes the input current to match the AC voltage waveform, achieving near-unity power factor so that apparent power aligns with real power delivered to the battery. This minimizes reactive current and harmonic distortion on the grid while maximizing charging efficiency, reducing both electricity costs and strain on supply infrastructure. Moving to the diagnostic side, OBC faults fall into five main categories — AC input issues like voltage irregularities or connector damage, over-temperature conditions from coolant system failures, and output-stage problems that I'm about to detail.\n\nOutput voltage or current deviations point to internal electronics failures or battery contactor issues, while isolation faults indicate moisture ingress or HV-LV barrier damage. Communication faults mean the OBC can't receive charge commands from the BMS or VCU, suggesting harness damage or ECU power supply problems. Each fault category directs the diagnostic approach differently — thermal issues require cooling system inspection while communication problems lead to harness and power supply checks.\n\nNow looking at DC fast charging with CCS2, the circuit topology changes significantly compared to Level 2 AC charging. During AC charging, current flows through the OBC's conversion stages before reaching the battery, but DC fast charging bypasses the onboard charger entirely, routing high-current DC directly from the external charger through the HV bus to the battery pack. The DC-DC converter must run continuously whenever the vehicle is in READY mode to maintain 12V power to critical systems like the BMS and contactors. If it fails, the 12V auxiliary battery drains quickly, and once voltage drops below the threshold needed to keep the main contactors energized, the HV battery disconnects and the vehicle cannot start. After replacement, I need to verify HV insulation resistance to ensure no leakage from the new converter installation, along with other functional validation tests before the vehicle returns to service. Now I'm looking at the answer key for a 25-mark exam with multiple choice questions in Section A and short-answer questions in Section B, where each correct response needs to demonstrate the key technical actions or concepts being tested. I'm estimating the line count for the Q-format section—roughly 200 lines total across all three sections with headers, questions, sample answers, and the marking scheme. That fits comfortably within the available space, so I can proceed with adding this content to the existing file.\n\nNow I'm checking whether C01 needs updates to match the acceptance criteria format. The current questions use bracket notation like `[CoCU C01.1.1]`, but the criteria example shows the full format with descriptions like `CoCU C01.3.2 - HV Safety Procedures`. The grep test would still catch the bracket format, so technically the requirement is met, but I should verify if adding those descriptions would improve clarity without breaking anything.\n\nLooking at the example more closely, the reference seems off—C01 Work Activity 3, Step 2 is about identifying BEV type, not HV safety procedures—so the acceptance criteria example might just be illustrative rather than prescriptive. Since C01 was already modified and the test passes with the current format, I'll focus only on completing C02 KA.md, which appears to be the actual gap.\n\nNow I'm adding the Q-format section with 40 multiple-choice questions and double-checking my answer key to make sure the options align with what I've written—Q7 should be B for over-charge/mechanical crush/overtemperature, and I'm verifying the rest of the answers match their corresponding options. I'm finishing up the remaining questions—Q17 through Q20 all check out. Now I'm verifying the answer key against the marking scheme to make sure everything aligns correctly, and it does. All 20 answers are confirmed and ready to be formatted into the final table.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7052,"cache_read_input_tokens":57588,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7052},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"794df4fc-0917-40f0-aba2-b2d02fc662d7","uuid":"ed790bcf-145f-4b9d-847d-dd48ccfaff29"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_019zB2vVJGxhfGkzF35qZtRE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have full context. Let me emit the plan block.\n\n<plan>\n{\n  \"files_to_create\": [],\n  \"files_to_modify\": [\"bev-diagnostic-rectification/C02/KA.md\"],\n  \"functions_to_add\": [],\n  \"estimated_loc\": 210\n}\n</plan>\n\n## Current State\nC01/KA.md already contains a complete Q-format section (Q1–Q35, 25 marks, CoCU C01 tags). C02/KA.md only has the traditional Section A–C exam format (10 MCQ + 5 short + 3 structured = ~95 lines) with no Q-format question bank and no CoCU reference codes.\n\n## Problem Identified\nUS-013 requires C02/KA.md to contain 40+ questions tagged with `CoCU C02` references, a 25-mark answer key, BMS theory 35% / HV cable systems 35% / OBC 30% distribution, and ≥30% scenario-based questions with 50+ word sample answers. None of these exist in the current C02/KA.md.\n\n## Planned Changes\n- Append a complete Q-Format Question Bank to `bev-diagnostic-rectification/C02/KA.md`:\n  - Section A: Q1–Q20 MCQ (20 marks, 1 each) — Q1–Q7 BMS Theory, Q8–Q14 HV Cables, Q15–Q20 OBC\n  - Section B: Q21–Q25 Graded Scenarios (5 marks, 1 each) — with 50–80 word sample answers\n  - Section C: Q26–Q40 Enrichment Scenarios (0 marks) — with 50–80 word sample answers\n  - Marking scheme table with all MCQ answers\n  - Distribution: BMS Theory 35% (14/40), HV Cable Systems 35% (14/40), OBC 30% (12/40)\n  - All 40 questions tagged with `[CoCU C02.x.x]` references"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7052,"cache_read_input_tokens":57588,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7052},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"794df4fc-0917-40f0-aba2-b2d02fc662d7","uuid":"647a3d41-bf8c-4eb8-9d6b-3cab457303fb"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_019zB2vVJGxhfGkzF35qZtRE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XwpBUS64AotvQopgW7MjFp","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C02\\KA.md","old_string":"**C3** (10 marks): Table with OBC vs DC-DC: Function (AC to DC vs HV DC to 12V DC), Input (AC 230/400V vs 200-800V DC), Output (200-800V DC vs 13.5-14.4V DC), Cooling (liquid vs liquid), Diagnostic (charging power/codes vs output voltage/stability).","new_string":"**C3** (10 marks): Table with OBC vs DC-DC: Function (AC to DC vs HV DC to 12V DC), Input (AC 230/400V vs 200-800V DC), Output (200-800V DC vs 13.5-14.4V DC), Cooling (liquid vs liquid), Diagnostic (charging power/codes vs output voltage/stability).\n\n---\n\n## BANK SOALAN Q-FORMAT / Q-FORMAT QUESTION BANK\n\n*Format JPK WIM — Jumlah: 40 soalan, 25 markah / Total: 40 questions, 25 marks*\n*Taburan / Distribution: BMS Theory 35% (Q1–Q7, Q21–Q22, Q26–Q30), HV Cable Systems 35% (Q8–Q14, Q23–Q24, Q31–Q35), OBC 30% (Q15–Q20, Q25, Q36–Q40)*\n\n---\n\n### Bahagian A / Section A — Pilihan Berganda / Multiple Choice (20 markah / marks)\n\n*Pilih jawapan TERBAIK. Setiap soalan bernilai 1 markah. / Choose the BEST answer. Each question carries 1 mark.*\n\n**Q1.** Battery cells connected in SERIES increase: A. Capacity (Ah) only  B. System voltage  C. Individual cell temperature  D. Charging speed only  [CoCU C02.5.3 — Battery Module System Inspection]\n**Q2.** Nominal voltage of a standard Li-ion (NMC) traction cell: A. 1.5V  B. 2.4V  C. 3.6–3.7V  D. 7.4V  [CoCU C02.5.3]\n**Q3.** The BMS does NOT directly control: A. Cell voltage monitoring  B. SOC calculation  C. Traction motor torque output  D. Cell temperature monitoring  [CoCU C02.1.2 — BMS Safety Requirement]\n**Q4.** The Cell Management Unit (CMU) reports to the: A. On-Board Charger  B. Motor Control Unit  C. BMS master controller  D. DC-DC converter  [CoCU C02.1.2]\n**Q5.** Passive cell balancing dissipates excess energy as: A. Mechanical work  B. Heat through bleed resistors  C. Stored charge in supercapacitors  D. Additional charge to adjacent cells  [CoCU C02.1.2]\n**Q6.** The pre-charge circuit limits: A. Coolant flow rate  B. Cell voltage imbalance  C. Inrush current surge when main contactors close against uncharged DC-link capacitors  D. HV insulation leakage  [CoCU C02.4.3 — Pre-charge Circuit Inspection]\n**Q7.** Li-ion thermal runaway can be triggered by: A. Normal low-current discharge only  B. Over-charge, mechanical crush, or sustained overtemperature  C. Exposure to temperatures below 10°C  D. Regenerative braking above 0.3g  [CoCU C02.5.3]\n**Q8.** HV cables in a BEV are identified by: A. Red insulation  B. Yellow conduit  C. Orange insulation/conduit  D. Blue shielded cable  [CoCU C02.2.1 — HV Cable Replacement Safety]\n**Q9.** Removing the manual service plug (MSP) physically: A. Disconnects the 12V auxiliary battery only  B. Activates the fire suppression  C. Splits the battery string into two isolated halves, breaking the main HV circuit  D. Triggers ABS pump self-test  [CoCU C02.2.4 — Disconnect HV Circuit]\n**Q10.** The HV interlock loop ensures contactors open when: A. Battery SOC drops below 10%  B. Any HV connector is unseated or the service plug is removed  C. Cabin temperature exceeds 30°C  D. The ABS controller requests braking  [CoCU C02.2.6 — Interlock Inspection]\n**Q11.** Minimum personnel required for traction battery pack removal: A. 1 technician  B. 2 technicians  C. 3 persons (technician + lift operator + safety observer)  D. 5 persons  [CoCU C02.5.7 — Battery Pack Removal]\n**Q12.** HV cable insulation resistance between conductor and chassis must exceed: A. 100 Ω  B. 1 kΩ  C. 1 MΩ  D. 1 GΩ  [CoCU C02.2.5 — HV Cable Condition Inspection]\n**Q13.** Lockout-Tagout (LOTO) is applied after de-energisation to: A. Restore vehicle to READY state  B. Prevent accidental re-energisation while work proceeds  C. Calibrate the current sensor  D. Initiate active cell balancing  [CoCU C02.1.4 — Hazard Identification]\n**Q14.** Standard pre-charge resistor value range: A. 0.1–1 Ω  B. 5–15 Ω  C. 50–200 Ω  D. 10 kΩ  [CoCU C02.4.3]\n**Q15.** The On-Board Charger (OBC) converts: A. DC battery power to AC for the motor  B. Battery HV DC to 12V DC  C. AC supply from the charging station to DC for battery charging  D. Kinetic energy to heat during braking  [CoCU C02.3.3 — OBC Functionality Check]\n**Q16.** The DC-DC converter in a BEV replaces which ICE vehicle component? A. Fuel pump  B. Alternator  C. Starter motor  D. Throttle body  [CoCU C02.6.3 — DC-DC Functionality Check]\n**Q17.** Typical DC-DC converter output voltage range: A. 5–6V  B. 9–10V  C. 13.5–14.4V  D. 48V  [CoCU C02.6.3]\n**Q18.** OBC coolant drained during replacement must be classified as: A. Municipal effluent  B. Scheduled Waste SW305  C. Recyclable process water  D. Category III industrial liquid  [CoCU C02.3.6 — Coolant Drain Procedure]\n**Q19.** A Level 2 AC charger connects to the vehicle via: A. 12V auxiliary port  B. Orange HV DC cable  C. AC inlet socket (J1772/Type 2)  D. OBD-II CAN bus port  [CoCU C02.3.7 — OBC HV Cable Disconnect]\n**Q20.** The FIRST safety test after OBC replacement is: A. Road test  B. HV insulation resistance test  C. Cabin temperature check  D. 12V battery load test  [CoCU C02.3.11 — OBC Functionality Test]\n\n---\n\n### Bahagian B / Section B — Soalan Senario Bermarkah / Graded Scenario Questions (5 markah / marks)\n\n*Jawab SEMUA soalan. Setiap soalan bernilai 1 markah. / Answer ALL questions. Each question carries 1 mark.*\n\n**Q21.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] During BMS diagnostics, Module 4 shows all cell voltages at 3.78–3.80V except Cell #11, which reads 3.52V — a deviation of 0.27V. Interpret this finding and state the required action. [CoCU C02.5.8 — Battery Module System Inspection]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* A deviation of 0.27V far exceeds the acceptable ±0.05V tolerance, indicating severe capacity loss in Cell #11 likely due to lithium plating, internal micro-short circuit, or advanced electrolyte decomposition. The BMS is forced to terminate discharge when Cell #11 reaches cut-off voltage, significantly reducing pack range and performance. Escalate immediately to a traction battery pack replacement work order. Document all individual cell voltages in the diagnostic report. Do NOT clear the fault code; preserve freeze-frame data for root cause analysis.\n\n**Q22.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] A BEV arrives with fault code indicating \"Pre-charge Circuit Timeout — capacitor voltage did not reach threshold within 2.0 seconds.\" Describe your diagnostic response. [CoCU C02.4.3 / C02.4.6 — Pre-charge Diagnosis]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Record the fault code and freeze-frame data before clearing. Pre-charge timeout means DC-link capacitors failed to reach approximately 95% of battery voltage within the allowed window, indicating high resistance in the pre-charge path or a defective relay. Measure the pre-charge resistor value against manufacturer specification. Check pre-charge relay coil resistance and contact resistance. Inspect all wiring and connectors in the pre-charge loop for corrosion, pin damage, or breaks. If the relay is confirmed faulty, initiate a pre-charge relay pack replacement work order per CoCU C02.4.7.\n\n**Q23.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] During underbody inspection, you observe an orange HV cable with abrasion damage — the outer conduit is worn down to the inner insulation layer but no conductor is visible. Describe your required action. [CoCU C02.2.5 / C02.2.7 — HV Cable Inspection and Replacement]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Even though no copper conductor is exposed, damage to the outer conduit compromises the double-insulation protection required for HV circuits and creates risk of further abrasion against chassis metal. Do NOT clear the vehicle for service. Photograph the damage and document exact location and cable run in the work order. Measure cable insulation resistance at the damaged point using a CAT III insulation tester — if below 1 MΩ, the cable must be replaced immediately. Initiate an HV cable replacement work order referencing CoCU C02.2.7.\n\n**Q24.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] A service plug is missing from a BEV brought in for OBC replacement. Another technician suggests proceeding with HV de-energisation using only the 12V disconnect. Explain why this is unacceptable and state the correct action. [CoCU C02.1.4 / C02.2.4]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* A missing service plug means the main HV battery circuit is not physically broken — the contactors are the only remaining protection, and they can close unexpectedly if a control fault occurs or the 12V battery is briefly reconnected. Proceeding without a service plug violates manufacturer safety protocol and OSHA 1994 requirements. The correct action is to STOP all HV work immediately, notify the supervisor, document the missing plug in the work order, and obtain a replacement service plug from the parts department before any HV work begins.\n\n**Q25.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] A customer reports Level 2 AC charging stopped after 20 minutes, with an amber warning displayed. Fault codes P0AA9 (Battery Voltage Sensor) and U0100 (OBC CAN lost communication) are stored together. Describe your diagnostic approach. [CoCU C02.3.3 / C02.3.8 — OBC Diagnosis]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Record both fault codes with freeze-frame data before clearing. P0AA9 and U0100 together suggest OBC CAN communication failure or a shared supply fault causing simultaneous ECU loss of synchronisation. Check the OBC CAN bus connector for corrosion, pin push-out, and harness damage near the OBC unit. Test OBC 12V supply voltage and ground integrity. Inspect the AC inlet socket connector for burn marks or deformation indicating connection overheating. If OBC is confirmed faulty through substitution testing, initiate OBC replacement per CoCU C02.3.9.\n\n---\n\n### Bahagian C / Section C — Senario Pengayaan / Enrichment Practice Scenarios (0 markah / marks)\n\n*Soalan latihan untuk kefahaman mendalam. / Practice questions for deeper understanding.*\n\n**Q26.** Explain the difference between State of Charge (SOC) and State of Health (SOH). Why must both be monitored in a BEV? [CoCU C02.1.2]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* SOC is the current charge level as a percentage of the pack's present usable capacity — it indicates available driving energy. SOH is the pack's current capacity relative to its original new capacity — it indicates long-term degradation. Both must be monitored because a 100% SOC in a pack with 70% SOH delivers far less range than the same SOC in a healthy pack. The BMS uses SOH to progressively adjust SOC charge and discharge limits, protecting degraded cells from over-charge and over-discharge stress that accelerates thermal runaway risk.\n\n**Q27.** Describe active vs passive cell balancing. Explain which is used in most production BEVs and why. [CoCU C02.1.2]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Passive balancing bleeds excess energy from higher-charged cells as heat through shunt resistors, equalising all cells to the lowest cell's voltage. Active balancing transfers charge from stronger to weaker cells using inductors or DC-DC converters, losing very little energy. Production BEVs overwhelmingly use passive balancing because it is simpler, less expensive, and sufficiently effective during normal charge cycles when voltage deviations are small. Active balancing adds cost and complexity only justified in ultra-high-performance applications where even small energy losses are unacceptable.\n\n**Q28.** Explain thermal runaway propagation in a battery module and why BEV battery fires require a different approach than standard vehicle fires. [CoCU C02.5.3]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* When one cell enters thermal runaway, it generates intense heat and vents toxic gases including hydrogen fluoride and carbon monoxide, causing adjacent cells to enter runaway in a chain reaction that can propagate through an entire module in seconds. The sealed battery housing means standard fire suppression cannot reach internal cells. Large volumes of water are required to cool the battery externally and slow propagation — CO₂ or dry powder only suppresses surface flames temporarily. JBPM guidelines require submerging or continuously flooding the battery pack until thermal runaway ceases completely.\n\n**Q29.** Explain the role of the BMS contactor driver circuit in preventing battery damage during a collision. [CoCU C02.1.2]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* The BMS receives crash signals from the airbag control module via CAN bus within milliseconds of impact detection. Upon receiving the crash signal, the BMS immediately opens both main contactors and disables the pre-charge circuit, disconnecting the battery pack from all vehicle HV circuits. This prevents high energy stored in the pack from flowing through damaged HV cables or components short-circuited by the collision. Post-crash, the contactors remain open until a technician manually resets the system, preventing unexpected HV re-energisation during rescue operations.\n\n**Q30.** Cell voltage deviations increase significantly during high-rate discharge but appear normal at low rate. Explain what this indicates and how to confirm the diagnosis. [CoCU C02.5.8]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Voltage deviations appearing only under high current load but normalising at low load are the classic signature of increased internal resistance in specific cells. Higher internal resistance causes greater voltage sag under load (V = EMF − I×R), making affected cells appear weaker at high discharge rates. At low rates, the small IR drop is negligible and cells appear equal. The root cause is typically advanced electrolyte decomposition or lithium plating. Confirm by measuring individual cell internal resistance using the BMS diagnostic tool; cells significantly above the manufacturer baseline require module replacement.\n\n**Q31.** Describe the six-step Lockout-Tagout (LOTO) procedure for BEV HV work. State what documentation must be completed after LOTO is applied. [CoCU C02.1.4]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Six steps: (1) Notify all affected personnel that energy isolation is being performed. (2) Turn READY off and remove key to the safety lockbox. (3) Disconnect 12V auxiliary battery negative terminal. (4) Remove the manual service plug and secure in the safety lockbox. (5) Apply LOTO padlock and \"DANGER — DO NOT OPERATE\" tag to the service plug port and battery disconnect points. (6) Verify zero energy with a CAT III multimeter at minimum three HV points. Documentation: complete the LOTO record form with technician name, work order number, date, time, zero-energy readings, and supervisor sign-off before any HV contact.\n\n**Q32.** Explain HV cable routing rules that must be followed during replacement. Why must HV cables not share conduit with LV harnesses? [CoCU C02.2.1 / C02.2.7]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Routing rules: maintain minimum bend radius (typically 3× cable outer diameter) to prevent insulation stress; secure in manufacturer-specified clamps at required intervals; protect from heat sources with heat shields; route away from sharp chassis edges. HV cables must NOT share conduit with LV harnesses because any insulation failure or pinching of the HV cable inside shared conduit could induce dangerous HV voltage onto LV signal wires, damaging ECUs or creating shock hazard through LV connectors that technicians handle without HV PPE. Segregation is a mandatory safety design principle in all BEV platforms.\n\n**Q33.** Describe the complete inspection and replacement procedure for a damaged HV cable section. Include pre-work and post-work tests. [CoCU C02.2.5 / C02.2.7 / C02.2.8]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Pre-work: full HV de-energisation, LOTO application, zero-energy verification at three points. Inspection: photograph damage, measure insulation resistance of damaged cable — must exceed 1 MΩ to chassis. Replacement: disconnect both ends using insulated tools; record cable routing path, segregation points, and clamp positions; install new cable following manufacturer's routing specification; reconnect all interlock connectors. Post-work: remove LOTO in reverse order; reconnect service plug and 12V battery; cycle to READY; verify no HV fault codes; perform HV insulation resistance test; confirm interlock continuity; complete functional test.\n\n**Q34.** Describe the symptoms and diagnosis of a welded main positive contactor. What risk does this create for workshop personnel? [CoCU C02.4.3 / C02.2.6]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Symptoms: HV system remains energised even after READY OFF and service plug removal; current sensing shows HV bus voltage present; BMS logs contactor commanded open but voltage does not drop. Diagnosis: measure HV bus voltage after full de-energisation — if voltage remains, contact faces have welded shut from inrush current arc damage. Risk: the welded contactor creates a permanently live HV bus that cannot be isolated by normal procedures, removing the primary safety isolation mechanism. The vehicle must be treated as permanently energised, requiring specialist insulation tools and advanced disassembly procedures before any rectification work proceeds.\n\n**Q35.** A technician omits the post-replacement HV insulation resistance test and returns the vehicle to the customer. Explain what could happen and the legal implications. [CoCU C02.2.8]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Without post-replacement insulation resistance testing, an improperly installed cable with insufficient insulation or a damaged grommet could create a leakage path between the HV circuit and the vehicle chassis. Moisture ingress over time could reduce insulation resistance below 500 Ω/V, causing the IMD to trigger isolation faults, unexpected HV system shutdown while driving, or in severe cases HV shock if occupants contact conducting body panels. Legally, the workshop bears liability under OSHA 1994 Section 17 (duty of care to non-employees) and the Road Transport Act 1987 for returning an unsafe vehicle to public roads.\n\n**Q36.** Explain how an OBC power factor correction (PFC) circuit improves charging efficiency and reduces impact on the AC supply grid. [CoCU C02.3.3]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Without PFC, the OBC draws pulsed current from the AC grid, creating harmonic distortion that degrades power quality for all connected equipment. The PFC circuit uses an active boost converter to shape input current to closely match the AC voltage sinusoid, maintaining near-unity power factor. This means apparent power consumed equals real power delivered to the battery, minimising reactive current draw from the grid. PFC reduces harmonic injection, prevents voltage distortion on shared circuits, and maximises charging efficiency — allowing the OBC to deliver more energy to the battery per unit of AC energy drawn.\n\n**Q37.** Describe the typical OBC diagnostic fault categories and what each indicates about the component or circuit at fault. [CoCU C02.3.8]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Five main categories: (1) AC input fault — incorrect voltage/frequency, damaged inlet socket, or AC cable issue. (2) OBC over-temperature — blocked coolant circuit, failed coolant pump, or ambient overload. (3) Output voltage/current deviation — internal OBC electronics failure, battery contactor fault, or CMU data conflict. (4) Isolation fault — moisture ingress to OBC housing or damaged HV-LV isolation barrier. (5) CAN communication fault — OBC unable to receive charge commands from BMS/VCU, indicating harness damage or ECU supply issue. Each category directs different diagnostic focus — thermal faults lead to cooling circuit inspection; communication faults lead to harness and ECU supply checks.\n\n**Q38.** A BEV is equipped with DC fast charging (CCS2). Explain how the HV circuit topology differs during DC fast charging compared to Level 2 AC charging. [CoCU C02.3.7]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* During Level 2 AC charging, current flows through the OBC: AC inlet → OBC AC input → OBC power conversion → HV bus → battery pack; the OBC controls charge rate. During DC fast charging via CCS2, the external fast charger directly supplies DC at the required battery voltage — the OBC AC conversion stage is bypassed entirely. The DC inlet connects directly to the HV bus through a dedicated inlet contactor, allowing far higher power delivery up to 350 kW. The BMS communicates with the external charger via ISO 15118 protocol to dynamically adjust charge voltage and current throughout the session.\n\n**Q39.** Explain the dual supply paths for the DC-DC converter and why the converter must operate correctly for the vehicle to start. [CoCU C02.6.3]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* The DC-DC converter steps down HV battery voltage to 13.5–14.4V DC to charge the 12V auxiliary battery and supply all 12V ECUs, lights, and accessories. Unlike ICE vehicles where the alternator only operates when the engine runs, the BEV DC-DC converter runs continuously when READY is on. If the converter fails, the 12V system depletes rapidly. Below a critical 12V threshold, the BMS master controller, contactors, and interlock circuits lose power — the main contactors fail open, disconnecting HV from the drive system. The vehicle cannot enter READY state without 12V power, making DC-DC converter operation essential for every start.\n\n**Q40.** After DC-DC converter replacement, what functional tests must be conducted before returning the vehicle to service? [CoCU C02.6.10 — DC-DC Converter Functionality Test]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Mandatory tests: (1) HV insulation resistance test — confirm no HV-to-chassis leakage from new converter installation. (2) 12V output voltage measurement — must read 13.5–14.4V DC under load with READY on. (3) 12V auxiliary battery charge current — confirm converter is actively charging the auxiliary battery. (4) BMS fault code scan — confirm no HV isolation or DC-DC communication faults. (5) Coolant circuit pressure check — confirm no leaks at converter connections after coolant refill. (6) Full load functional test — operate headlights, climate, and multimedia simultaneously and verify output voltage remains stable under maximum 12V load.\n\n---\n\n### Skema Pemarkahan Q-Format / Q-Format Marking Scheme (25 Markah / Marks)\n\n**Bahagian A / Section A (Q1–Q20 — 1 markah setiap soalan / 1 mark each):**\n\n| Q | Jwp | Q | Jwp | Q | Jwp | Q | Jwp |\n|---|-----|---|-----|---|-----|---|-----|\n| Q1 | B | Q6 | C | Q11 | C | Q16 | B |\n| Q2 | C | Q7 | B | Q12 | C | Q17 | C |\n| Q3 | C | Q8 | C | Q13 | B | Q18 | B |\n| Q4 | C | Q9 | C | Q14 | C | Q19 | C |\n| Q5 | B | Q10 | B | Q15 | C | Q20 | B |\n\n**Bahagian B / Section B (Q21–Q25 — 1 markah setiap soalan / 1 mark each):**\nAward 1 mark for responses that correctly identify the key technical action(s) and reference relevant CoCU assessment criteria. See sample answers above.\n\n**Bahagian C / Section C (Q26–Q40 — Pengayaan / Enrichment — 0 markah / 0 marks):**\nSample answers to be provided by trainer for guided discussion.\n\n**Jumlah Q-Format / Q-Format Total: 25 markah / marks**\n*Lulus / Pass: 60% = 15 markah/marks. Rujukan / References: CoCU C02 — semua kriteria penilaian / all assessment criteria.*"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7052,"cache_read_input_tokens":57588,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7052},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"794df4fc-0917-40f0-aba2-b2d02fc662d7","uuid":"0b94c9d4-507c-4322-a531-ee425cb2ffbe"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XwpBUS64AotvQopgW7MjFp","type":"tool_result","content":"The file C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C02\\KA.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"794df4fc-0917-40f0-aba2-b2d02fc662d7","uuid":"ff91a116-cf6b-43fb-b587-14d9670dd3d5","timestamp":"2026-04-16T15:09:53.589Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C02\\KA.md","oldString":"**C3** (10 marks): Table with OBC vs DC-DC: Function (AC to DC vs HV DC to 12V DC), Input (AC 230/400V vs 200-800V DC), Output (200-800V DC vs 13.5-14.4V DC), Cooling (liquid vs liquid), Diagnostic (charging power/codes vs output voltage/stability).","newString":"**C3** (10 marks): Table with OBC vs DC-DC: Function (AC to DC vs HV DC to 12V DC), Input (AC 230/400V vs 200-800V DC), Output (200-800V DC vs 13.5-14.4V DC), Cooling (liquid vs liquid), Diagnostic (charging power/codes vs output voltage/stability).\n\n---\n\n## BANK SOALAN Q-FORMAT / Q-FORMAT QUESTION BANK\n\n*Format JPK WIM — Jumlah: 40 soalan, 25 markah / Total: 40 questions, 25 marks*\n*Taburan / Distribution: BMS Theory 35% (Q1–Q7, Q21–Q22, Q26–Q30), HV Cable Systems 35% (Q8–Q14, Q23–Q24, Q31–Q35), OBC 30% (Q15–Q20, Q25, Q36–Q40)*\n\n---\n\n### Bahagian A / Section A — Pilihan Berganda / Multiple Choice (20 markah / marks)\n\n*Pilih jawapan TERBAIK. Setiap soalan bernilai 1 markah. / Choose the BEST answer. Each question carries 1 mark.*\n\n**Q1.** Battery cells connected in SERIES increase: A. Capacity (Ah) only  B. System voltage  C. Individual cell temperature  D. Charging speed only  [CoCU C02.5.3 — Battery Module System Inspection]\n**Q2.** Nominal voltage of a standard Li-ion (NMC) traction cell: A. 1.5V  B. 2.4V  C. 3.6–3.7V  D. 7.4V  [CoCU C02.5.3]\n**Q3.** The BMS does NOT directly control: A. Cell voltage monitoring  B. SOC calculation  C. Traction motor torque output  D. Cell temperature monitoring  [CoCU C02.1.2 — BMS Safety Requirement]\n**Q4.** The Cell Management Unit (CMU) reports to the: A. On-Board Charger  B. Motor Control Unit  C. BMS master controller  D. DC-DC converter  [CoCU C02.1.2]\n**Q5.** Passive cell balancing dissipates excess energy as: A. Mechanical work  B. Heat through bleed resistors  C. Stored charge in supercapacitors  D. Additional charge to adjacent cells  [CoCU C02.1.2]\n**Q6.** The pre-charge circuit limits: A. Coolant flow rate  B. Cell voltage imbalance  C. Inrush current surge when main contactors close against uncharged DC-link capacitors  D. HV insulation leakage  [CoCU C02.4.3 — Pre-charge Circuit Inspection]\n**Q7.** Li-ion thermal runaway can be triggered by: A. Normal low-current discharge only  B. Over-charge, mechanical crush, or sustained overtemperature  C. Exposure to temperatures below 10°C  D. Regenerative braking above 0.3g  [CoCU C02.5.3]\n**Q8.** HV cables in a BEV are identified by: A. Red insulation  B. Yellow conduit  C. Orange insulation/conduit  D. Blue shielded cable  [CoCU C02.2.1 — HV Cable Replacement Safety]\n**Q9.** Removing the manual service plug (MSP) physically: A. Disconnects the 12V auxiliary battery only  B. Activates the fire suppression  C. Splits the battery string into two isolated halves, breaking the main HV circuit  D. Triggers ABS pump self-test  [CoCU C02.2.4 — Disconnect HV Circuit]\n**Q10.** The HV interlock loop ensures contactors open when: A. Battery SOC drops below 10%  B. Any HV connector is unseated or the service plug is removed  C. Cabin temperature exceeds 30°C  D. The ABS controller requests braking  [CoCU C02.2.6 — Interlock Inspection]\n**Q11.** Minimum personnel required for traction battery pack removal: A. 1 technician  B. 2 technicians  C. 3 persons (technician + lift operator + safety observer)  D. 5 persons  [CoCU C02.5.7 — Battery Pack Removal]\n**Q12.** HV cable insulation resistance between conductor and chassis must exceed: A. 100 Ω  B. 1 kΩ  C. 1 MΩ  D. 1 GΩ  [CoCU C02.2.5 — HV Cable Condition Inspection]\n**Q13.** Lockout-Tagout (LOTO) is applied after de-energisation to: A. Restore vehicle to READY state  B. Prevent accidental re-energisation while work proceeds  C. Calibrate the current sensor  D. Initiate active cell balancing  [CoCU C02.1.4 — Hazard Identification]\n**Q14.** Standard pre-charge resistor value range: A. 0.1–1 Ω  B. 5–15 Ω  C. 50–200 Ω  D. 10 kΩ  [CoCU C02.4.3]\n**Q15.** The On-Board Charger (OBC) converts: A. DC battery power to AC for the motor  B. Battery HV DC to 12V DC  C. AC supply from the charging station to DC for battery charging  D. Kinetic energy to heat during braking  [CoCU C02.3.3 — OBC Functionality Check]\n**Q16.** The DC-DC converter in a BEV replaces which ICE vehicle component? A. Fuel pump  B. Alternator  C. Starter motor  D. Throttle body  [CoCU C02.6.3 — DC-DC Functionality Check]\n**Q17.** Typical DC-DC converter output voltage range: A. 5–6V  B. 9–10V  C. 13.5–14.4V  D. 48V  [CoCU C02.6.3]\n**Q18.** OBC coolant drained during replacement must be classified as: A. Municipal effluent  B. Scheduled Waste SW305  C. Recyclable process water  D. Category III industrial liquid  [CoCU C02.3.6 — Coolant Drain Procedure]\n**Q19.** A Level 2 AC charger connects to the vehicle via: A. 12V auxiliary port  B. Orange HV DC cable  C. AC inlet socket (J1772/Type 2)  D. OBD-II CAN bus port  [CoCU C02.3.7 — OBC HV Cable Disconnect]\n**Q20.** The FIRST safety test after OBC replacement is: A. Road test  B. HV insulation resistance test  C. Cabin temperature check  D. 12V battery load test  [CoCU C02.3.11 — OBC Functionality Test]\n\n---\n\n### Bahagian B / Section B — Soalan Senario Bermarkah / Graded Scenario Questions (5 markah / marks)\n\n*Jawab SEMUA soalan. Setiap soalan bernilai 1 markah. / Answer ALL questions. Each question carries 1 mark.*\n\n**Q21.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] During BMS diagnostics, Module 4 shows all cell voltages at 3.78–3.80V except Cell #11, which reads 3.52V — a deviation of 0.27V. Interpret this finding and state the required action. [CoCU C02.5.8 — Battery Module System Inspection]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* A deviation of 0.27V far exceeds the acceptable ±0.05V tolerance, indicating severe capacity loss in Cell #11 likely due to lithium plating, internal micro-short circuit, or advanced electrolyte decomposition. The BMS is forced to terminate discharge when Cell #11 reaches cut-off voltage, significantly reducing pack range and performance. Escalate immediately to a traction battery pack replacement work order. Document all individual cell voltages in the diagnostic report. Do NOT clear the fault code; preserve freeze-frame data for root cause analysis.\n\n**Q22.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] A BEV arrives with fault code indicating \"Pre-charge Circuit Timeout — capacitor voltage did not reach threshold within 2.0 seconds.\" Describe your diagnostic response. [CoCU C02.4.3 / C02.4.6 — Pre-charge Diagnosis]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Record the fault code and freeze-frame data before clearing. Pre-charge timeout means DC-link capacitors failed to reach approximately 95% of battery voltage within the allowed window, indicating high resistance in the pre-charge path or a defective relay. Measure the pre-charge resistor value against manufacturer specification. Check pre-charge relay coil resistance and contact resistance. Inspect all wiring and connectors in the pre-charge loop for corrosion, pin damage, or breaks. If the relay is confirmed faulty, initiate a pre-charge relay pack replacement work order per CoCU C02.4.7.\n\n**Q23.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] During underbody inspection, you observe an orange HV cable with abrasion damage — the outer conduit is worn down to the inner insulation layer but no conductor is visible. Describe your required action. [CoCU C02.2.5 / C02.2.7 — HV Cable Inspection and Replacement]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Even though no copper conductor is exposed, damage to the outer conduit compromises the double-insulation protection required for HV circuits and creates risk of further abrasion against chassis metal. Do NOT clear the vehicle for service. Photograph the damage and document exact location and cable run in the work order. Measure cable insulation resistance at the damaged point using a CAT III insulation tester — if below 1 MΩ, the cable must be replaced immediately. Initiate an HV cable replacement work order referencing CoCU C02.2.7.\n\n**Q24.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] A service plug is missing from a BEV brought in for OBC replacement. Another technician suggests proceeding with HV de-energisation using only the 12V disconnect. Explain why this is unacceptable and state the correct action. [CoCU C02.1.4 / C02.2.4]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* A missing service plug means the main HV battery circuit is not physically broken — the contactors are the only remaining protection, and they can close unexpectedly if a control fault occurs or the 12V battery is briefly reconnected. Proceeding without a service plug violates manufacturer safety protocol and OSHA 1994 requirements. The correct action is to STOP all HV work immediately, notify the supervisor, document the missing plug in the work order, and obtain a replacement service plug from the parts department before any HV work begins.\n\n**Q25.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] A customer reports Level 2 AC charging stopped after 20 minutes, with an amber warning displayed. Fault codes P0AA9 (Battery Voltage Sensor) and U0100 (OBC CAN lost communication) are stored together. Describe your diagnostic approach. [CoCU C02.3.3 / C02.3.8 — OBC Diagnosis]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Record both fault codes with freeze-frame data before clearing. P0AA9 and U0100 together suggest OBC CAN communication failure or a shared supply fault causing simultaneous ECU loss of synchronisation. Check the OBC CAN bus connector for corrosion, pin push-out, and harness damage near the OBC unit. Test OBC 12V supply voltage and ground integrity. Inspect the AC inlet socket connector for burn marks or deformation indicating connection overheating. If OBC is confirmed faulty through substitution testing, initiate OBC replacement per CoCU C02.3.9.\n\n---\n\n### Bahagian C / Section C — Senario Pengayaan / Enrichment Practice Scenarios (0 markah / marks)\n\n*Soalan latihan untuk kefahaman mendalam. / Practice questions for deeper understanding.*\n\n**Q26.** Explain the difference between State of Charge (SOC) and State of Health (SOH). Why must both be monitored in a BEV? [CoCU C02.1.2]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* SOC is the current charge level as a percentage of the pack's present usable capacity — it indicates available driving energy. SOH is the pack's current capacity relative to its original new capacity — it indicates long-term degradation. Both must be monitored because a 100% SOC in a pack with 70% SOH delivers far less range than the same SOC in a healthy pack. The BMS uses SOH to progressively adjust SOC charge and discharge limits, protecting degraded cells from over-charge and over-discharge stress that accelerates thermal runaway risk.\n\n**Q27.** Describe active vs passive cell balancing. Explain which is used in most production BEVs and why. [CoCU C02.1.2]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Passive balancing bleeds excess energy from higher-charged cells as heat through shunt resistors, equalising all cells to the lowest cell's voltage. Active balancing transfers charge from stronger to weaker cells using inductors or DC-DC converters, losing very little energy. Production BEVs overwhelmingly use passive balancing because it is simpler, less expensive, and sufficiently effective during normal charge cycles when voltage deviations are small. Active balancing adds cost and complexity only justified in ultra-high-performance applications where even small energy losses are unacceptable.\n\n**Q28.** Explain thermal runaway propagation in a battery module and why BEV battery fires require a different approach than standard vehicle fires. [CoCU C02.5.3]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* When one cell enters thermal runaway, it generates intense heat and vents toxic gases including hydrogen fluoride and carbon monoxide, causing adjacent cells to enter runaway in a chain reaction that can propagate through an entire module in seconds. The sealed battery housing means standard fire suppression cannot reach internal cells. Large volumes of water are required to cool the battery externally and slow propagation — CO₂ or dry powder only suppresses surface flames temporarily. JBPM guidelines require submerging or continuously flooding the battery pack until thermal runaway ceases completely.\n\n**Q29.** Explain the role of the BMS contactor driver circuit in preventing battery damage during a collision. [CoCU C02.1.2]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* The BMS receives crash signals from the airbag control module via CAN bus within milliseconds of impact detection. Upon receiving the crash signal, the BMS immediately opens both main contactors and disables the pre-charge circuit, disconnecting the battery pack from all vehicle HV circuits. This prevents high energy stored in the pack from flowing through damaged HV cables or components short-circuited by the collision. Post-crash, the contactors remain open until a technician manually resets the system, preventing unexpected HV re-energisation during rescue operations.\n\n**Q30.** Cell voltage deviations increase significantly during high-rate discharge but appear normal at low rate. Explain what this indicates and how to confirm the diagnosis. [CoCU C02.5.8]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Voltage deviations appearing only under high current load but normalising at low load are the classic signature of increased internal resistance in specific cells. Higher internal resistance causes greater voltage sag under load (V = EMF − I×R), making affected cells appear weaker at high discharge rates. At low rates, the small IR drop is negligible and cells appear equal. The root cause is typically advanced electrolyte decomposition or lithium plating. Confirm by measuring individual cell internal resistance using the BMS diagnostic tool; cells significantly above the manufacturer baseline require module replacement.\n\n**Q31.** Describe the six-step Lockout-Tagout (LOTO) procedure for BEV HV work. State what documentation must be completed after LOTO is applied. [CoCU C02.1.4]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Six steps: (1) Notify all affected personnel that energy isolation is being performed. (2) Turn READY off and remove key to the safety lockbox. (3) Disconnect 12V auxiliary battery negative terminal. (4) Remove the manual service plug and secure in the safety lockbox. (5) Apply LOTO padlock and \"DANGER — DO NOT OPERATE\" tag to the service plug port and battery disconnect points. (6) Verify zero energy with a CAT III multimeter at minimum three HV points. Documentation: complete the LOTO record form with technician name, work order number, date, time, zero-energy readings, and supervisor sign-off before any HV contact.\n\n**Q32.** Explain HV cable routing rules that must be followed during replacement. Why must HV cables not share conduit with LV harnesses? [CoCU C02.2.1 / C02.2.7]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Routing rules: maintain minimum bend radius (typically 3× cable outer diameter) to prevent insulation stress; secure in manufacturer-specified clamps at required intervals; protect from heat sources with heat shields; route away from sharp chassis edges. HV cables must NOT share conduit with LV harnesses because any insulation failure or pinching of the HV cable inside shared conduit could induce dangerous HV voltage onto LV signal wires, damaging ECUs or creating shock hazard through LV connectors that technicians handle without HV PPE. Segregation is a mandatory safety design principle in all BEV platforms.\n\n**Q33.** Describe the complete inspection and replacement procedure for a damaged HV cable section. Include pre-work and post-work tests. [CoCU C02.2.5 / C02.2.7 / C02.2.8]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Pre-work: full HV de-energisation, LOTO application, zero-energy verification at three points. Inspection: photograph damage, measure insulation resistance of damaged cable — must exceed 1 MΩ to chassis. Replacement: disconnect both ends using insulated tools; record cable routing path, segregation points, and clamp positions; install new cable following manufacturer's routing specification; reconnect all interlock connectors. Post-work: remove LOTO in reverse order; reconnect service plug and 12V battery; cycle to READY; verify no HV fault codes; perform HV insulation resistance test; confirm interlock continuity; complete functional test.\n\n**Q34.** Describe the symptoms and diagnosis of a welded main positive contactor. What risk does this create for workshop personnel? [CoCU C02.4.3 / C02.2.6]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Symptoms: HV system remains energised even after READY OFF and service plug removal; current sensing shows HV bus voltage present; BMS logs contactor commanded open but voltage does not drop. Diagnosis: measure HV bus voltage after full de-energisation — if voltage remains, contact faces have welded shut from inrush current arc damage. Risk: the welded contactor creates a permanently live HV bus that cannot be isolated by normal procedures, removing the primary safety isolation mechanism. The vehicle must be treated as permanently energised, requiring specialist insulation tools and advanced disassembly procedures before any rectification work proceeds.\n\n**Q35.** A technician omits the post-replacement HV insulation resistance test and returns the vehicle to the customer. Explain what could happen and the legal implications. [CoCU C02.2.8]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Without post-replacement insulation resistance testing, an improperly installed cable with insufficient insulation or a damaged grommet could create a leakage path between the HV circuit and the vehicle chassis. Moisture ingress over time could reduce insulation resistance below 500 Ω/V, causing the IMD to trigger isolation faults, unexpected HV system shutdown while driving, or in severe cases HV shock if occupants contact conducting body panels. Legally, the workshop bears liability under OSHA 1994 Section 17 (duty of care to non-employees) and the Road Transport Act 1987 for returning an unsafe vehicle to public roads.\n\n**Q36.** Explain how an OBC power factor correction (PFC) circuit improves charging efficiency and reduces impact on the AC supply grid. [CoCU C02.3.3]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Without PFC, the OBC draws pulsed current from the AC grid, creating harmonic distortion that degrades power quality for all connected equipment. The PFC circuit uses an active boost converter to shape input current to closely match the AC voltage sinusoid, maintaining near-unity power factor. This means apparent power consumed equals real power delivered to the battery, minimising reactive current draw from the grid. PFC reduces harmonic injection, prevents voltage distortion on shared circuits, and maximises charging efficiency — allowing the OBC to deliver more energy to the battery per unit of AC energy drawn.\n\n**Q37.** Describe the typical OBC diagnostic fault categories and what each indicates about the component or circuit at fault. [CoCU C02.3.8]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Five main categories: (1) AC input fault — incorrect voltage/frequency, damaged inlet socket, or AC cable issue. (2) OBC over-temperature — blocked coolant circuit, failed coolant pump, or ambient overload. (3) Output voltage/current deviation — internal OBC electronics failure, battery contactor fault, or CMU data conflict. (4) Isolation fault — moisture ingress to OBC housing or damaged HV-LV isolation barrier. (5) CAN communication fault — OBC unable to receive charge commands from BMS/VCU, indicating harness damage or ECU supply issue. Each category directs different diagnostic focus — thermal faults lead to cooling circuit inspection; communication faults lead to harness and ECU supply checks.\n\n**Q38.** A BEV is equipped with DC fast charging (CCS2). Explain how the HV circuit topology differs during DC fast charging compared to Level 2 AC charging. [CoCU C02.3.7]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* During Level 2 AC charging, current flows through the OBC: AC inlet → OBC AC input → OBC power conversion → HV bus → battery pack; the OBC controls charge rate. During DC fast charging via CCS2, the external fast charger directly supplies DC at the required battery voltage — the OBC AC conversion stage is bypassed entirely. The DC inlet connects directly to the HV bus through a dedicated inlet contactor, allowing far higher power delivery up to 350 kW. The BMS communicates with the external charger via ISO 15118 protocol to dynamically adjust charge voltage and current throughout the session.\n\n**Q39.** Explain the dual supply paths for the DC-DC converter and why the converter must operate correctly for the vehicle to start. [CoCU C02.6.3]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* The DC-DC converter steps down HV battery voltage to 13.5–14.4V DC to charge the 12V auxiliary battery and supply all 12V ECUs, lights, and accessories. Unlike ICE vehicles where the alternator only operates when the engine runs, the BEV DC-DC converter runs continuously when READY is on. If the converter fails, the 12V system depletes rapidly. Below a critical 12V threshold, the BMS master controller, contactors, and interlock circuits lose power — the main contactors fail open, disconnecting HV from the drive system. The vehicle cannot enter READY state without 12V power, making DC-DC converter operation essential for every start.\n\n**Q40.** After DC-DC converter replacement, what functional tests must be conducted before returning the vehicle to service? [CoCU C02.6.10 — DC-DC Converter Functionality Test]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Mandatory tests: (1) HV insulation resistance test — confirm no HV-to-chassis leakage from new converter installation. (2) 12V output voltage measurement — must read 13.5–14.4V DC under load with READY on. (3) 12V auxiliary battery charge current — confirm converter is actively charging the auxiliary battery. (4) BMS fault code scan — confirm no HV isolation or DC-DC communication faults. (5) Coolant circuit pressure check — confirm no leaks at converter connections after coolant refill. (6) Full load functional test — operate headlights, climate, and multimedia simultaneously and verify output voltage remains stable under maximum 12V load.\n\n---\n\n### Skema Pemarkahan Q-Format / Q-Format Marking Scheme (25 Markah / Marks)\n\n**Bahagian A / Section A (Q1–Q20 — 1 markah setiap soalan / 1 mark each):**\n\n| Q | Jwp | Q | Jwp | Q | Jwp | Q | Jwp |\n|---|-----|---|-----|---|-----|---|-----|\n| Q1 | B | Q6 | C | Q11 | C | Q16 | B |\n| Q2 | C | Q7 | B | Q12 | C | Q17 | C |\n| Q3 | C | Q8 | C | Q13 | B | Q18 | B |\n| Q4 | C | Q9 | C | Q14 | C | Q19 | C |\n| Q5 | B | Q10 | B | Q15 | C | Q20 | B |\n\n**Bahagian B / Section B (Q21–Q25 — 1 markah setiap soalan / 1 mark each):**\nAward 1 mark for responses that correctly identify the key technical action(s) and reference relevant CoCU assessment criteria. See sample answers above.\n\n**Bahagian C / Section C (Q26–Q40 — Pengayaan / Enrichment — 0 markah / 0 marks):**\nSample answers to be provided by trainer for guided discussion.\n\n**Jumlah Q-Format / Q-Format Total: 25 markah / marks**\n*Lulus / Pass: 60% = 15 markah/marks. Rujukan / References: CoCU C02 — semua kriteria penilaian / all assessment criteria.*","originalFile":"# PENILAIAN PENGETAHUAN / KNOWLEDGE ASSESSMENT\n\n**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C02/KA\n**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** MERAH JAMBU / PINK\n\n---\n\n## Competency Unit: Perform Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification\n\n| Field | Detail |\n|-------|--------|\n| Duration | 2 hours |\n| Total Marks | 100 |\n| Passing Mark | 60% |\n| References | KP(1/6) through KP(6/6) |\n\n---\n\n## SECTION A: Multiple Choice (20 marks — 2 marks each)\n\n**A1.** What colour identifies HV cables in a BEV? A. Red B. Blue C. Orange D. Yellow\n\n**A2.** What is the correct sequence for HV de-energisation? A. Remove interlock, then 12V, then key B. Remove key, then interlock, then 12V C. Turn off READY, store key, disconnect 12V, remove interlock, wait, verify D. Disconnect 12V, remove interlock, store key\n\n**A3.** The pre-charge system prevents: A. Battery overcharging B. Inrush current surge to inverter capacitors C. Coolant leakage D. Motor overheating\n\n**A4.** What is the typical pre-charge resistor value? A. 0.1 ohm B. 50-200 ohm C. 10,000 ohm D. 1 MOhm\n\n**A5.** The OBC converts: A. DC to AC for the motor B. AC from external source to DC for battery charging C. 12V to HV D. HV to heat\n\n**A6.** The DC-DC converter replaces which ICE vehicle component? A. Fuel pump B. Alternator C. Starter motor D. Radiator\n\n**A7.** Minimum personnel for battery pack removal: A. 1 person B. 2 persons C. 3 persons D. 5 persons\n\n**A8.** What should defective battery modules be stored at before disposal? A. 100% SOC B. >80% SOC C. <30% SOC D. 50% SOC\n\n**A9.** Insulation resistance of HV cables must exceed: A. 100 ohm B. 1 kOhm C. 1 MOhm D. 1 GOhm\n\n**A10.** Normal DC-DC converter output voltage: A. 5V B. 9V C. 13.5-14.4V D. 48V\n\n---\n\n## SECTION B: Short Answer (40 marks)\n\n**B1.** (10 marks) Describe the complete HV de-energisation procedure in correct sequence. Include all seven steps.\n\n**B2.** (8 marks) Explain the function of the following BMS components: a) HV contactors b) Pre-charge relay c) Interlock/service plug d) Current sensor\n\n**B3.** (8 marks) Describe the coolant drainage procedure for OBC replacement. Include waste management requirements.\n\n**B4.** (8 marks) Explain the traction battery pack architecture. List and describe SIX (6) main components.\n\n**B5.** (6 marks) List THREE (3) common OBC fault categories and explain the possible cause of each.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION C: Structured Questions (40 marks)\n\n**C1.** (15 marks) A BEV arrives with a fault code indicating pre-charge timeout.\na) Explain what this fault means and what happens during normal pre-charge sequence. (5 marks)\nb) Describe the diagnostic procedure you would follow. (5 marks)\nc) Describe the replacement procedure for the pre-charge relay pack. (5 marks)\n\n**C2.** (15 marks) You are tasked with replacing a faulty battery module in a traction battery pack.\na) Describe the complete battery pack removal procedure from de-energisation to securing on transport hanger. (7 marks)\nb) Describe how you would identify the faulty module and perform the replacement. (5 marks)\nc) What functionality tests must be performed after reinstallation? (3 marks)\n\n**C3.** (10 marks) Compare the OBC and DC-DC converter in terms of: function, input/output voltages, cooling requirements, and diagnostic parameters. Present in table format.\n\n---\n\n## SKEMA PEMARKAHAN / MARKING SCHEME\n\n### Section A\nA1:C A2:C A3:B A4:B A5:B A6:B A7:C A8:C A9:C A10:C\n\n### Section B\n**B1:** (10 marks) 1. READY OFF (1m) 2. Key in safety box (1m) 3. Understand HV signage (1m) 4. Disconnect 12V negative (2m) 5. Remove interlock/store in safety box (2m) 6. Wait 5-10 min for capacitor discharge (1m) 7. Verify zero energy with CAT III multimeter — 3 measurements (2m)\n\n**B2:** (8 marks — 2 each) a) Switch HV power on/off from battery to vehicle circuits b) Energises before main contactor to prevent inrush current c) Manual HV circuit break point for safety d) Measures charge/discharge current for BMS monitoring\n\n**B3:** (8 marks) Place container (1m), identify hoses (1m), loosen clamps with insulated tools (1m), drain completely (1m), cap hoses (1m), transfer to sealed container (1m), label as SW305 (1m), dispose via licensed contractor (1m)\n\n**B4:** (8 marks — any 6 at 1.3m each) Battery modules, cells, BMS controller, CMUs, contactors, current sensor, cooling plates, housing, service plug, HV junction box\n\n**B5:** (6 marks — 2 each) Communication fault — wiring/ECU failure; Over-temperature — blocked coolant; DC output fault — internal electronics failure; Isolation fault — moisture intrusion; AC input fault — cable/inlet issue. Any three.\n\n### Section C\n**C1** (15 marks): a) Pre-charge timeout = capacitor voltage did not reach threshold within time limit. Normal sequence described (5m). b) Read fault codes, measure resistor value, relay coil resistance, check wiring, inspect connectors (5m). c) HV de-energisation, access relay pack, disconnect, remove, install new, reconnect, verify, test (5m).\n\n**C2** (15 marks): a) De-energise, drain coolant, raise vehicle, disconnect HV/LV cables, position lift table, remove bolts, lower pack, secure on hanger (7m). b) Open housing, visual inspection, measure module voltages, identify deviant module, disconnect bus-bars/CMU, remove, install new, balance voltages (5m). c) Cell voltages, insulation resistance, BMS communication, pre-charge, cooling, no fault codes (3m).\n\n**C3** (10 marks): Table with OBC vs DC-DC: Function (AC to DC vs HV DC to 12V DC), Input (AC 230/400V vs 200-800V DC), Output (200-800V DC vs 13.5-14.4V DC), Cooling (liquid vs liquid), Diagnostic (charging power/codes vs output voltage/stability).\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":92,"oldLines":3,"newStart":92,"newLines":152,"lines":[" **C2** (15 marks): a) De-energise, drain coolant, raise vehicle, disconnect HV/LV cables, position lift table, remove bolts, lower pack, secure on hanger (7m). b) Open housing, visual inspection, measure module voltages, identify deviant module, disconnect bus-bars/CMU, remove, install new, balance voltages (5m). c) Cell voltages, insulation resistance, BMS communication, pre-charge, cooling, no fault codes (3m)."," "," **C3** (10 marks): Table with OBC vs DC-DC: Function (AC to DC vs HV DC to 12V DC), Input (AC 230/400V vs 200-800V DC), Output (200-800V DC vs 13.5-14.4V DC), Cooling (liquid vs liquid), Diagnostic (charging power/codes vs output voltage/stability).","+","+---","+","+## BANK SOALAN Q-FORMAT / Q-FORMAT QUESTION BANK","+","+*Format JPK WIM — Jumlah: 40 soalan, 25 markah / Total: 40 questions, 25 marks*","+*Taburan / Distribution: BMS Theory 35% (Q1–Q7, Q21–Q22, Q26–Q30), HV Cable Systems 35% (Q8–Q14, Q23–Q24, Q31–Q35), OBC 30% (Q15–Q20, Q25, Q36–Q40)*","+","+---","+","+### Bahagian A / Section A — Pilihan Berganda / Multiple Choice (20 markah / marks)","+","+*Pilih jawapan TERBAIK. Setiap soalan bernilai 1 markah. / Choose the BEST answer. Each question carries 1 mark.*","+","+**Q1.** Battery cells connected in SERIES increase: A. Capacity (Ah) only  B. System voltage  C. Individual cell temperature  D. Charging speed only  [CoCU C02.5.3 — Battery Module System Inspection]","+**Q2.** Nominal voltage of a standard Li-ion (NMC) traction cell: A. 1.5V  B. 2.4V  C. 3.6–3.7V  D. 7.4V  [CoCU C02.5.3]","+**Q3.** The BMS does NOT directly control: A. Cell voltage monitoring  B. SOC calculation  C. Traction motor torque output  D. Cell temperature monitoring  [CoCU C02.1.2 — BMS Safety Requirement]","+**Q4.** The Cell Management Unit (CMU) reports to the: A. On-Board Charger  B. Motor Control Unit  C. BMS master controller  D. DC-DC converter  [CoCU C02.1.2]","+**Q5.** Passive cell balancing dissipates excess energy as: A. Mechanical work  B. Heat through bleed resistors  C. Stored charge in supercapacitors  D. Additional charge to adjacent cells  [CoCU C02.1.2]","+**Q6.** The pre-charge circuit limits: A. Coolant flow rate  B. Cell voltage imbalance  C. Inrush current surge when main contactors close against uncharged DC-link capacitors  D. HV insulation leakage  [CoCU C02.4.3 — Pre-charge Circuit Inspection]","+**Q7.** Li-ion thermal runaway can be triggered by: A. Normal low-current discharge only  B. Over-charge, mechanical crush, or sustained overtemperature  C. Exposure to temperatures below 10°C  D. Regenerative braking above 0.3g  [CoCU C02.5.3]","+**Q8.** HV cables in a BEV are identified by: A. Red insulation  B. Yellow conduit  C. Orange insulation/conduit  D. Blue shielded cable  [CoCU C02.2.1 — HV Cable Replacement Safety]","+**Q9.** Removing the manual service plug (MSP) physically: A. Disconnects the 12V auxiliary battery only  B. Activates the fire suppression  C. Splits the battery string into two isolated halves, breaking the main HV circuit  D. Triggers ABS pump self-test  [CoCU C02.2.4 — Disconnect HV Circuit]","+**Q10.** The HV interlock loop ensures contactors open when: A. Battery SOC drops below 10%  B. Any HV connector is unseated or the service plug is removed  C. Cabin temperature exceeds 30°C  D. The ABS controller requests braking  [CoCU C02.2.6 — Interlock Inspection]","+**Q11.** Minimum personnel required for traction battery pack removal: A. 1 technician  B. 2 technicians  C. 3 persons (technician + lift operator + safety observer)  D. 5 persons  [CoCU C02.5.7 — Battery Pack Removal]","+**Q12.** HV cable insulation resistance between conductor and chassis must exceed: A. 100 Ω  B. 1 kΩ  C. 1 MΩ  D. 1 GΩ  [CoCU C02.2.5 — HV Cable Condition Inspection]","+**Q13.** Lockout-Tagout (LOTO) is applied after de-energisation to: A. Restore vehicle to READY state  B. Prevent accidental re-energisation while work proceeds  C. Calibrate the current sensor  D. Initiate active cell balancing  [CoCU C02.1.4 — Hazard Identification]","+**Q14.** Standard pre-charge resistor value range: A. 0.1–1 Ω  B. 5–15 Ω  C. 50–200 Ω  D. 10 kΩ  [CoCU C02.4.3]","+**Q15.** The On-Board Charger (OBC) converts: A. DC battery power to AC for the motor  B. Battery HV DC to 12V DC  C. AC supply from the charging station to DC for battery charging  D. Kinetic energy to heat during braking  [CoCU C02.3.3 — OBC Functionality Check]","+**Q16.** The DC-DC converter in a BEV replaces which ICE vehicle component? A. Fuel pump  B. Alternator  C. Starter motor  D. Throttle body  [CoCU C02.6.3 — DC-DC Functionality Check]","+**Q17.** Typical DC-DC converter output voltage range: A. 5–6V  B. 9–10V  C. 13.5–14.4V  D. 48V  [CoCU C02.6.3]","+**Q18.** OBC coolant drained during replacement must be classified as: A. Municipal effluent  B. Scheduled Waste SW305  C. Recyclable process water  D. Category III industrial liquid  [CoCU C02.3.6 — Coolant Drain Procedure]","+**Q19.** A Level 2 AC charger connects to the vehicle via: A. 12V auxiliary port  B. Orange HV DC cable  C. AC inlet socket (J1772/Type 2)  D. OBD-II CAN bus port  [CoCU C02.3.7 — OBC HV Cable Disconnect]","+**Q20.** The FIRST safety test after OBC replacement is: A. Road test  B. HV insulation resistance test  C. Cabin temperature check  D. 12V battery load test  [CoCU C02.3.11 — OBC Functionality Test]","+","+---","+","+### Bahagian B / Section B — Soalan Senario Bermarkah / Graded Scenario Questions (5 markah / marks)","+","+*Jawab SEMUA soalan. Setiap soalan bernilai 1 markah. / Answer ALL questions. Each question carries 1 mark.*","+","+**Q21.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] During BMS diagnostics, Module 4 shows all cell voltages at 3.78–3.80V except Cell #11, which reads 3.52V — a deviation of 0.27V. Interpret this finding and state the required action. [CoCU C02.5.8 — Battery Module System Inspection]","+","+*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* A deviation of 0.27V far exceeds the acceptable ±0.05V tolerance, indicating severe capacity loss in Cell #11 likely due to lithium plating, internal micro-short circuit, or advanced electrolyte decomposition. The BMS is forced to terminate discharge when Cell #11 reaches cut-off voltage, significantly reducing pack range and performance. Escalate immediately to a traction battery pack replacement work order. Document all individual cell voltages in the diagnostic report. Do NOT clear the fault code; preserve freeze-frame data for root cause analysis.","+","+**Q22.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] A BEV arrives with fault code indicating \"Pre-charge Circuit Timeout — capacitor voltage did not reach threshold within 2.0 seconds.\" Describe your diagnostic response. [CoCU C02.4.3 / C02.4.6 — Pre-charge Diagnosis]","+","+*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Record the fault code and freeze-frame data before clearing. Pre-charge timeout means DC-link capacitors failed to reach approximately 95% of battery voltage within the allowed window, indicating high resistance in the pre-charge path or a defective relay. Measure the pre-charge resistor value against manufacturer specification. Check pre-charge relay coil resistance and contact resistance. Inspect all wiring and connectors in the pre-charge loop for corrosion, pin damage, or breaks. If the relay is confirmed faulty, initiate a pre-charge relay pack replacement work order per CoCU C02.4.7.","+","+**Q23.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] During underbody inspection, you observe an orange HV cable with abrasion damage — the outer conduit is worn down to the inner insulation layer but no conductor is visible. Describe your required action. [CoCU C02.2.5 / C02.2.7 — HV Cable Inspection and Replacement]","+","+*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Even though no copper conductor is exposed, damage to the outer conduit compromises the double-insulation protection required for HV circuits and creates risk of further abrasion against chassis metal. Do NOT clear the vehicle for service. Photograph the damage and document exact location and cable run in the work order. Measure cable insulation resistance at the damaged point using a CAT III insulation tester — if below 1 MΩ, the cable must be replaced immediately. Initiate an HV cable replacement work order referencing CoCU C02.2.7.","+","+**Q24.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] A service plug is missing from a BEV brought in for OBC replacement. Another technician suggests proceeding with HV de-energisation using only the 12V disconnect. Explain why this is unacceptable and state the correct action. [CoCU C02.1.4 / C02.2.4]","+","+*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* A missing service plug means the main HV battery circuit is not physically broken — the contactors are the only remaining protection, and they can close unexpectedly if a control fault occurs or the 12V battery is briefly reconnected. Proceeding without a service plug violates manufacturer safety protocol and OSHA 1994 requirements. The correct action is to STOP all HV work immediately, notify the supervisor, document the missing plug in the work order, and obtain a replacement service plug from the parts department before any HV work begins.","+","+**Q25.** [SENARIO / SCENARIO] A customer reports Level 2 AC charging stopped after 20 minutes, with an amber warning displayed. Fault codes P0AA9 (Battery Voltage Sensor) and U0100 (OBC CAN lost communication) are stored together. Describe your diagnostic approach. [CoCU C02.3.3 / C02.3.8 — OBC Diagnosis]","+","+*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Record both fault codes with freeze-frame data before clearing. P0AA9 and U0100 together suggest OBC CAN communication failure or a shared supply fault causing simultaneous ECU loss of synchronisation. Check the OBC CAN bus connector for corrosion, pin push-out, and harness damage near the OBC unit. Test OBC 12V supply voltage and ground integrity. Inspect the AC inlet socket connector for burn marks or deformation indicating connection overheating. If OBC is confirmed faulty through substitution testing, initiate OBC replacement per CoCU C02.3.9.","+","+---","+","+### Bahagian C / Section C — Senario Pengayaan / Enrichment Practice Scenarios (0 markah / marks)","+","+*Soalan latihan untuk kefahaman mendalam. / Practice questions for deeper understanding.*","+","+**Q26.** Explain the difference between State of Charge (SOC) and State of Health (SOH). Why must both be monitored in a BEV? [CoCU C02.1.2]","+","+*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* SOC is the current charge level as a percentage of the pack's present usable capacity — it indicates available driving energy. SOH is the pack's current capacity relative to its original new capacity — it indicates long-term degradation. Both must be monitored because a 100% SOC in a pack with 70% SOH delivers far less range than the same SOC in a healthy pack. The BMS uses SOH to progressively adjust SOC charge and discharge limits, protecting degraded cells from over-charge and over-discharge stress that accelerates thermal runaway risk.","+","+**Q27.** Describe active vs passive cell balancing. Explain which is used in most production BEVs and why. [CoCU C02.1.2]","+","+*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Passive balancing bleeds excess energy from higher-charged cells as heat through shunt resistors, equalising all cells to the lowest cell's voltage. Active balancing transfers charge from stronger to weaker cells using inductors or DC-DC converters, losing very little energy. Production BEVs overwhelmingly use passive balancing because it is simpler, less expensive, and sufficiently effective during normal charge cycles when voltage deviations are small. Active balancing adds cost and complexity only justified in ultra-high-performance applications where even small energy losses are unacceptable.","+","+**Q28.** Explain thermal runaway propagation in a battery module and why BEV battery fires require a different approach than standard vehicle fires. [CoCU C02.5.3]","+","+*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* When one cell enters thermal runaway, it generates intense heat and vents toxic gases including hydrogen fluoride and carbon monoxide, causing adjacent cells to enter runaway in a chain reaction that can propagate through an entire module in seconds. The sealed battery housing means standard fire suppression cannot reach internal cells. Large volumes of water are required to cool the battery externally and slow propagation — CO₂ or dry powder only suppresses surface flames temporarily. JBPM guidelines require submerging or continuously flooding the battery pack until thermal runaway ceases completely.","+","+**Q29.** Explain the role of the BMS contactor driver circuit in preventing battery damage during a collision. [CoCU C02.1.2]","+","+*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* The BMS receives crash signals from the airbag control module via CAN bus within milliseconds of impact detection. Upon receiving the crash signal, the BMS immediately opens both main contactors and disables the pre-charge circuit, disconnecting the battery pack from all vehicle HV circuits. This prevents high energy stored in the pack from flowing through damaged HV cables or components short-circuited by the collision. Post-crash, the contactors remain open until a technician manually resets the system, preventing unexpected HV re-energisation during rescue operations.","+","+**Q30.** Cell voltage deviations increase significantly during high-rate discharge but appear normal at low rate. Explain what this indicates and how to confirm the diagnosis. [CoCU C02.5.8]","+","+*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Voltage deviations appearing only under high current load but normalising at low load are the classic signature of increased internal resistance in specific cells. Higher internal resistance causes greater voltage sag under load (V = EMF − I×R), making affected cells appear weaker at high discharge rates. At low rates, the small IR drop is negligible and cells appear equal. The root cause is typically advanced electrolyte decomposition or lithium plating. Confirm by measuring individual cell internal resistance using the BMS diagnostic tool; cells significantly above the manufacturer baseline require module replacement.","+","+**Q31.** Describe the six-step Lockout-Tagout (LOTO) procedure for BEV HV work. State what documentation must be completed after LOTO is applied. [CoCU C02.1.4]","+","+*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Six steps: (1) Notify all affected personnel that energy isolation is being performed. (2) Turn READY off and remove key to the safety lockbox. (3) Disconnect 12V auxiliary battery negative terminal. (4) Remove the manual service plug and secure in the safety lockbox. (5) Apply LOTO padlock and \"DANGER — DO NOT OPERATE\" tag to the service plug port and battery disconnect points. (6) Verify zero energy with a CAT III multimeter at minimum three HV points. Documentation: complete the LOTO record form with technician name, work order number, date, time, zero-energy readings, and supervisor sign-off before any HV contact.","+","+**Q32.** Explain HV cable routing rules that must be followed during replacement. Why must HV cables not share conduit with LV harnesses? [CoCU C02.2.1 / C02.2.7]","+","+*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Routing rules: maintain minimum bend radius (typically 3× cable outer diameter) to prevent insulation stress; secure in manufacturer-specified clamps at required intervals; protect from heat sources with heat shields; route away from sharp chassis edges. HV cables must NOT share conduit with LV harnesses because any insulation failure or pinching of the HV cable inside shared conduit could induce dangerous HV voltage onto LV signal wires, damaging ECUs or creating shock hazard through LV connectors that technicians handle without HV PPE. Segregation is a mandatory safety design principle in all BEV platforms.","+","+**Q33.** Describe the complete inspection and replacement procedure for a damaged HV cable section. Include pre-work and post-work tests. [CoCU C02.2.5 / C02.2.7 / C02.2.8]","+","+*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Pre-work: full HV de-energisation, LOTO application, zero-energy verification at three points. Inspection: photograph damage, measure insulation resistance of damaged cable — must exceed 1 MΩ to chassis. Replacement: disconnect both ends using insulated tools; record cable routing path, segregation points, and clamp positions; install new cable following manufacturer's routing specification; reconnect all interlock connectors. Post-work: remove LOTO in reverse order; reconnect service plug and 12V battery; cycle to READY; verify no HV fault codes; perform HV insulation resistance test; confirm interlock continuity; complete functional test.","+","+**Q34.** Describe the symptoms and diagnosis of a welded main positive contactor. What risk does this create for workshop personnel? [CoCU C02.4.3 / C02.2.6]","+","+*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Symptoms: HV system remains energised even after READY OFF and service plug removal; current sensing shows HV bus voltage present; BMS logs contactor commanded open but voltage does not drop. Diagnosis: measure HV bus voltage after full de-energisation — if voltage remains, contact faces have welded shut from inrush current arc damage. Risk: the welded contactor creates a permanently live HV bus that cannot be isolated by normal procedures, removing the primary safety isolation mechanism. The vehicle must be treated as permanently energised, requiring specialist insulation tools and advanced disassembly procedures before any rectification work proceeds.","+","+**Q35.** A technician omits the post-replacement HV insulation resistance test and returns the vehicle to the customer. Explain what could happen and the legal implications. [CoCU C02.2.8]","+","+*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Without post-replacement insulation resistance testing, an improperly installed cable with insufficient insulation or a damaged grommet could create a leakage path between the HV circuit and the vehicle chassis. Moisture ingress over time could reduce insulation resistance below 500 Ω/V, causing the IMD to trigger isolation faults, unexpected HV system shutdown while driving, or in severe cases HV shock if occupants contact conducting body panels. Legally, the workshop bears liability under OSHA 1994 Section 17 (duty of care to non-employees) and the Road Transport Act 1987 for returning an unsafe vehicle to public roads.","+","+**Q36.** Explain how an OBC power factor correction (PFC) circuit improves charging efficiency and reduces impact on the AC supply grid. [CoCU C02.3.3]","+","+*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Without PFC, the OBC draws pulsed current from the AC grid, creating harmonic distortion that degrades power quality for all connected equipment. The PFC circuit uses an active boost converter to shape input current to closely match the AC voltage sinusoid, maintaining near-unity power factor. This means apparent power consumed equals real power delivered to the battery, minimising reactive current draw from the grid. PFC reduces harmonic injection, prevents voltage distortion on shared circuits, and maximises charging efficiency — allowing the OBC to deliver more energy to the battery per unit of AC energy drawn.","+","+**Q37.** Describe the typical OBC diagnostic fault categories and what each indicates about the component or circuit at fault. [CoCU C02.3.8]","+","+*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Five main categories: (1) AC input fault — incorrect voltage/frequency, damaged inlet socket, or AC cable issue. (2) OBC over-temperature — blocked coolant circuit, failed coolant pump, or ambient overload. (3) Output voltage/current deviation — internal OBC electronics failure, battery contactor fault, or CMU data conflict. (4) Isolation fault — moisture ingress to OBC housing or damaged HV-LV isolation barrier. (5) CAN communication fault — OBC unable to receive charge commands from BMS/VCU, indicating harness damage or ECU supply issue. Each category directs different diagnostic focus — thermal faults lead to cooling circuit inspection; communication faults lead to harness and ECU supply checks.","+","+**Q38.** A BEV is equipped with DC fast charging (CCS2). Explain how the HV circuit topology differs during DC fast charging compared to Level 2 AC charging. [CoCU C02.3.7]","+","+*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* During Level 2 AC charging, current flows through the OBC: AC inlet → OBC AC input → OBC power conversion → HV bus → battery pack; the OBC controls charge rate. During DC fast charging via CCS2, the external fast charger directly supplies DC at the required battery voltage — the OBC AC conversion stage is bypassed entirely. The DC inlet connects directly to the HV bus through a dedicated inlet contactor, allowing far higher power delivery up to 350 kW. The BMS communicates with the external charger via ISO 15118 protocol to dynamically adjust charge voltage and current throughout the session.","+","+**Q39.** Explain the dual supply paths for the DC-DC converter and why the converter must operate correctly for the vehicle to start. [CoCU C02.6.3]","+","+*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* The DC-DC converter steps down HV battery voltage to 13.5–14.4V DC to charge the 12V auxiliary battery and supply all 12V ECUs, lights, and accessories. Unlike ICE vehicles where the alternator only operates when the engine runs, the BEV DC-DC converter runs continuously when READY is on. If the converter fails, the 12V system depletes rapidly. Below a critical 12V threshold, the BMS master controller, contactors, and interlock circuits lose power — the main contactors fail open, disconnecting HV from the drive system. The vehicle cannot enter READY state without 12V power, making DC-DC converter operation essential for every start.","+","+**Q40.** After DC-DC converter replacement, what functional tests must be conducted before returning the vehicle to service? [CoCU C02.6.10 — DC-DC Converter Functionality Test]","+","+*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Mandatory tests: (1) HV insulation resistance test — confirm no HV-to-chassis leakage from new converter installation. (2) 12V output voltage measurement — must read 13.5–14.4V DC under load with READY on. (3) 12V auxiliary battery charge current — confirm converter is actively charging the auxiliary battery. (4) BMS fault code scan — confirm no HV isolation or DC-DC communication faults. (5) Coolant circuit pressure check — confirm no leaks at converter connections after coolant refill. (6) Full load functional test — operate headlights, climate, and multimedia simultaneously and verify output voltage remains stable under maximum 12V load.","+","+---","+","+### Skema Pemarkahan Q-Format / Q-Format Marking Scheme (25 Markah / Marks)","+","+**Bahagian A / Section A (Q1–Q20 — 1 markah setiap soalan / 1 mark each):**","+","+| Q | Jwp | Q | Jwp | Q | Jwp | Q | Jwp |","+|---|-----|---|-----|---|-----|---|-----|","+| Q1 | B | Q6 | C | Q11 | C | Q16 | B |","+| Q2 | C | Q7 | B | Q12 | C | Q17 | C |","+| Q3 | C | Q8 | C | Q13 | B | Q18 | B |","+| Q4 | C | Q9 | C | Q14 | C | Q19 | C |","+| Q5 | B | Q10 | B | Q15 | C | Q20 | B |","+","+**Bahagian B / Section B (Q21–Q25 — 1 markah setiap soalan / 1 mark each):**","+Award 1 mark for responses that correctly identify the key technical action(s) and reference relevant CoCU assessment criteria. See sample answers above.","+","+**Bahagian C / Section C (Q26–Q40 — Pengayaan / Enrichment — 0 markah / 0 marks):**","+Sample answers to be provided by trainer for guided discussion.","+","+**Jumlah Q-Format / Q-Format Total: 25 markah / marks**","+*Lulus / Pass: 60% = 15 markah/marks. Rujukan / References: CoCU C02 — semua kriteria penilaian / all assessment criteria.*"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01LMGLpD5M6g9a8ZS4itqoMn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now verify the results:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":18870,"cache_read_input_tokens":64640,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":18870},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"794df4fc-0917-40f0-aba2-b2d02fc662d7","uuid":"0799dcce-b71d-452c-84a7-5bb09660a6f7"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01LMGLpD5M6g9a8ZS4itqoMn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RZaBR3X1F2zwUj6cmztuh4","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd \"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\" && python -c \"\nimport re\n\nfor cu in ['C01', 'C02']:\n    path = f'bev-diagnostic-rectification/{cu}/KA.md'\n    with open(path, encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        content = f.read()\n    \n    # Count Q-format questions\n    q_matches = re.findall(r'\\*\\*Q(\\d+)\\.\\*\\*', content)\n    q_count = len(q_matches)\n    \n    # Check CoCU references\n    cocu_refs = re.findall(r'CoCU C0[12]\\.\\d+\\.\\d+', content)\n    cocu_count = len(cocu_refs)\n    \n    # Check 25-mark key\n    has_25mark = '25 markah' in content or '25 marks' in content\n    \n    # Count scenario questions (graded + enrichment)\n    scenario_count = len(re.findall(r'\\[SENARIO / SCENARIO\\]', content))\n    enrichment_count = len(re.findall(r'\\*\\*Q[23][0-9]\\.\\*\\*|\\*\\*Q[4][0-9]\\.\\*\\*', content))\n    \n    print(f'{cu}/KA.md:')\n    print(f'  Q-format questions: {q_count}')\n    print(f'  CoCU references: {cocu_count}')\n    print(f'  Has 25-mark key: {has_25mark}')\n    print(f'  Graded scenarios (SENARIO): {scenario_count}')\n    print(f'  Q21+ questions: {len([q for q in q_matches if int(q) >= 21])}')\n    print()\n\"\n","description":"Verify Q counts, CoCU refs, and 25-mark key for both KA files"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":18870,"cache_read_input_tokens":64640,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":18870},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"794df4fc-0917-40f0-aba2-b2d02fc662d7","uuid":"51d79212-9592-41be-90bb-0ae990705dd0"}
